window.STUDY_SEARCH={"courses":[{"slug":"classical-myth","name":"Classical Myth in Art","home":"/study/art-history/classical-myth.html","img":"/study/art-history/classical-myth/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"color-theory","name":"Color Theory","home":"/study/color-theory.html","img":"/study/color-theory/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"composition","name":"Composition","home":"/study/composition.html","img":"/study/composition/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"early-renaissance","name":"Early Renaissance in Italy","home":"/study/art-history/early-renaissance.html","img":"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"fauvism-and-expressionism","name":"Fauvism and Early Expressionism","home":"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism.html","img":"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"high-renaissance","name":"High Renaissance","home":"/study/art-history/high-renaissance.html","img":"/study/art-history/high-renaissance/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"impressionism","name":"Impressionism","home":"/study/art-history/impressionism.html","img":"/study/art-history/impressionism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"japanese-woodblock-prints","name":"Japanese Woodblock Prints","home":"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints.html","img":"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"japonisme","name":"Japonisme","home":"/study/art-history/japonisme.html","img":"/study/art-history/japonisme/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"neoclassicism","name":"Neoclassicism","home":"/study/art-history/neoclassicism.html","img":"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"northern-renaissance","name":"Northern Renaissance","home":"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance.html","img":"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"oil-vs-acrylic","name":"Oil vs Acrylic","home":"/study/oil-vs-acrylic.html","img":"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"pigments-and-materials","name":"Pigments and Materials","home":"/study/pigments-and-materials.html","img":"/study/pigments-and-materials/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"post-impressionism","name":"Post-Impressionism","home":"/study/art-history/post-impressionism.html","img":"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"realism","name":"Realism","home":"/study/art-history/realism.html","img":"/study/art-history/realism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"rococo","name":"Rococo","home":"/study/art-history/rococo.html","img":"/study/art-history/rococo/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"romanticism","name":"Romanticism","home":"/study/art-history/romanticism.html","img":"/study/art-history/romanticism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"value-and-light","name":"Value and Light","home":"/study/value-and-light.html","img":"/study/value-and-light/img/","lane":"Technique"}],"items":[[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth.html","Classical Myth in Art · Overview","What to Look For","","h1_aphrodite","The gods and heroes of Greece and Rome have been painted and carved for some 2,400 years. The works here date from about 530 BCE to about 1890. · Sandro Botticelli · The Birth of Venus · about 1485"],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/dionysus-cup.html","Classical Myth in Art","Dionysus Cup","Exekias","h9_exekias","Exekias · about 530 BCE · Athenian potter and painter, 6th century BCE. Black-figure terracotta, about 12 in. across. Staatliche Antikensammlungen, Munich · Exekias painted the figures as flat black silhouettes and scratched the detail into them. · Lines like the ship's planking are cut through the black to the clay, so detail shows without breaking the solid shapes. · God, ship and dolphins are solid black on the orange-red ground, so each reads by its outline alone, like a paper cut-out. · The technique is called black-figure. The god is Dionysus; the dolphins are the pirates he turned into fish. Only the inside is shown here."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/krater-with-heracles-wrestling-antaeus.html","Classical Myth in Art","Krater with Heracles Wrestling Antaeus","Euphronios","h12_antaios","Euphronios · about 515–510 BCE · Athenian painter, 6th–5th century BCE. Red-figure terracotta, 18 in. high. Louvre, Paris · Euphronios drew the wrestlers with a brush, in thin lines on the reddish clay, and painted the background black. · Muscles and ribs are fine dark lines on the bare clay, so anatomy shows inside each body and the figure stays light against the black. · Antaeus's hair is a rough fringe and Heracles's a cap of tiny raised dots, so the two heads look different at a glance. · The technique is called red-figure, the reverse of black-figure. Heracles kneels and strangles Antaeus; three women flee."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/bronze-god-from-cape-artemision.html","Classical Myth in Art","Bronze God from Cape Artemision","Greek, sculptor unknown","h1_poseidon","Greek, sculptor unknown · about 460 BCE · Bronze, 82 1/4 in. high. National Archaeological Museum, Athens · The whole body is one wide stance, arms stretched out to balance a throw that is about to happen. · The arms stretch about as wide as the figure is tall, so the pose fills a square and looks braced and steady. · The feet are planted far apart, so the legs make a wide triangle that looks able to balance the outstretched arms. · One of the few original Greek statues of its period to survive. Nothing is in either hand, so it is read as Zeus with a thunderbolt or Poseidon with a trident. It was pulled from the sea off Euboea."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/apollo-belvedere.html","Classical Myth in Art","Apollo Belvedere","Roman, after a Greek bronze","h1_apollo","Roman, after a Greek bronze · early 2nd century CE · Marble. Vatican Museums, Vatican City · The body bends in a slow S from the turned head down through the hip to the back foot. · One leg carries the weight and the other trails, which tips the hips and starts the curve. · The head and outstretched arm turn one way and the weight settles on the opposite leg, so the figure twists. · A Roman marble copy of a Greek bronze of about 330 BCE. Apollo has just loosed an arrow; the outstretched hand once held the bow."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/venus-de-milo.html","Classical Myth in Art","Venus de Milo","Greek, sculptor unknown","h6_milo","Greek, sculptor unknown · about 150–125 BCE · Parian marble, 80 1/4 in. high. Louvre, Paris · The hips turn one way and the shoulders the other, so the body twists in a spiral. · The left hip pushes out and the stomach's center line bends, so the torso reads as a turning curve, not a straight column. · The head faces right while the chest faces forward, so the body's turn carries on up through the neck. · Carved in two blocks of marble joined at the hips. Both arms are missing, all but a stub."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/the-birth-of-venus.html","Classical Myth in Art","The Birth of Venus","Sandro Botticelli","h1_aphrodite","Sandro Botticelli · about 1485 · Italian, 1445–1510. Tempera on canvas, 68 × 109 3/4 in. Uffizi Gallery, Florence · Botticelli drew every figure with a clean contour and kept the color inside nearly flat. · A thin brown line edges the goddess's arms and legs, so her pale body stays sharply separate from the sea behind. · The figures hardly cast shadows and the skin is shaded only faintly, so the bodies look nearly flat, not rounded. · Painted in tempera on canvas, a support then used for decorations in noble houses; probably a Medici commission. Venus comes ashore on a shell, blown by the wind gods."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/diana-and-actaeon.html","Classical Myth in Art","Diana and Actaeon","Titian","h6_actaeon","Titian · 1556–59 · Italian, about 1488–1576. Oil on canvas, 72 3/4 × 79 1/2 in. The National Gallery, London, and National Galleries of Scotland · Titian split the picture in two: the hunter stands alone at the left and the goddess's group fills the right. · A red curtain and a stone pillar divide the canvas unevenly, so the hunter stands apart in the narrower left part. · The goddess and nymphs are the palest shapes and sit close together, so they read as one group against the single, darker hunter. · One of six myth paintings for Philip II of Spain, known as the poesie, with subjects from Ovid. A stag's skull on the stonework foretells the hunter's fate."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/the-rape-of-europa.html","Classical Myth in Art","The Rape of Europa","Titian","h5_europa","Titian · 1559–62 · Oil on canvas, 70 × 80 3/4 in. Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum, Boston · Titian painted the scarf, the clouds and the water in loose, dragged strokes that never tidy up into detail. · The scarf at upper right is a few wide red sweeps with visible strokes, so the cloth seems to whip in the wind. · The bull's head and the woman are sharper than the sky and water, so they look close and solid and the rest hazy. · Also painted for Philip II, in Venice, and shipped to Madrid in 1562. Signed at lower left, under the cupid on the dolphin."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/bacchus.html","Classical Myth in Art","Bacchus","Caravaggio","h9_bacchus","Caravaggio · about 1598 · Italian, 1571–1610. Oil on canvas, 37 1/2 × 33 1/2 in. Uffizi Gallery, Florence · One light from the upper left falls on skin, cloth and fruit, and the wall behind drops away into brown. · The white drapery is modeled in big folds with hard lights and deep creases, so the cloth looks as solid as carved marble. · The shoulder at left is the brightest skin and the carafe's shadow falls right, so the light reads as coming from the upper left. · An early work from Caravaggio's years in Rome under Cardinal del Monte, who gave it to the Medici in 1608. A youth crowned with vine leaves plays the wine god, offering a glass."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/the-fall-of-phaeton.html","Classical Myth in Art","The Fall of Phaeton","Peter Paul Rubens","h3_phaeton","Peter Paul Rubens · about 1604–05, probably reworked about 1606–08 · Flemish, 1577–1640. Oil on canvas, 38 3/4 × 51 1/2 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Everything tumbles along one diagonal, from the rearing horse at upper right down to the tangle of horses at lower left. · Horses and bodies bunch into one swirling mass with no still point, so the eye keeps sliding down and never settles. · Pale shafts of light slant down toward the lower left, the way the horses fall, so the diagonal looks even stronger. · Rubens painted it as a young artist in Rome and probably reworked it later; painted-over straps and reins still show. The sun's chariot breaks apart, and the butterfly-winged seasons at left react in terror."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/laocoon.html","Classical Myth in Art","Laocoön","El Greco","h15_laocoon","El Greco · about 1610–14 · Greek, 1541–1614. Oil on canvas, 54 × 68 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · El Greco stretched the bodies long and thin and let their edges flicker. · The standing figures at left and right run most of the canvas height and have small heads, so their bodies look stretched. · Gray shadows and white highlights streak loosely along the limbs and the outlines are uneven, so the bodies' edges seem to shimmer. · El Greco's only known painting of a myth. The Trojan priest and his sons are attacked by snakes; the city behind is Toledo."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/vulcans-forge.html","Classical Myth in Art","Vulcan's Forge","Diego Velázquez","h1_hephaestus","Diego Velázquez · 1630 · Spanish, 1599–1660. Oil on canvas, 87 3/4 × 114 1/4 in. Museo del Prado, Madrid · Velázquez painted the god as a glow of thin, quick strokes and built the smiths up solidly. · Rays around Apollo's head are flicked on in short pale lines, so the halo reads as light, not a solid object. · The smiths' backs and arms turn from warm light to cool shadow across each muscle, so the bodies look round and solid. · His first large work on a pale lead-white priming, which the Prado credits for its luminous look. Apollo tells the smith god that his wife is unfaithful; the workers freeze at the news."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/the-abduction-of-proserpina.html","Classical Myth in Art","The Abduction of Proserpina","Rembrandt","h9_bernini","Rembrandt · about 1630–31 · Dutch, 1606–1669. Oil on oak panel, 33 1/4 × 31 1/4 in. Gemäldegalerie, Berlin · The lit parts are thick, textured paint, and the rest sinks into a dark ground. · The dress and chariot are built up in crusted, pale strokes that catch the light, so they seem to stand forward out of the dark. · The bright sky and dress sit in the left two thirds, so the right reads as one dark mass the horses head into. · The sky is painted in lapis lazuli. The museum reads the bright corner as life on earth and the dark as the realm of Pluto, who drags the girl down while her companions cling to her cloak."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/the-judgement-of-paris.html","Classical Myth in Art","The Judgement of Paris","Peter Paul Rubens","h15_paris","Peter Paul Rubens · probably 1632–35 · Oil on panel, probably oak, 57 × 76 1/4 in. The National Gallery, London · Three pale, warm bodies stand against dark olive-brown trees and sky, with strong red held to a few robes. · The left nude is the palest and the one seen from behind the most golden, so the three are easy to tell apart. · Red shows only in cloth, at far left, on the goddess seen from behind and at upper right, so each patch stands out. · Paris hands the apple to Venus, the middle of the three goddesses; the owl marks Minerva at left and the peacock marks Juno, seen from behind."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/oedipus-explains-the-riddle-of-the-sphinx.html","Classical Myth in Art","Oedipus Explains the Riddle of the Sphinx","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","h14_ingres","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · 1808 · French, 1780–1867. Oil on canvas, 74 1/2 × 56 3/4 in. Louvre, Paris · Ingres drew the body with a single unbroken contour and left no visible brushwork inside it. · The figure is in strict profile, so the outline from brow to heel reads as one continuous line. · The lit body stands against a dark cave, which makes the contour sharper still. · Sent to Paris in 1808 as student work from the French Academy in Rome, and shown at the Salon of 1827. Oedipus answers the Sphinx's riddle; a foot and bones lie at lower left."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/ulysses-deriding-polyphemus.html","Classical Myth in Art","Ulysses Deriding Polyphemus","J. M. W. Turner","h16_turner","J. M. W. Turner · 1829 · English, 1775–1851. Oil on canvas, 52 1/4 × 80 in. The National Gallery, London · Turner told the story with a sunrise of yellow, pink and blue, and the figures are hard to find. · The low sun at right is the brightest point, with pale yellow paint around it, so the eye goes there before any figure. · The giant at upper left is the same gray-purple as the clouds, so his shape is hard to tell from the sky. · Painted for the Royal Academy exhibition of 1829, from oil sketches made in Italy. Ulysses, in red under a red banner, taunts the giant."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/oedipus-and-the-sphinx.html","Classical Myth in Art","Oedipus and the Sphinx","Gustave Moreau","h18_moreau","Gustave Moreau · 1864 · French, 1826–1898. Oil on canvas, 81 1/4 × 41 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Moreau painted the wings feather by feather and the jewels bead by bead, with every edge sharp. · Feathers are painted one by one, long dark ones at the top, short white ones at the shoulder, so the wing reads as rows. · The sphinx's red beads and the patterned urn at right are as crisp as the faces, so small things hold the eye all over. · Moreau made his mark with it at the Paris Salon of 1864. The riddle scene is the one Ingres painted, now a close standoff."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/greece-to-paris-530-bce-1890/pygmalion-and-galatea.html","Classical Myth in Art","Pygmalion and Galatea","Jean-Léon Gérôme","h6_pygmalion","Jean-Léon Gérôme · about 1890 · French, 1824–1904. Oil on canvas, 35 × 27 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Gérôme painted the statue chalk white at the feet, warming to living flesh as it rises. · The white legs warm to pink with no dividing line, so the stone seems to be turning to skin. · The paint is smooth with no strokes showing, so nothing breaks the slow shift from white feet to pink shoulders. · One of three paintings of Gérôme's own marble group, each from a different angle. The statue comes to life as the sculptor kisses it."],[1,"/study/color-theory.html","Color Theory","Color Theory","","runge-farbenkugel","Modern color theory starts with Isaac Newton's Opticks (1704) and his prism. Goethe, the chemist Chevreul and the teacher Munsell then asked a painter's questions: how colors change beside each other, and how to name them exactly."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/color-comes-from-light.html","Color Theory","Color Comes from Light","","newton-opticks-circle","Color begins as light. White sunlight already holds every color, and a glass prism bends each one by a different amount, spreading them into a band, the spectrum. · The circle at right wraps the prism's band into a ring, so red and violet, the band's two ends, sit side by side. · Newton sized the seven unequal arcs to match the seven intervals of a musical scale. The seven names are his choice; the band of light itself has no sharp divisions. · Claude Monet · Rouen Cathedral, West Façade · 1894 · Monet painted this front more than thirty times; here the stone is pale blue, lavender and peach, colors of one light and weather, not of the stone. · A red apple absorbs most of the light falling on it and returns mainly the red."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/color-comes-from-light.html","Color Theory · Example","Rouen Cathedral, West Façade","Claude Monet","thumb/monet-rouen-west-facade","1894 · Monet painted this front more than thirty times; here the stone is pale blue, lavender and peach, colors of one light and weather, not of the stone. · Color Comes from Light"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/mixing-light-and-mixing-paint.html","Color Theory","Mixing Light and Mixing Paint","","bezold-plate-i-mixtures","Mixing light adds colors together; mixing paint takes light away, so paint mixtures turn darker and duller. · Each swatch sits beside the part of the spectrum its pigment, the colored powder in a paint, sends back: gamboge yellow returns red through green, Prussian blue a dim yellow, then green through blue. · Stirred together as paint, each pigment swallows what the other returns, and only the green band they share is left. · Mixed as light, the same two keep both bands and come out pale gray, not green. · Titian · Bacchus and Ariadne · 1520–23 · Titian laid his pigments on nearly pure and lightened them only with white, so the ultramarine sky keeps the strength a mixture would lose."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/mixing-light-and-mixing-paint.html","Color Theory · Example","Bacchus and Ariadne","Titian","thumb/titian-bacchus-ariadne","1520–23 · Titian laid his pigments on nearly pure and lightened them only with white, so the ultramarine sky keeps the strength a mixture would lose. · Mixing Light and Mixing Paint"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/hue-value-and-saturation.html","Color Theory","Hue, Value and Saturation","","ostwald-farbenfibel-triangle","Three things pin down any color: hue (red, yellow, blue and so on), value (how light or dark) and saturation (how strong or grayed). Each can change while the others hold. · From the pure yellow at the left point, the top edge adds white and the bottom black: value changes, the hue stays yellow. · The one chip between the edges takes in both white and black and turns a dull olive: its saturation drops. · The right-hand column loses the yellow and runs from white to dark gray: value alone, no hue or saturation left. · Sassoferrato · The Virgin in Prayer · 1640–50 · Sassoferrato kept the mantle one ultramarine blue; only its value moves, from bright folds to deep navy shadow, and that alone gives the cloth depth."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/hue-value-and-saturation.html","Color Theory · Example","The Virgin in Prayer","Sassoferrato","thumb/sassoferrato-virgin-prayer","1640–50 · Sassoferrato kept the mantle one ultramarine blue; only its value moves, from bright folds to deep navy shadow, and that alone gives the cloth depth. · Hue, Value and Saturation"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/the-color-wheel.html","Color Theory","The Color Wheel","","chevreul-chromatic-circle","A color wheel bends the hues into a circle, so neighbors and opposites can be read at a glance. · Narrow wedges step from red through orange, yellow, green, blue and violet, then back to red: the hues form a loop with no beginning or end. · Each mixed hue sits between the two it comes from: orange between red and yellow, green between yellow and blue. · Paul Signac · Evening Calm, Concarneau, Opus 220 (Allegro Maestoso) · 1891 · Signac took his shadows from across the wheel: yellow-orange evening light on the hill, violet-blue on the rocks, so lit and shaded ground stand clearly apart. · Painters build the wheel from red, yellow and blue, yet no three real paints can mix every hue on it."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/the-color-wheel.html","Color Theory · Example","Evening Calm, Concarneau, Opus 220 (Allegro Maestoso)","Paul Signac","thumb/signac-evening-calm","1891 · Signac took his shadows from across the wheel: yellow-orange evening light on the hill, violet-blue on the rocks, so lit and shaded ground stand clearly apart. · The Color Wheel"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/the-value-of-a-color.html","Color Theory","The Value of a Color","","munsell-atlas-value-axis","Each hue is strongest at a lightness of its own: pure yellow is light and pure purple dark, so two strong colors can differ in value as much as two grays. · A line ties each color to the gray of equal lightness: yellow lands near the white end and purple near the black, though both are at full strength. · Red and blue meet the same gray step, so a black-and-white photograph would turn them about the same gray. · James McNeill Whistler · Symphony in White, No. 1: The White Girl · 1861–1863, 1872 · Whistler kept dress and curtain to one high, white value, so the red hair, the flowers and the blue carpet carry the few colors."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/the-value-of-a-color.html","Color Theory · Example","Symphony in White, No. 1: The White Girl","James McNeill Whistler","thumb/whistler-white-girl","1861–1863, 1872 · Whistler kept dress and curtain to one high, white value, so the red hair, the flowers and the blue carpet carry the few colors. · The Value of a Color"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/saturation.html","Color Theory","Saturation","","munsell-atlas-chroma-branches","Saturation is a color's distance from a gray of the same lightness: close to the gray it looks dull, far from it pure and strong. · Each row of chips starts at the gray column and steps outward, every chip stronger than the one before. · The rows differ in length because pigments differ in strength: red reaches ten steps from gray, while blue and purple stop at six. · Francisco de Goya · Manuel Osorio Manrique de Zuñiga · 1787–88 · Goya set one saturated red suit in a room of grays and browns, so the child is the strongest color in the picture. · Mixing in a gray of the same value dulls a color while keeping its lightness close to where it was."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/saturation.html","Color Theory · Example","Manuel Osorio Manrique de Zuñiga","Francisco de Goya","thumb/goya-red-boy","1787–88 · Goya set one saturated red suit in a room of grays and browns, so the child is the strongest color in the picture. · Saturation"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/warm-and-cool.html","Color Theory","Warm and Cool","","field-chromatography-fig-2","Reds, oranges and yellows read as warm, like fire and sunlight, and blues, greens and violets as cool, like water and shade, though any color is warmer or cooler only beside another. · The bold line runs from Hot, beside orange, to Cold, beside blue: the warmest and coolest hues sit opposite, with every other hue between. · A second axis runs from Adv., advancing, beside yellow to Ret., retiring, beside purple: the old studio idea that warm colors seem to come forward, cool ones to fall back. · Joseph Mallord William Turner · The Fighting Temeraire · 1839 · Turner splits the sky between a blazing orange sunset on the right and cool blue on the left; each half looks warmer or cooler beside the other. · Advancing warm color is only a tendency; on a light background the effect can weaken or reverse."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/warm-and-cool.html","Color Theory · Example","The Fighting Temeraire","Joseph Mallord William Turner","thumb/turner-temeraire","1839 · Turner splits the sky between a blazing orange sunset on the right and cool blue on the left; each half looks warmer or cooler beside the other. · Warm and Cool"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/complementary-colors.html","Color Theory","Complementary Colors","","chevreul-1890-plate-i","Complementary colors sit directly across the wheel: red and green, orange and blue, yellow and violet. Side by side each makes the other look stronger; mixed as paint they dull each other. · Each disk is ringed with fine lines of its opposite: green round the red, red round the green, blue round the orange. The pairing runs both ways. · Chevreul printed the rings to show the tint the eye adds by itself around a strong color. · Vincent van Gogh · The Night Café · 1888 · Van Gogh set blood-red walls against a green ceiling and billiard table; the opposites heighten each other into a clash he meant to feel harsh."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/complementary-colors.html","Color Theory · Example","The Night Café","Vincent van Gogh","thumb/vangogh-night-cafe","1888 · Van Gogh set blood-red walls against a green ceiling and billiard table; the opposites heighten each other into a clash he meant to feel harsh. · Complementary Colors"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/neutrals.html","Color Theory","Neutrals","","rood-1879-mixing-pigments","Neutrals are grays, browns and dull greens. A painter can mix them from opposite sides of the wheel, because each paint soaks up most of the light the other sends back. · In the row starting with pure green, the next square adds chrome yellow, a wheel neighbor, and stays bright yellow-green: neighbors mix clean. · That row's last two squares add two reds, vermilion and crimson, from across the wheel, and drop to brown and maroon: opposites mix dull. · Camille Corot · Ville-d'Avray · 1870 · Corot built the scene from gray-greens, dull browns and a pewter sky, so the figure's small yellow headscarf and blue sleeve are the scene's strongest colors. · Earth pigments such as ochre and umber are low in saturation straight from the ground."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/neutrals.html","Color Theory · Example","Ville-d'Avray","Camille Corot","thumb/corot-ville-davray","1870 · Corot built the scene from gray-greens, dull browns and a pewter sky, so the figure's small yellow headscarf and blue sleeve are the scene's strongest colors. · Neutrals"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/monochromatic.html","Color Theory","Monochromatic","","chevreul-1890-plate-v","A monochromatic scheme uses a single hue, varied from light to dark. Nothing clashes because nothing differs in hue, so shapes are told apart by lightness alone. · One orange runs through a numbered scale from 0, white, at the bottom to 21, black, at the top: a single hue yields twenty distinct tones between them. · The middle steps carry the strongest orange; toward white it pales and toward black it sinks, so a one-hue picture's lightest and darkest parts are its least colorful. · James McNeill Whistler · Nocturne: Blue and Silver—Battersea Reach · 1870–75 · Whistler washed river, sky and barges in thin layers of one gray-blue, so shapes separate only by darkness; a few small lights are the one break in the hue."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/monochromatic.html","Color Theory · Example","Nocturne: Blue and Silver—Battersea Reach","James McNeill Whistler","thumb/whistler-battersea-reach","1870–75 · Whistler washed river, sky and barges in thin layers of one gray-blue, so shapes separate only by darkness; a few small lights are the one break in the hue. · Monochromatic"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/analogous-colors.html","Color Theory","Analogous Colors","","chevreul-1890-plate-vi-detail","Analogous colors are neighbors on the wheel, such as yellow, yellow-green and green. Each shares some of the next one's color, so they look calm together. · Between the yellow and the deep green at the top, each wedge holds a little more green than the last, so no two neighbors differ by much. · Any short run of wedges, such as orange-yellow into orange, is an analogous set; stretched from green all the way to orange, its ends no longer look related. · Claude Monet · Water Lilies (Agapanthus) · c. 1915–26 · Monet kept the pond to violets, blues and greens, neighbors that blend into one continuous surface; a few pale lily pads and warm blossoms break the run."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/analogous-colors.html","Color Theory · Example","Water Lilies (Agapanthus)","Claude Monet","thumb/monet-water-lilies-agapanthus","c. 1915–26 · Monet kept the pond to violets, blues and greens, neighbors that blend into one continuous surface; a few pale lily pads and warm blossoms break the run. · Analogous Colors"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/triads.html","Color Theory","Triads","","bezold-plate-iv-triads","A triad is three colors spaced evenly around the wheel, such as red, yellow and blue. Each is as far from the other two as possible, so the set gives variety with balance. · Each disk is cut into three equal wedges, one color to a wedge; in every disk no two colors are near neighbors on the wheel. · Disk 9 holds the painter's red, yellow and blue; disk 10 a blue-green, an orange and a dark red-violet. Any three evenly spaced hues make a triad. · Johannes Vermeer · The Milkmaid · c. 1660 · Vermeer's kitchen rests on a triad of yellow bodice, red skirt and deep blue apron and cloth; blue clearly leads, and the three stand out against the pale wall."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/triads.html","Color Theory · Example","The Milkmaid","Johannes Vermeer","thumb/vermeer-milkmaid","c. 1660 · Vermeer's kitchen rests on a triad of yellow bodice, red skirt and deep blue apron and cloth; blue clearly leads, and the three stand out against the pale wall. · Triads"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/contrast-of-area.html","Color Theory","Contrast of Area","","ct-extension","How much of each color a picture holds changes the balance: a small patch of a light or strong color can stand up to a large field. · Yellow, the lightest, needs only a quarter of the bar to hold its own against violet. · Orange takes a third against blue; red and green, alike in lightness, split the bar evenly: the darker the partner, the more room it gets. · Camille Corot · River with a Distant Tower · 1865 · Corot gives most of the canvas to pale sky, silvery greens and gray water; a few touches of red on the figures catch the eye at once. · The ratios are rough estimates, not measurements; they probably trace back to brightness values given for each color in 1816."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/contrast-of-area.html","Color Theory · Example","River with a Distant Tower","Camille Corot","thumb/corot-river-tower","1865 · Corot gives most of the canvas to pale sky, silvery greens and gray water; a few touches of red on the figures catch the eye at once. · Contrast of Area"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/simultaneous-contrast.html","Color Theory","Simultaneous Contrast","","chevreul-1890-plate-ii","A color changes with its surroundings: the eye pushes it toward the opposite of its neighbor, in hue and lightness, so one paint looks different on different grounds, or backgrounds. · One ornament in a single light ink sits on six colored grounds, so every difference between the six comes from the ground. · It almost disappears on the light yellow ground and stands out nearly white on the dark red: a dark surround makes a pale color look lighter. · Vincent van Gogh · Irises · 1889 · Van Gogh set blue-violet irises against orange flowers and green leaves against red earth, so each color makes its neighbor look stronger. · Gray patterns, Chevreul found, look greenish on rose, pink on green, lilac on yellow and orange-gray on blue."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/simultaneous-contrast.html","Color Theory · Example","Irises","Vincent van Gogh","thumb/vangogh-irises-getty","1889 · Van Gogh set blue-violet irises against orange flowers and green leaves against red earth, so each color makes its neighbor look stronger. · Simultaneous Contrast"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/optical-mixing.html","Color Theory","Optical Mixing","","chevreul-1890-plate-x","Small touches of two colors side by side blend in the eye into a third, because from a distance the eye can no longer tell them apart. · Red and yellow threads, never blended, read as orange in the top band; red and blue read as a dark violet in the middle one. · In the bottom band the fine stripes at left merge into green while the wide blue stripes at right stay blue: the mix needs small touches. · Henri-Edmond Cross · Pines Along the Shore · 1896 · Cross laid the shore in separate dabs of orange, pink and violet; from a step back they merge into warm, sunlit ground. · Chevreul had samples woven at the Gobelins tapestry workshop and found that threads of opposite colors cancel toward gray."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/optical-mixing.html","Color Theory · Example","Pines Along the Shore","Henri-Edmond Cross","thumb/cross-pines-shore","1896 · Cross laid the shore in separate dabs of orange, pink and violet; from a step back they merge into warm, sunlit ground. · Optical Mixing"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/local-and-expressive-color.html","Color Theory","Local and Expressive Color","","ct-local-expressive","Local color is a thing's color in plain daylight; expressive color is chosen for feeling or design instead. A tree or a hill can still be read by its shape, which leaves the color free. · The left panel gives each thing the color it is known by; the middle one tints the same forms with sunset light, as the eye sees them at that hour. · The right panel invents a green sky, pink hill and yellow tree that no daylight would give, yet the unchanged shapes keep them readable. · Paul Gauguin · Mahana no atua (Day of the God) · 1894 · Gauguin painted this in Paris from memory and fantasy: a pink bank and water in flat zones of yellow, blue and violet that still read as bank and water."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/local-and-expressive-color.html","Color Theory · Example","Mahana no atua (Day of the God)","Paul Gauguin","thumb/gauguin-day-of-god","1894 · Gauguin painted this in Paris from memory and fantasy: a pink bank and water in flat zones of yellow, blue and violet that still read as bank and water. · Local and Expressive Color"],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/color-and-emotion.html","Color Theory","Color and Emotion","","goethe-schiller-temperamentenrose","Colors carry mood: strong, warm colors tend to read as lively or tense and cool, dull ones as quiet or sad, though viewers differ. · Goethe and Schiller divided a ring of colors among the four temperaments, an old scheme of personality types written around the rim, tying each color to a kind of character. · Orange and orange-red fall to the choleric, or hot-tempered, type and blue to the phlegmatic, or calm, type: warm colors for heat of temper, cool ones for calm. · Edvard Munch · The Scream · 1893 · Munch set bands of blood-red and orange over a blue-black fjord and painted the figure in dull browns and grays, so the sky's hot color, not the figure, is loudest."],[1,"/study/color-theory/light-paint-and-the-eye/color-and-emotion.html","Color Theory · Example","The Scream","Edvard Munch","thumb/munch-scream","1893 · Munch set bands of blood-red and orange over a blue-black fjord and painted the figure in dull browns and grays, so the sky's hot color, not the figure, is loudest. · Color and Emotion"],[2,"/study/composition.html","Composition","Composition","","poore-picture-vision","Composition is how a painter arranges the parts of a picture. In 1435 Leon Battista Alberti named it one of three parts of a painter's job and compared the picture to an open window. In his 1899 book Composition, the American teacher Arthur Wesley Dow drew heavily on Japanese art, with line, dark-and-light and color as its elements."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/format.html","Composition","Format","","dow-three-proportions","Format is the outline of the whole picture: tall, wide, square or round. It decides how much room each part of the subject gets, so one scene reads differently in each format. · The tall, narrow frame cuts the house off at its left edge and leaves the tree standing clear, so the tree dominates. · In the two squarer frames the house is larger and claims far more space, so house and tree share the picture. · Arthur Wesley Dow · View of Ipswich, or A Bend in the River · ca. 1895 · The Met links this tiny upright print to Japanese pillar prints; its narrow frame stacks the view in bands, field to houses to river to hills."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/format.html","Composition · Example","View of Ipswich, or A Bend in the River","Arthur Wesley Dow","thumb/dow-view-of-ipswich","ca. 1895 · The Met links this tiny upright print to Japanese pillar prints; its narrow frame stacks the view in bands, field to houses to river to hills. · Format"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/cropping.html","Composition","Cropping","","dow-rectangles-various-shape","Cropping decides what the frame keeps and where its edges cut through the rest. Moving an edge changes what fills the picture, and so what the picture is about. · The tall frame at lower left keeps a long stretch of trunk, so the height of the trees becomes the subject. · The wide frame at lower right cuts the same trunks short and stretches the horizon, so the picture spreads sideways. · Edgar Degas · A Woman Seated beside a Vase of Flowers (Madame Paul Valpinçon?) · 1865 · The bouquet takes the center and the right edge cuts off the woman; a Met curator argues Degas planned such layouts to look like chance. · Degas took bold cropping from Japanese prints, and from sixteenth-century painters who sometimes left a figure partly outside the picture."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/cropping.html","Composition · Example","A Woman Seated beside a Vase of Flowers (Madame Paul Valpinçon?)","Edgar Degas","thumb/degas-woman-seated-beside-a-vase-of-flowers","1865 · The bouquet takes the center and the right edge cuts off the woman; a Met curator argues Degas planned such layouts to look like chance. · Cropping"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/positive-and-negative-space.html","Composition","Positive and Negative Space","","dow-notan-reversal","Positive space is the subject of a picture; negative space is everything around it. The edge of one is always the edge of the other, so both are designed together. · Printed three ways with blacks and whites traded, the round design keeps its outlines: every edge belongs to a dark shape and a light one at once. · On the right the six-sided center turns white inside dark petals; the outline that looked solid on the left now looks like an opening. · Félix Vallotton · Laziness (La Paresse) · 1896 · Vallotton uses no gray at all; the pale body is mostly bare paper, given its outline by the black around it."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/positive-and-negative-space.html","Composition · Example","Laziness (La Paresse)","Félix Vallotton","thumb/vallotton-laziness","1896 · Vallotton uses no gray at all; the pale body is mostly bare paper, given its outline by the black around it. · Positive and Negative Space"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/value-massing.html","Composition","Value Massing","","dow-notan-spotting","Value is how light or dark something is. Value massing groups a picture's lights and darks into a few big shapes, so the design reads at a glance; the Japanese word is notan, \"dark-light.\" · At lower right Corot's big tree shows no separate leaf or branch, yet its one dark mass against pale sky still reads as a tree. · Manet's beach at top left is a pale field with one large dark shape; the eye goes straight to it because nothing else is as big and as dark. · Katsushika Hokusai · Under the Wave off Kanagawa (The Great Wave) · ca. 1830–32 · Hokusai keeps to a few big shapes, deep blue wave, pale sky and a gray band, and against the gray Fuji's small white peak stands out. · To Dow, notan was a pattern the painter designs, separate from natural light and shadow."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/value-massing.html","Composition · Example","Under the Wave off Kanagawa (The Great Wave)","Katsushika Hokusai","thumb/hokusai-under-the-wave-off-kanagawa","ca. 1830–32 · Hokusai keeps to a few big shapes, deep blue wave, pale sky and a gray band, and against the gray Fuji's small white peak stands out. · Value Massing"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/focal-point.html","Composition","Focal Point","","poore-lion-desert","The focal point is where the eye lands first. Painters build it with contrast, isolation and placement, because the eye goes to whatever differs most from its surroundings. · The lion is the darkest shape in a pale desert with nothing near it, so contrast and isolation both send the eye there first. · The other darks, a horizon strip and a few plants at lower right, are thin and broken, so none competes with the lion. · Caravaggio · The Denial of Saint Peter · 1610 · Lit faces and hands stand out against near-black darkness, and the Met reads the pointing fingers as three accusations, matching Peter's three denials. · The English artist J. T. Smith advised in 1797 against two equal main lights: one should lead and the rest give way."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/focal-point.html","Composition · Example","The Denial of Saint Peter","Caravaggio","thumb/caravaggio-denial-of-saint-peter","1610 · Lit faces and hands stand out against near-black darkness, and the Met reads the pointing fingers as three accusations, matching Peter's three denials. · Focal Point"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/symmetric-balance.html","Composition","Symmetric Balance","","poore-salute-wounded","In symmetric balance the two sides of a center line carry about the same visual weight, often by mirroring each other, so the picture looks steady. Visual weight is the pull a shape has on the eye. · A ruled vertical splits the scene into two groups of about the same size, so neither half looks heavier. · Officers on horseback at left and a column of men at right are not mirror images, yet the two dark groups balance across the line. · Raphael · Madonna and Child Enthroned with Saints (Colonna Altarpiece) · ca. 1504 · Raphael centers the Virgin and, in the arch above, God the Father, with a female and a male saint on each side, so the halves nearly mirror each other."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/symmetric-balance.html","Composition · Example","Madonna and Child Enthroned with Saints (Colonna Altarpiece)","Raphael","thumb/raphael-colonna-altarpiece","ca. 1504 · Raphael centers the Virgin and, in the arch above, God the Father, with a female and a male saint on each side, so the halves nearly mirror each other. · Symmetric Balance"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/asymmetric-balance.html","Composition","Asymmetric Balance","","poore-steelyard","Asymmetric balance pairs two unlike sides that still feel level. It works like a seesaw: a large shape near the center can be balanced by a small one far out toward the edge. · In the middle drawing a small weight far out on the long arm holds the bar level against a heavy one close to the pivot. · On the seesaw a small child at the far end balances a large man near the pivot: distance makes up for size. · Mary Cassatt · Little Girl in a Blue Armchair · 1878 · Cassatt answers the large chair and sprawled girl right of center with a small dark dog and open floor at left, which keep the right from outweighing the left. · No part of a picture is weightless; even a blank area pulls a little."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/asymmetric-balance.html","Composition · Example","Little Girl in a Blue Armchair","Mary Cassatt","thumb/cassatt-little-girl-in-a-blue-armchair","1878 · Cassatt answers the large chair and sprawled girl right of center with a small dark dog and open floor at left, which keep the right from outweighing the left. · Asymmetric Balance"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/armatures.html","Composition","Armatures","","poore-fundamental-forms","An armature is a simple figure, such as a triangle, circle, S-curve, cross or L, that a picture's main shapes are built around, so many separate parts read as one arrangement. · Under the triangle, a figure group stands tall in the middle and lower at the sides, so it sits firmly, like a pyramid. · Below, the triangle lies on its side: a road wide at the bottom edge narrows to a far point that pulls the eye into the distance. · Andrea del Sarto · The Holy Family with the Young Saint John the Baptist · ca. 1528 · The Met notes how the four figures' hands spiral around the globe John passes to the Christ Child, gathering the group around one point."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/armatures.html","Composition · Example","The Holy Family with the Young Saint John the Baptist","Andrea del Sarto","thumb/sarto-holy-family","ca. 1528 · The Met notes how the four figures' hands spiral around the globe John passes to the Christ Child, gathering the group around one point. · Armatures"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/thirds-and-the-golden-ratio.html","Composition","Thirds and the Golden Ratio","","poore-strong-points","The rule of thirds puts key things on lines cutting the frame in three each way; the golden ratio splits it about 62 to 38. Both treat some spots as stronger than others, on thin evidence. · The trunk stands on a ruled line left of center, leaving the wider right side open for the distant shore. · The horizon runs along another line, a little above the middle, so land and sky get unequal shares. · Georges Seurat · Circus Sideshow (Parade de cirque) · 1887–88 · Seurat's stiff horizontals and verticals invite golden-ratio lines drawn over them, yet a study of his notes, sketches and paintings, summarized in 1981, found no sign he used the ratio. · A 2014 study of 727 paintings found the rule of thirds plays at most a minor role."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/thirds-and-the-golden-ratio.html","Composition · Example","Circus Sideshow (Parade de cirque)","Georges Seurat","thumb/seurat-circus-sideshow","1887–88 · Seurat's stiff horizontals and verticals invite golden-ratio lines drawn over them, yet a study of his notes, sketches and paintings, summarized in 1981, found no sign he used the ratio. · Thirds and the Golden Ratio"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/line-and-direction.html","Composition","Line and Direction","","poore-aesthetics-of-line","Every line, edge or gesture pushes the eye along it, and its direction carries a mood: level lines look at rest, uprights look steady, slants look as if moving or falling. · Upright trunks fill the forest at top left, and nothing in it seems to move; the flock in the middle spreads along a level horizon, the direction of rest. · At lower left a ring of dancers carries the eye around and back; at lower right swallows dive on a slant, the direction of motion. · Jacques Louis David · The Death of Socrates · 1787 · David lines up Socrates' followers in one flat, level band, so the upward thrust of his bent arm and pointing finger stands out against it."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/line-and-direction.html","Composition · Example","The Death of Socrates","Jacques Louis David","thumb/david-death-of-socrates","1787 · David lines up Socrates' followers in one flat, level band, so the upward thrust of his bent arm and pointing finger stands out against it. · Line and Direction"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/rhythm.html","Composition","Rhythm","","dow-repetition","Rhythm comes from repeating a shape or line, and the spacing between the repeats sets its beat. · Across the top border, one stepped unit of black and white repeats at equal intervals, so the eye moves along it at one even pace. · Below, farmhouse gables repeat at uneven intervals and in different sizes, crowded at left with a gap before the last house: a looser, less regular beat. · Utagawa Hiroshige · Sudden Shower over Shin-Ōhashi Bridge and Atake · 1857 · Fine parallel rain lines cover the whole print, and beneath the deck the bridge's posts make a slower, heavier repeat. · Dow thought repetition likely the most ancient form of design; some variety keeps a rhythm lively."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/rhythm.html","Composition · Example","Sudden Shower over Shin-Ōhashi Bridge and Atake","Utagawa Hiroshige","thumb/hiroshige-sudden-shower","1857 · Fine parallel rain lines cover the whole print, and beneath the deck the bridge's posts make a slower, heavier repeat. · Rhythm"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/leading-the-eye.html","Composition","Leading the Eye","","poore-shepherdess-exit","Painters plan a route for the eye: a way in, stops and a way out. Bright spots, strong contrasts and openings pull the eye, so their placement can lay out the route. · The shepherdess is a dark figure against the palest patch of sky, a strong contrast that draws the eye to her. · Pale sky opens behind the shepherdess and again beyond the trees at right, drawing the eye out in two directions. · Johannes Vermeer · Young Woman with a Water Pitcher · ca. 1662 · Window light falls on the white headdress and collar, the pitcher and the basin; a Met audio-guide speaker says it \"is guiding me around the room.\" · A 2020 eye-tracking study found composition does draw the eye to the chief elements, though the order of looking varies."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/leading-the-eye.html","Composition · Example","Young Woman with a Water Pitcher","Johannes Vermeer","thumb/vermeer-young-woman-with-a-water-pitcher","ca. 1662 · Window light falls on the white headdress and collar, the pitcher and the basin; a Met audio-guide speaker says it \"is guiding me around the room.\" · Leading the Eye"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/depth.html","Composition","Depth","","dow-depth-hiroshige","Depth cues make a flat surface look deep by copying how things appear to the eye: a near object hides part of what is behind it (overlap), things look smaller with distance, and receding lines draw together (linear perspective). · Two huge gate posts cut across the building and the trees, and whatever is cut across reads as farther back. · The two walkers by the building are drawn much larger than the figures at right, so the small ones read as far away. · Utagawa Hiroshige · Plum Estate, Kameido · 1857 · Hiroshige sets the plum trunk so close it runs off the frame and shows the orchard and its small visitors through its branches, with almost nothing in between: space jumps from very near to far."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/depth.html","Composition · Example","Plum Estate, Kameido","Utagawa Hiroshige","thumb/hiroshige-plum-estate-kameido","1857 · Hiroshige sets the plum trunk so close it runs off the frame and shows the orchard and its small visitors through its branches, with almost nothing in between: space jumps from very near to far. · Depth"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/unity-and-variety.html","Composition","Unity and Variety","","poore-unity","Unity is the feeling that all the parts make one whole, and it comes from parts that share something: a shape, a color, a direction or a single center. Variety adds enough difference to hold attention. · Above, two figures press into one tight embrace, so the picture has a single center. · Below, two seated figures each hold their own bird, and the picture splits into two halves. · Georges Seurat · A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884 · 1884–86 · Seurat ties dozens of different figures together with repeats, upright profiles, parasols and curved hats; his aim, the Art Institute says, was a modern-life painting to match a classical Greek frieze."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/unity-and-variety.html","Composition · Example","A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884","Georges Seurat","thumb/seurat-grande-jatte","1884–86 · Seurat ties dozens of different figures together with repeats, upright profiles, parasols and curved hats; his aim, the Art Institute says, was a modern-life painting to match a classical Greek frieze. · Unity and Variety"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/proportion-and-scale.html","Composition","Proportion and Scale","","dow-rectangles-proportion","Proportion is how the sizes of a picture's parts relate. The eye judges size by comparison, so a thing looks big or small only beside its neighbors; that relative size is its scale. · In the Japanese room the wide opening looks broad because the shelves stacked beside it are narrow. · At the bottom one square is divided three ways into parts of different sizes, and each division looks different though the outline never changes. · Thomas Cole · View from Mount Holyoke, Northampton, Massachusetts, after a Thunderstorm — The Oxbow · 1836 · Cole painted himself as a tiny figure near the bottom, looking up from his sketch, and beside that speck the valley reads as vast. · A painting's own size matters too: a big canvas pushes viewers back, while a small one draws them close."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/proportion-and-scale.html","Composition · Example","View from Mount Holyoke, Northampton, Massachusetts, after a Thunderstorm — The Oxbow","Thomas Cole","thumb/cole-oxbow","1836 · Cole painted himself as a tiny figure near the bottom, looking up from his sketch, and beside that speck the valley reads as vast. · Proportion and Scale"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/checking-a-composition.html","Composition","Checking a Composition","","dow-two-value-sketches","To judge a design, painters make it look strange to their own eyes: shrunk, mirrored, upside down or seen from across the room. Detail and familiarity drop away, leaving the plain arrangement of shapes. · Each scene is set down small, in black and white with no middle tones, so only the placement of the big shapes shows. · Trees are solid blots and water a few dashes, yet boats, road and house stay easy to make out: the layout does not depend on detail. · Georges Seurat · Oil Sketch for \"A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884\" · 1884 · On this small panel, one of more than 20 painted studies, Seurat tries the layout with figures as flat patches of color; the Art Institute notes the three at right were completely rethought."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-a-picture/checking-a-composition.html","Composition · Example","Oil Sketch for \"A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884\"","Georges Seurat","thumb/seurat-oil-sketch-grande-jatte","1884 · On this small panel, one of more than 20 painted studies, Seurat tries the layout with figures as flat patches of color; the Art Institute notes the three at right were completely rethought. · Checking a Composition"],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance.html","Early Renaissance in Italy · Overview","What to Look For","","botticelli-primavera","Solid figures in measured space · Early Renaissance is the name given to Italian art of roughly the fifteenth century. Florence was its leading center. · Sandro Botticelli · Primavera · about 1480"],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/italy-1423-1490/adoration-of-the-magi-strozzi-altarpiece.html","Early Renaissance in Italy","Adoration of the Magi (Strozzi Altarpiece)","Gentile da Fabriano","gentile-adoration","Gentile da Fabriano · 1423 · Italian, about 1370–1427. Tempera on wood, 118 × 111 in. The Uffizi, Florence · Gentile filled the panel with gold leaf and patterned cloth, and stacked the crowd upward instead of back into space. · Gold on the crowns, halos and horse harness catches the eye before any face, so the surface reads as a jeweled pattern. · Heads at the right rise one above another toward the top edge, so the crowd looks piled up, not standing on level ground. · Painted in tempera, color mixed with egg, for the banker Palla Strozzi's family chapel in the church of Santa Trinita, Florence. Some of the gold leaf, real gold beaten into thin sheets, is raised in relief, so details such as a rider's spurs stand out from the surface."],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/italy-1423-1490/the-tribute-money.html","Early Renaissance in Italy","The Tribute Money","Masaccio","masaccio-tribute-money","Masaccio · about 1425–27 · Italian, 1401–1428 or 1429. Fresco. Brancacci Chapel, Santa Maria del Carmine, Florence · Masaccio lit every figure from the right and gave each a cast shadow, so the men look solid and stand on the ground. · Each cloak has a lit side and a shaded side, so the bodies under them look round like columns. · The heads line up at one level while the feet sit at different heights, so the men look set back in depth. · A fresco, painted on wet plaster so the color sets into the wall. Christ's head took one day's session of plastering and painting."],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/italy-1423-1490/holy-trinity.html","Early Renaissance in Italy","Holy Trinity","Masaccio","masaccio-trinity","Masaccio · about 1425–27 · Fresco. Santa Maria Novella, Florence · The vault's lines meet at one low point, a method called linear perspective, and the flat wall looks like a deep chapel. · The sunken squares in the ceiling shrink row by row toward the back, so the vault looks like it runs deep behind the figures. · The two kneeling figures overlap the painted columns, so they look closer to the viewer than the chapel behind them. · Counted among the first major paintings built on perspective. In 1570 it was hidden behind a stone altar; since 1952 it has been back in its first place on the church wall."],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/italy-1423-1490/the-annunciation.html","Early Renaissance in Italy","The Annunciation","Fra Angelico","angelico-annunciation","Fra Angelico · about 1425–26 · Italian, 1395–1455. Tempera on poplar panel. Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid · Fra Angelico kept each color clean and bright, pink beside deep blue beside gold, inside a plain portico that recedes in space. · The pink robe and the blue cloak are each one strong color with pale highlights, so both figures stand out from the cream walls. · Two arches frame the angel and the Virgin separately, so each figure sits in a clear space of its own. · Painted for San Domenico, a convent church at Fiesole, near Florence. A 2019 restoration aimed to bring back the strong color and the white light of the scene, which dirt had dulled."],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/italy-1423-1490/the-battle-of-san-romano.html","Early Renaissance in Italy","The Battle of San Romano","Paolo Uccello","uccello-san-romano","Paolo Uccello · probably about 1438–40 · Italian, about 1397–1475. Egg tempera with some oil on wood, 71 3/4 × 126 in. The National Gallery, London · Uccello treated the battlefield as a measured floor, with broken lances laid out on it like lines on a grid. · The broken lances on the pink ground point toward the back, so the ground looks like a floor running away from the viewer. · The fallen soldier at the lower left lies feet toward the viewer and looks short, so the ground reads as flat and deep. · One of three battle panels made for the Florentine house of Leonardo Bartolini Salimbeni. The picture is much darker than when it was painted; among other changes, the silver leaf of the armor has tarnished."],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/italy-1423-1490/the-baptism-of-christ.html","Early Renaissance in Italy","The Baptism of Christ","Piero della Francesca","piero-baptism","Piero della Francesca · probably about 1437–45 · Italian, about 1415/20–1492. Egg tempera on wood, 65 3/4 × 45 3/4 in. The National Gallery, London · Piero lined the picture up on a center axis, so nothing in the scene seems to move. · Christ's joined hands, the bowl above his head and the dove sit on one vertical line, so the middle of the picture looks fixed. · Skin, cloth and tree trunk are close in tone, so the figures look as solid and still as the trunk beside them. · Made for a church in Sansepolcro, Piero's home town, and called his earliest surviving work. The hills and town in the background are read as Sansepolcro and its valley."],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/italy-1423-1490/adoration-of-the-magi.html","Early Renaissance in Italy","Adoration of the Magi","Sandro Botticelli","botticelli-adoration","Sandro Botticelli · about 1470–75 · Italian, 1445–1510. Tempera grassa on wood, 43 3/4 × 54 in. The Uffizi, Florence · Botticelli arranged the crowd as a triangle with the Virgin and Child at its top, so every figure leads the eye up to them. · The two side groups slope up toward the middle like the sides of a roof, so the crowd points to the Virgin. · The red cloak at the bottom center is the largest patch of one color, so the eye starts there and climbs to the Virgin. · Painted for the chapel of Gaspare del Lama in Santa Maria Novella, Florence. The kings are thought to be portraits of the Medici family, and the young man looking out at the far right may be Botticelli."],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/italy-1423-1490/the-duke-and-duchess-of-urbino.html","Early Renaissance in Italy","The Duke and Duchess of Urbino","Piero della Francesca","piero-montefeltro","Piero della Francesca · about 1473–75 · Oil on wood, each panel 18 1/2 × 13 in. The Uffizi, Florence · Piero set two hard-edged profiles against a landscape that fades to pale blue, so the heads stand far in front of the distance. · The hills get smaller, paler and bluer toward the horizon, so the land looks many miles deep. · Each profile is a clean outline against the sky with little shadow on the face, so the heads look like portraits on coins. · Painted in oil, color mixed with oil instead of egg. The sitters are Federico da Montefeltro and Battista Sforza; the backs of the panels are painted too, and the two were once a diptych, a two-panel picture. Battista died in 1472, and her pale skin may allude to that."],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/italy-1423-1490/the-martyrdom-of-saint-sebastian.html","Early Renaissance in Italy","The Martyrdom of Saint Sebastian","Antonio and Piero del Pollaiuolo","pollaiuolo-sebastian","Antonio and Piero del Pollaiuolo · completed 1475 · Italian, about 1432–1498 and about 1441–before 1496. Oil on wood, 114 3/4 × 79 3/4 in. The National Gallery, London · The six archers are three poses, each shown twice from different sides, as if one model had been turned around. · The two bending archers in front are one pose seen from the back and from the front, so the body reads as fully round. · Muscles in the bare legs and arms are drawn tight under the skin, so each archer looks strained by the pull of the bow. · Made for the Pucci family's chapel at Santissima Annunziata, Florence, where it had to be read from more than 50 feet away. It is in oil at a time when tempera was usual in Italy."],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/italy-1423-1490/saint-jerome-in-his-study.html","Early Renaissance in Italy","Saint Jerome in his Study","Antonello da Messina","antonello-jerome","Antonello da Messina · about 1475 · Italian, active 1456, died 1479. Oil on wood, 18 × 14 1/4 in. The National Gallery, London · Antonello used oil paint to show tiny objects and daylight from several windows on a panel only 18 inches high. · Light enters through the front arch and the back windows, so parts of the room are bright and others dim, like a real interior. · Books, jars and a hanging towel are painted at pinhead scale with their own highlights, so the tiny room looks full and real. · Painted in Venice. Antonello learned his handling of oil from Netherlandish painting, and an early viewer described this picture as a work by Jan van Eyck."],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/italy-1423-1490/st-francis-in-the-desert.html","Early Renaissance in Italy","St. Francis in the Desert","Giovanni Bellini","bellini-francis","Giovanni Bellini · about 1475–80 · Italian, about 1424/35–1516. Oil on panel, 49 × 56 in. The Frick Collection, New York · Bellini gave the rock, the town and the saint the same light, so the whole landscape looks like one place at one hour. · The cliff is blue-gray in shade and cream where the sun hits, so the rock looks lit from the upper left. · The saint stands low and right of center, well below the top of the cliff, so the landscape takes up most of the picture. · Bellini painted over a smooth white ground that reflects light back through the oil colors. The panel was later cut down, and who ordered the picture is not known."],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/italy-1423-1490/primavera.html","Early Renaissance in Italy","Primavera","Sandro Botticelli","botticelli-primavera","Sandro Botticelli · about 1480 · Tempera grassa on wood, 81 1/2 × 125 1/2 in. The Uffizi, Florence · Botticelli spread the figures across the front of the picture and closed the space behind them with a wall of trees. · The pale figures stand out against the dark trees, so the picture reads as a flat pattern, close to a tapestry. · Feet barely press the grass and no figure casts a shadow, so the bodies look weightless. · First recorded in a Medici house in Florence, hanging above a chest with a back. At least 42 kinds of plants have been identified in the picture."],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/italy-1423-1490/lamentation-over-the-dead-christ.html","Early Renaissance in Italy","Lamentation over the Dead Christ","Andrea Mantegna","mantegna-dead-christ","Andrea Mantegna · about 1483 · Italian, 1431–1506. Tempera on canvas, 26 3/4 × 32 in. Pinacoteca di Brera, Milan · Mantegna painted the body feet first and squeezed its whole length into a short space, a method called foreshortening. · The soles are at the front edge and the head at the back, so the body extends straight away from the viewer. · The feet are not much larger than the head, though they are far closer, so the whole body stays readable. · Probably made for the painter's own use; it is thought to be the picture found in his studio at his death."],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/italy-1423-1490/venus-and-mars.html","Early Renaissance in Italy","Venus and Mars","Sandro Botticelli","botticelli-venus-mars","Sandro Botticelli · about 1485 · Egg tempera and oil on wood, 27 1/4 × 68 1/4 in. The National Gallery, London · Botticelli drew each body with a clear, unbroken outline and kept the shading light, so line does most of the work. · A dark edge runs around Mars's arm, chest and leg, so the pale body separates cleanly from the pink cloth under it. · The folds of Venus's white dress are drawn as long thin curves, so the dress reads as a pattern of lines, not heavy cloth. · Probably ordered for a marriage. The long, low panel fits the kind set into a bedchamber wall, at shoulder height or higher."],[3,"/study/art-history/early-renaissance/italy-1423-1490/portrait-of-an-old-man-and-a-young-boy.html","Early Renaissance in Italy","Portrait of an Old Man and a Young Boy","Domenico Ghirlandaio","ghirlandaio-old-man","Domenico Ghirlandaio · 1490 · Italian, 1449–1494. Poplar wood panel, 24 3/4 × 18 1/4 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Ghirlandaio painted every bump and wrinkle of the old man's face and set it beside the smooth face of a child. · The growths on the nose and the mark on the forehead have their own small highlights, so the skin looks rough and real. · Both figures wear the same red, so the two heads read as one close group. · The sitters are not known, though names have been proposed."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism · Overview","What to Look For","","matisse-woman-with-a-hat","Color let loose, then line and paint pushed to the edge · In 1905 a group of painters in Paris, soon nicknamed the Fauves, the wild beasts, began using color for its own force rather than to describe things. Within a few years painters in Dresden, Munich and Vienna pushed the same freedom further, into jagged line, raw paint and, by 1913, pictures with no objects at all. · Henri Matisse · Woman with a Hat · 1905"],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/woman-with-a-hat.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Woman with a Hat","Henri Matisse","matisse-woman-with-a-hat","Henri Matisse · 1905 · French, 1869–1954. Oil on canvas, 31 3/4 × 23 1/2 in. San Francisco Museum of Modern Art · Matisse built the face from patches of green, mauve, yellow and orange, with only a few touches the color of skin. · Green runs down the nose and jawline where shadow would fall, so the face has shape without brown or gray. · The background is loose patches of the face's green, pink and mauve, so head and wall read as one patchwork. · Shown at the Salon d'Automne in Paris in 1905, where a critic called Matisse and his colleagues 'les fauves', the wild beasts. The sitter is the artist's wife, Amélie."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/portrait-of-madame-matisse-the-green-line.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Portrait of Madame Matisse. The Green Line","Henri Matisse","matisse-the-green-line","Henri Matisse · 1905 · Oil on canvas, 16 × 12 3/4 in. Statens Museum for Kunst, Copenhagen · Matisse split the face with one green stripe into a warm yellow half and a cool pink half. · The halves meet at the green line without blending, so the stripe stands in for a shadow and the nose reads as a ridge. · The wall is orange-red beside the yellow half and green beside the pink, so the split carries on behind the head. · The museum dates it, with a 'presumably', to autumn 1905, after the summer at Collioure."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/open-window-collioure.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Open Window, Collioure","Henri Matisse","matisse-open-window-collioure","Henri Matisse · 1905 · Oil on canvas, 21 3/4 × 18 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Matisse laid the paint on in loose, separate strokes and left bare canvas showing between them across much of the picture. · Pale canvas shows between the short dabs in the window, so sea and sky read as bright light, not solid surfaces. · The bottom flowers are red dabs beside green, opposites on the color wheel, so each looks brighter and stays separate. · Shown at the 1905 Salon d'Automne; the view is of sailboats off Collioure, a town on the Mediterranean coast."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/mountains-at-collioure.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Mountains at Collioure","André Derain","derain-mountains-at-collioure","André Derain · 1905 · French, 1880–1954. Oil on canvas, 32 × 39 1/2 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Derain made the trees from separate dashes of pure blue, green and yellow, with pale ground showing between them. · Each dash is one flat color, so the foliage reads as a mosaic. · The darkest tree dashes are deep blue and blue-green, not black or brown, so the shaded foliage stays strongly colored. · Derain and Matisse worked side by side at Collioure in summer 1905."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/le-bonheur-de-vivre.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Le Bonheur de vivre","Henri Matisse","matisse-joy-of-life","Henri Matisse · 1905–06 · Oil on canvas, 69 1/2 × 94 3/4 in. Barnes Foundation, Philadelphia · Matisse painted the figures and trees as flat areas of color inside long curving outlines, with almost no modeling. · The two central reclining women are unshaded pale pink, so only red and green outlines separate them from the yellow ground. · The ground colors stay as strong near the horizon, so the meadow looks flatter than the small far figures suggest. · Painted between October 1905 and March 1906 and shown at the 1906 Salon des Indépendants; bought there by Leo Stein."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/street-dresden.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Street, Dresden","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-street-dresden","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1908, reworked 1919 · German, 1880–1938. Oil on canvas, 59 1/4 × 79 in. The Museum of Modern Art, New York · Kirchner painted the street acid pink and gave the crowd pressing at the front edge green and orange faces. · The pink pavement climbs the canvas without getting paler or grayer, so the street seems to stand up behind the crowd. · The pale yellow blouse sits above a dark blue skirt, beside dark coats, so it pulls the eye to the front of the crowd. · Kirchner helped found Die Brücke (The Bridge), a group of German painters, in Dresden in 1905; the street is the Königstrasse, and the canvas carries a date of 1907, a year before it was painted."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/franzi-in-front-of-carved-chair.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Fränzi in Front of Carved Chair","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-franzi-carved-chair","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1910 · Oil on canvas, 28 × 19 1/2 in. Museo Nacional Thyssen-Bornemisza, Madrid · Kirchner painted the face bright green with red lips and drew it in a few jagged dark strokes. · Thick dark strokes ring the green face and mark eyes and brows, so it stands out flat and hard-edged, not rounded. · The background is flat blocks of yellow, green and blue, so nothing recedes and the chair looks pressed behind the girl's head. · Fränzi was a girl from a working-class district of Dresden who posed for the Brücke painters; the chair was most likely carved by Kirchner himself."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/english-step-dancers.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","English Step Dancers","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-english-step-dancers","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1911 · Color woodcut, image 16 × 10 3/4 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Kirchner cut the lines in wood, so every contour is stiff and splintery, and printed three flat colors with the black. · Thick black lines around the leaves and arms break off in straight cuts, so the contours look chipped, not flowing. · Pink, green and ochre are printed flat, so dancers, wall and floor read as cut-out shapes side by side. · Printed by the artist himself, as the inscription says; given to the Jena art society in 1918, seized from it in 1937 as 'degenerate art', and in New York by 1940."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/self-portrait-as-a-soldier.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Self-Portrait as a Soldier","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-self-portrait-as-soldier","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1915 · Oil on canvas, 27 1/4 × 25 in. Allen Memorial Art Museum, Oberlin College, Oberlin, Ohio · Kirchner cut the face and uniform into sharp planes and dragged the paint in hatched strokes that leave every edge hard. · The face is a yellow-ochre wedge with a hard-edged shadow down the nose and cheeks, so it looks carved from planes, not rounded. · The dark blue cap and uniform sit against an orange wall, blue's opposite on the color wheel, so the edge between them looks sharper. · Kirchner was inducted in 1915 and released for illness; he shows his painting hand as a bloody stump. In 1937 the picture hung in the Munich exhibition the Nazis called Degenerate Art."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/murnau-view-from-the-window-of-the-griesbrau.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Murnau – View from the Window of the Griesbräu","Wassily Kandinsky","kandinsky-murnau-griesbraeu","Wassily Kandinsky · 1908 · Russian, 1866–1944. Oil on cardboard, 19 1/2 × 27 1/2 in. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus, Munich · Kandinsky reduced the village to blocks of color: a violet and pink road, one orange roof and flat green fields. · The houses are white, blue and cream boxes with short dark strokes for windows, so each wall reads as one block. · Long brushstrokes follow the road's curve and each roof's slope, so every flat block still shows which way it tilts. · Painted from the window of the inn where Kandinsky and his friends lodged in summer 1908; the museum describes that stay as the moment their painting broke loose into free, expressive color."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/portrait-of-the-dancer-alexander-sakharoff.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Portrait of the Dancer Alexander Sakharoff","Alexej von Jawlensky","jawlensky-alexander-sakharoff","Alexej von Jawlensky · 1909 · Russian, 1864–1941. Oil on cardboard, 27 1/4 × 26 1/4 in. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus, Munich · Jawlensky wrapped the red costume and black hair in a thick dark contour and kept the color inside the lines flat. · The red costume has no folds or shadows, so the body reads as one shape held in by its dark outline. · The pale green background is red's opposite on the color wheel, so the costume looks stronger and its dark contour stands out. · The museum records that Jawlensky painted it in less than thirty minutes when the dancer visited his studio in costume before a performance."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/blue-horse-i.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Blue Horse I","Franz Marc","marc-blue-horse-i","Franz Marc · 1911 · German, 1880–1916. Oil on canvas, 44 × 33 1/4 in. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus, Munich · Marc painted the horse blue from mane to hoof and set it in equally unreal hills of red, yellow and green. · Red against green at the bottom, opposites on the color wheel, makes the ground look as vivid as the blue horse. · The hills behind are curved bands repeating the curves of the horse's neck and back, so horse and land tie together. · Marc wrote to Macke in December 1910 that blue stood for the male principle, yellow for the female, red for matter; the museum says this picture shows that color theory unusually clearly."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/lady-in-a-green-jacket.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Lady in a Green Jacket","August Macke","macke-lady-green-jacket","August Macke · 1913 · German, 1887–1914. Oil on canvas, 17 1/4 × 17 in. Museum Ludwig, Cologne · Macke painted the figures as smooth, rounded color shapes with no faces, in a landscape of equally smooth blocks. · The flat blue lake and yellow-ochre path are as plain as the coats, so people and landscape read as one pattern. · Inside each shape one tone blends softly into the next, as in the green jacket, so the shapes look rounded and soft-edged. · Bought in 1947 for the Wallraf-Richartz-Museum in Cologne and transferred to the Museum Ludwig in 1976."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/improvisation-no-30-cannons.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Improvisation No. 30 (Cannons)","Wassily Kandinsky","kandinsky-improvisation-30-cannons","Wassily Kandinsky · 1913 · Oil on canvas, 43 3/4 × 43 3/4 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Kandinsky left two cannons and some leaning towers to pick out; the rest is color and line with nothing to name. · A big blue shape runs diagonally through the center as the strongest color, so the eye lands there before the cannons. · Soft color blotches run past the black lines instead of filling them, so color and line read as two separate layers. · First shown in London in 1913; Kandinsky wrote to the collector Arthur Jerome Eddy that the cannons might reflect all the talk of war that year, but that the real content was what the viewer felt in the forms and colors."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/composition-vii.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Composition VII","Wassily Kandinsky","kandinsky-composition-vii","Wassily Kandinsky · 1913 · Oil on canvas, about 79 × 118 in. State Tretyakov Gallery, Moscow · Kandinsky filled the canvas with color patches and black lines that pull toward the center, and left almost no object recognizable. · The thickest black lines knot just left of center, crossing the colors without enclosing them, so that darkest spot pulls the eye inward. · The lower right corner is soft ochre without lines and the left edge mostly pale, so the eye drifts back to the crowded middle. · Painted in four days, 25 to 28 November 1913, after more than thirty studies; the museum notes faint echoes of Kandinsky's earlier pictures of the Apocalypse."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/self-portrait-laughing.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Self-Portrait, Laughing","Richard Gerstl","gerstl-laughing-self-portrait","Richard Gerstl · 1908 · Austrian, 1883–1908. Oil on canvas, 15 3/4 × 12 in. Belvedere, Vienna · Gerstl smeared and dabbed the paint so thickly that the right edge of the face blurs into the brown dabs behind it. · The background's thick brown and ochre dabs are close to the skin's browns and oranges, so the head stands apart mainly by being lighter. · The right cheek and jaw have no outline, only smeared orange meeting brown dabs, so that edge blurs into the background. · Gerstl taught himself to paint, kept apart from Vienna's artist groups and died at twenty-five; the museum reads this laugh as more menacing than cheerful."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/self-portrait-with-chinese-lantern-plant.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Self-Portrait with Chinese Lantern Plant","Egon Schiele","schiele-self-portrait-physalis","Egon Schiele · 1912 · Austrian, 1890–1918. Oil and opaque color on wood, 12 3/4 × 15 3/4 in. Leopold Museum, Vienna · Schiele ran a hard, wiry contour around the head and shoulders and set it against an almost empty pale ground. · Streaks of gray, blue and red shade the face inside a fine dark line, so the skin looks bruised, not lit. · The top edge cuts the hair and the bottom the chest, so the dark figure looks pushed close against the pale ground. · Painted as a pair with Schiele's portrait of Wally Neuzil, which the museum says accounts for the odd framing of both pictures."],[4,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris-germany-and-vienna-1905-1915/death-and-maiden.html","Fauvism and Early Expressionism","Death and Maiden","Egon Schiele","schiele-death-and-maiden","Egon Schiele · 1915 · Oil on canvas, 59 × 70 3/4 in. Belvedere, Vienna · Schiele broke the sheet and ground into jagged, crumpled shapes and painted the robe a darker shade of the ground's browns. · The white sheet's sharp folds are each edged with a dark line, so it reads as a broken surface, not soft cloth. · Brown and ochre lumps fill the ground to a thin pale top strip, so it looks tipped up like a wall. · The man is Schiele himself and the woman holding on to him is Wally Neuzil, whom he left to marry Edith Harms."],[5,"/study/art-history/high-renaissance.html","High Renaissance · Overview","What to Look For","","perugino-keys","High Renaissance is the name for Italian painting of the early 1500s, made mainly in Florence, Rome, and Venice. · Pietro Perugino · Christ Giving the Keys to Saint Peter · 1481–82"],[5,"/study/art-history/high-renaissance/italy-1481-1518/the-last-supper.html","High Renaissance","The Last Supper","Leonardo da Vinci","leonardo-last-supper","Leonardo da Vinci · about 1494–98 · Italian, 1452–1519. Tempera and oil on plaster, about 181 × 346 1/2 in. Refectory of Santa Maria delle Grazie, Milan · The museum that keeps it says this painting began a new era in art. The receding lines of the painted room run toward Christ's head, so the architecture itself points at him. · Leonardo painted on dry plaster instead of wet, which let him work slowly and change what he had done. The paint began to flake within a few years; a restoration finished in 1999 removed later repainting."],[5,"/study/art-history/high-renaissance/italy-1481-1518/mona-lisa.html","High Renaissance","Mona Lisa","Leonardo da Vinci","leonardo-mona-lisa","Leonardo da Vinci · 1503–19 · Oil on poplar panel, 31 1/4 × 21 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · The edges of the face fade into shadow with no drawn line, a method called sfumato. Painters of the time, the young Raphael among them, drew on this portrait for their own. · Leonardo began it by October 1503 and had not finished it when he died. Old, yellowed varnish now turns the blue of the sky toward green and darkens the lower part."],[5,"/study/art-history/high-renaissance/italy-1481-1518/holy-family-doni-tondo.html","High Renaissance","Holy Family (Doni Tondo)","Michelangelo","michelangelo-doni","Michelangelo · 1505–6 · Italian, 1475–1564. Tempera grassa (tempera with added oil) on wood, 47 1/4 in. across. The Uffizi, Florence · The hard, bright folds of cloth make the figures look carved, as if the group were sculpture. · Made for the Florentine merchant Agnolo Doni, who married Maddalena Strozzi in 1504. No other panel painting is certainly by Michelangelo."],[5,"/study/art-history/high-renaissance/italy-1481-1518/the-school-of-athens.html","High Renaissance","The School of Athens","Raphael","raphael-school-of-athens","Raphael · about 1509–11 · Italian, 1483–1520. Fresco, about 196 3/4 × 303 1/4 in. Stanza della Segnatura, Vatican · The Vatican Museums date the start of the High Renaissance to the frescoes in this room. The arches shrink, one inside the next, toward two men at the center. · Painted for Pope Julius II in a room he used as his library. The hall was inspired by Bramante's design for rebuilding St. Peter's. Raphael's cartoon for the figures survives in Milan."],[5,"/study/art-history/high-renaissance/italy-1481-1518/the-libyan-sibyl.html","High Renaissance","The Libyan Sibyl","Michelangelo","michelangelo-libyan-sibyl","Michelangelo · about 1511–12 · Fresco, about 178 3/4 × 149 1/2 in. (her section of the ceiling). Sistine Chapel ceiling, Vatican · The shoulders turn away while the knees face front, so the seated figure looks caught in the middle of a turn. Michelangelo's color in the second half of the ceiling influenced many of the painters later called Mannerists. · Painted in about twenty daily sections of plaster. A red chalk study for the pose, drawn from a male model, is in the Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York."],[5,"/study/art-history/high-renaissance/italy-1481-1518/the-sistine-madonna.html","High Renaissance","The Sistine Madonna","Raphael","raphael-sistine-madonna","Raphael · 1512–13 · Oil on canvas, 106 × 79 1/4 in. Gemäldegalerie Alte Meister, Dresden · Curtains drawn back at the top and a ledge at the bottom make the scene look like a vision. Since about 1800, the two small angels on the ledge have been copied on their own. · Painted for the high altar of the convent church of San Sisto in Piacenza, most likely on the order of Pope Julius II. Raphael added the two angels last."],[5,"/study/art-history/high-renaissance/italy-1481-1518/assumption-of-the-virgin.html","High Renaissance","Assumption of the Virgin","Titian","titian-assumption","Titian · 1516–18 · Italian, about 1488/90–1576. Oil on panel, 263 × 135 1/2 in. Santa Maria Gloriosa dei Frari, Venice · Raised arms link its three tiers of figures, so the eye climbs from the apostles on the ground to the top. · Unveiled on the high altar of the Frari, a Franciscan church, on May 19, 1518. The figure of God at the top, far above the viewer, is painted boldly with thick, unblended highlights."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism.html","Impressionism · Overview","What to Look For","","monet-impression","Impressionism is a way of painting that took shape in and around Paris from the late 1860s. Its painters first showed together in 1874, in an exhibition they organized themselves. · They worked in short, separate strokes of bright, unblended color and often painted shadows in color. Salon painting prized sober tones and a smooth finish, and conservative critics called the new pictures unfinished. · Claude Monet · Impression, Sunrise · 1872"],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/olympia.html","Impressionism","Olympia","Édouard Manet","manet-olympia","Édouard Manet · 1863 · French, 1832–1883. Oil on canvas, 51 1/2 × 75 1/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Manet painted the body as a pale, nearly flat shape, with almost no shading to round it. · Straight-on light shrinks shadow to thin dark lines at the body's edges, like an outline around a flat shape. · The dark green and brown background pushes the pale figure forward. · First shown at the Salon of 1865, the state-run exhibition in Paris, where it caused a scandal. The pose comes from Titian's Venus of Urbino."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/la-grenouillere.html","Impressionism","La Grenouillère","Claude Monet","monet-grenouillere","Claude Monet · 1869 · French, 1840–1926. Oil on canvas, 29 1/4 × 39 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Monet built the water from separate strokes of blue, white, green and black and left them unblended. · Each stroke stays distinct, so the water seems to move and catch the light. · The figures on the round island are a few faceless dabs each, so they look no more finished than the water. · Painted at a riverside bathing spot near Paris, with Renoir working beside him. The Met thinks it is one of the pictures Monet described in a September 1869 letter as rough sketches."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/impression-sunrise.html","Impressionism","Impression, Sunrise","Claude Monet","monet-impression","Claude Monet · 1872 · Oil on canvas, 19 3/4 × 25 1/2 in. Musée Marmottan Monet, Paris · Monet painted the harbor in a few hours, in paint thin enough to let the weave of the canvas show. · Ships and cranes are thin blue-gray silhouettes barely darker than the sky, so they look half lost in mist. · The orange sun and its broken reflection went on last, thick over thin blue-gray, so they stand out as the strongest color. · Shown at the first Impressionist exhibition, in Paris in 1874. The critic Louis Leroy used its title to mock the picture as a mere sketch, and the name stuck to the group."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/the-cradle.html","Impressionism","The Cradle","Berthe Morisot","morisot-cradle","Berthe Morisot · 1872 · French, 1841–1895. Oil on canvas, 22 × 18 1/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Morisot painted the veil over the cradle in thin strokes of white that let the baby show through. · Whites touched with pink and gray-blue cover most of the canvas, so the veil reads as shadowed folds, not a blank white patch. · The mother's dark sleeve shows through the veil as gray, so the cloth looks thin enough to see through. · Hung in the 1874 exhibition, where Morisot was the first woman to show with the group. The sitter is her sister Edma, watching her daughter Blanche."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/white-frost.html","Impressionism","White Frost","Camille Pissarro","pissarro-hoarfrost","Camille Pissarro · 1873 · French, 1830–1903. Oil on canvas, 25 3/4 × 36 3/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Pissarro painted the shadows blue, where painters of the day used gray or black. · Blue is the opposite of orange on the color wheel, so the shadows and the sunlit ground each make the other look stronger. · Warm light beside cool shadow makes the field read as cold and bright. · Shown in 1874 at the group's first exhibition, held in the photographer Nadar's former studio in Paris."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/boulevard-des-capucines.html","Impressionism","Boulevard des Capucines","Claude Monet","monet-capucines","Claude Monet · 1873–74 · Oil on canvas, 31 1/2 × 23 3/4 in. The Nelson-Atkins Museum of Art, Kansas City · Looking down from a high balcony, Monet set down each person in the crowd with a few quick strokes. · The street tilts up and its pale pavement fills the lower half, so each dark figure stands out as a separate stroke. · The bare trees are a haze of thin blue-gray strokes, so buildings show through and the far end of the street looks misty. · Painted from the building that housed the 1874 exhibition; the Nelson-Atkins reads the canvas as a sketch. Monet painted this view twice, and the version shown in 1874 was probably the other one."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/la-loge.html","Impressionism","La Loge","Pierre-Auguste Renoir","renoir-loge","Pierre-Auguste Renoir · 1874 · French, 1841–1919. Oil on canvas, 31 1/2 × 25 in. The Courtauld Gallery, London · Renoir used black as a strong color in the stripes of the dress, not only as shadow. · Black stripes against white make the picture's strongest contrast, so the dress stands out ahead of the man and the box. · The black stripes' feathered edges drag into the white, so the stripes read as soft cloth, not hard bands. · One of Renoir's pictures in the 1874 exhibition. His brother Edmond and a model, Nini Lopez, posed for it in the studio."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/the-dance-class.html","Impressionism","The Dance Class","Edgar Degas","degas-dance-class","Edgar Degas · 1874 · French, 1834–1917. Oil on canvas, 32 3/4 × 30 1/2 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Degas left the middle of the floor empty and pushed the dancers to the edges, where the frame cuts some off. · The dancers shrink fast from the lower left to the far upper-right corner, so the room looks deep. · The frame cuts a double bass at lower left and a dancer at the right edge, as if the room continued past it. · The Met links this cropping to Japanese woodblock prints. Shown at the second Impressionist exhibition, 1876; the teacher is the ballet master Jules Perrot."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/bal-du-moulin-de-la-galette.html","Impressionism","Bal du moulin de la Galette","Pierre-Auguste Renoir","renoir-galette","Pierre-Auguste Renoir · 1876 · Oil on canvas, 51 3/4 × 69 1/2 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Renoir painted sunlight through leaves as loose, pale patches laid across the dancers. · The patches cross the figures without following their shapes, so jackets and dresses break up into the dappled ground. · Strokes of blue, pink and yellow repeat across the whole crowd, tying it together. · Shown at the third Impressionist exhibition, 1877, where critics objected to the blurred forms. Some of Renoir's friends posed for it."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/paris-street-rainy-day.html","Impressionism","Paris Street; Rainy Day","Gustave Caillebotte","caillebotte-rainy-day","Gustave Caillebotte · 1877 · French, 1848–1894. Oil on canvas, 83 1/2 × 108 3/4 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Caillebotte applied the smooth finish and measured perspective of the art schools to a modern street with cropped figures. · The lines of the buildings and rows of paving stones narrow into the distance, so the street seems to stretch far back. · The paint is smooth, with almost no brushstroke showing, so the wet stones and umbrellas read as solid, shiny surfaces. · It dominated the 1877 exhibition, which Caillebotte largely organized."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/little-girl-in-a-blue-armchair.html","Impressionism","Little Girl in a Blue Armchair","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-blue-armchair","Mary Cassatt · 1878 · American, 1844–1926. Oil on canvas, 35 1/4 × 51 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Cassatt built the room from a few large blue shapes, with the gray-brown floor as a plain shape between them. · The chairs and sofa run off the canvas edges, so they read as big blue shapes more than as whole pieces of furniture. · Loose red, green and white strokes dot every chair but stop at the floor, so its plain gray-brown shape stands out. · Cassatt wrote that Degas helped with it; he probably painted part of the background."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/summers-day.html","Impressionism","Summer's Day","Berthe Morisot","morisot-summers-day","Berthe Morisot · c. 1879 · Oil on canvas, 18 × 29 1/2 in. The National Gallery, London · Morisot painted the whole picture in fast, open strokes, down to the zigzags on the dress at the lower left. · On the water, quick flicks of paint run mostly side to side, so the surface reads as ripples catching light. · The far shore sits near the top edge, so brushed water fills the space behind both women and nothing looks still. · Almost certainly shown at the fifth Impressionist exhibition, 1880, as The Lake in the Bois de Boulogne, a park in Paris."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/a-bar-at-the-folies-bergere.html","Impressionism","A Bar at the Folies-Bergère","Édouard Manet","manet-folies-bergere","Édouard Manet · 1882 · Oil on canvas, 37 3/4 × 51 1/4 in. The Courtauld Gallery, London · Manet filled the background with a mirror and painted its reflections out of line with what stands in front. · Her reflection sits far right though she faces straight out, so the mirror seems to show her from another angle. · Bottles and oranges in front are sharp and bright, the mirrored crowd gray and blurred, so the reflection looks far behind the bar. · Manet's last major painting, shown at the Salon of 1882. He made sketches on site, then painted the picture in his studio."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/woman-bathing-in-a-shallow-tub.html","Impressionism","Woman Bathing in a Shallow Tub","Edgar Degas","degas-shallow-tub","Edgar Degas · 1885 · Charcoal and pastel on paper, 32 × 22 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Degas drew the body in pastel, sticks of dry color, in hatched lines that stay separate. · Pale green and lilac strokes on the skin replace smooth shading, so the back looks round while every line stays visible. · Blue in the tub and ochre on the floor are rubbed in roughly, so the sheet's grain shows and the color looks powdery. · One of a set of nudes shown at the eighth and last Impressionist exhibition, 1886, where critics attacked the ungainly poses."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/stacks-of-wheat-end-of-summer.html","Impressionism","Stacks of Wheat (End of Summer)","Claude Monet","monet-wheat-stacks","Claude Monet · 1890–91 · Oil on canvas, 23 1/2 × 39 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Monet painted these stacks as a series, a group of pictures of one subject in different seasons and light. · A thin orange-pink line edges the stacks, so they look lit from behind by a low sun. · The same pinks, blues and greens are mixed through land, sky and stacks, so one light seems to fall over everything. · Monet worked on several canvases at once in the field and finished them in the studio. Fifteen of the stack paintings hung together in one room at a Paris gallery in May 1891."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/the-childs-bath.html","Impressionism","The Child's Bath","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-childs-bath","Mary Cassatt · 1893 · Oil on canvas, 40 × 26 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Cassatt used flat patterns and firm outlines, a style she developed after an 1890 exhibition of Japanese prints. · Broad stripes on the woman's dress are barely shaded, so it reads as flat pattern more than cloth over a body. · Carpet and wallpaper fill the background with pattern, so the floor seems to tilt up and the room looks shallow. · Shown in Cassatt's 1893 exhibition at Durand-Ruel's gallery in Paris."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/rouen-cathedral-west-facade-sunlight.html","Impressionism","Rouen Cathedral, West Façade, Sunlight","Claude Monet","monet-rouen-sunlight","Claude Monet · 1894 · Oil on canvas, 39 1/2 × 26 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Monet built the stone front from thick, crusted paint, as rough as the stone it shows. · The carvings break into thick patches of white, yellow, blue and pink, so the stone looks sunlit but no single carving shows. · The façade fills almost the whole canvas, leaving only slivers of blue sky, so the eye stays on the surface of the paint. · One of more than thirty views painted in 1892 and 1893 from rooms facing the cathedral. Monet moved between canvases as the light changed and finished them at Giverny in 1894."],[6,"/study/art-history/impressionism/paris-1863-1906/water-lilies.html","Impressionism","Water Lilies","Claude Monet","monet-water-lilies-1906","Claude Monet · 1906 · Oil on canvas, 35 1/2 × 37 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Monet left out the horizon, so the pond's surface fills the whole canvas. · Every edge of the canvas cuts through water or lily pads, so nothing marks where the pond ends. · Lily pads shrink and flatten toward the top, which suggests distance. · Monet's earlier water-lily pictures kept a horizon. Shown in Paris in 1909, in an exhibition of his water-lily paintings."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints · Overview","What to Look For","","hokusai-red-fuji","Line, flat color and bold design in prints of the floating world · Ukiyo-e, “pictures of the floating world,” were sold as woodblock prints from the 1680s into the 1880s. They were first made in Edo, the city now called Tokyo. Publishers issued them in quantity for the city's townspeople. The most common subjects were courtesans and actors of kabuki, a theater of dance and drama; landscape came later. Each print took four people: a designer, a block carver, a printer and the publisher who issued it for sale. · Katsushika Hokusai · South Wind, Clear Sky (Red Fuji) · about 1830–32"],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/the-entrance-to-ageya-machi.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","The Entrance to Ageya-machi","Hishikawa Moronobu","moronobu-entrance-ageya-machi","Hishikawa Moronobu · 1681–84 · Japanese, 1618–1694. Woodblock print; ink on paper, 10 × 15 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Moronobu designed everything for black ink alone, with no color and no shading. · The lines keep nearly one thickness around faces and roof tiles alike, so nothing looks rounded and the scene reads flat. · Solid black is kept to small areas such as hair and sashes, which stand out against the blank street. · A samurai and his servant have just come through the gate of the Yoshiwara, the pleasure quarter of Edo."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/on-the-yoshida-highway.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","On the Yoshida Highway","Sugimura Jihei (attributed)","sugimura-yoshida-highway","Sugimura Jihei (attributed) · about 1685 · Japanese, active about 1681–98. Hand-colored woodblock print, 23 1/4 × 11 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · The print itself is black only; the color was brushed on by hand afterward. · Orange-red and dull green strokes run loosely over collars and hems without filling the outlines, so they read as brushwork over the print. · Checks and crests printed in black cover most of each robe, so the picture looks complete even without the color. · A hand-colored print of this type is called a tan-e; before 1765 printmakers worked in one color and painted the rest in by hand. The labels name the characters, likely from an early play."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/perspective-view-of-the-interior-of-the-nakamura-theater.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Perspective View of the Interior of the Nakamura Theater","Okumura Masanobu","masanobu-nakamura-theater","Okumura Masanobu · 1740 · Japanese, 1686–1764. Hand-colored woodblock print, 15 3/4 × 25 in. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Masanobu slanted the ceiling boards and side galleries toward one point behind the stage, so the hall reads as deep space. · Heads in the crowd shrink row by row, so the front rows look close and the stage looks far back. · Each row of lanterns is smaller than the one in front, so the ceiling seems to run back over the crowd. · A perspective print of a kabuki theater. The designer was probably the earliest printmaker to use perspective."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/woman-admiring-plum-blossoms-at-night.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Woman Admiring Plum Blossoms at Night","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-plum-blossoms-night","Suzuki Harunobu · about 1766 · Japanese, 1725–1770. Woodblock print with embossing; ink and color on paper, 12 3/4 × 8 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Harunobu covered about half the sheet in a solid black night and set flat color against it. · White blossoms and the lantern stand out sharply against the dark. · The pale orange robe has thin stripes but no shading, so it reads as a flat cutout against the black. · Full-color printing, with a separate carved block for each color, began in Edo in 1765. The socks and inner robes are embossed: pressed into the paper without ink."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/lovers-walking-in-the-snow-crow-and-heron.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Lovers Walking in the Snow (Crow and Heron)","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-lovers-snow","Suzuki Harunobu · 1764–72 · Woodblock print with embossing; ink and color on paper, 11 1/4 × 8 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Harunobu made one figure a black shape and the other a white one, side by side on a plain ground. · The black robe has almost no inner lines, so it reads as one silhouette and makes the white robe look brighter. · The white robe has only a fine outline and a faint diamond pattern pressed into the paper, so it stays one pale shape. · The paired black and white robes explain the print's second title."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/a-boating-party-under-azuma-bridge.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","A Boating Party under Azuma Bridge","Torii Kiyonaga","kiyonaga-boating-azuma-bridge","Torii Kiyonaga · about 1785 · Japanese, 1752–1815. Triptych of woodblock prints; ink and color on paper, each sheet 15 1/4 × 10 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Kiyonaga drew the figures tall and slim and spread one design across three sheets. · Standing women have small heads on long bodies, so they look taller and slimmer than real people. · Boats and awnings continue across the joins between the sheets, so the three sheets read as one wide scene. · A triptych is one design printed on three sheets. Pleasure boats are tied up in the shade of a bridge on the Sumida River."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/ono-no-komachi-visiting-kiyomizu-temple.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Ono no Komachi Visiting Kiyomizu Temple","Chōbunsai Eishi","eishi-komachi-kiyomizu","Chōbunsai Eishi · about 1788 · Japanese, 1756–1829. Color woodblock print, 15 1/4 × 10 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Eishi kept the colors few and pale (pink, yellow, olive green and gray) and used black only in sharp patches. · Black on the hair and a sash is the only strong tone, so the colors around it look paler. · Cherry blossom is pale pink with no black outline, so it nearly fades into the paper and the dark leaves look sharper. · Two women and a boy view cherry blossoms at a temple in Kyoto; the boy fastens a slip of paper with a poem to a branch."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/three-beauties-of-the-kansei-period.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Three Beauties of the Kansei Period","Kitagawa Utamaro","utamaro-three-beauties-kansei","Kitagawa Utamaro · about 1791 · Japanese, about 1754–1806. Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 15 1/4 × 10 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Utamaro filled the sheet with three heads and shoulders, seen close up, and left the faces as bare paper. · A few thin lines mark nose, eyes and brow, so each face stays a smooth pale shape with no shading. · The hair is solid black with only fine pale lines for strands, so the bare-paper faces beneath it stand out. · A close-up bust portrait is called an ōkubi-e, a “big-head picture.”"],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/otani-oniji-iii-as-yakko-edobei.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Ōtani Oniji III as Yakko Edobei","Tōshūsai Sharaku","sharaku-otani-oniji","Tōshūsai Sharaku · 1794 · Japanese, active 1794–95. Woodblock print; ink, color and white mica on paper, 15 × 10 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Sharaku pushed the face and hands toward caricature and set them against a dark, flat ground. · Eyebrows, eyes and mouth are a few heavy black strokes on the pale face, so the scowl catches the eye first. · Pale hands, small next to the head, spread stiff fingers like claws, so they look exaggerated and stand out against the black robe. · A kabuki actor plays a ruthless manservant. The mica in the print is a mineral powder."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/under-the-wave-off-kanagawa-the-great-wave.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Under the Wave off Kanagawa (The Great Wave)","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-great-wave","Katsushika Hokusai · about 1830–32 · Japanese, 1760–1849. Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 10 × 15 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Hokusai set a huge near wave against a tiny far mountain and printed the wave in three blues. · Mount Fuji is drawn smaller than the crest of foam above it, so the wave looks taller than the mountain. · Dark and middle blue run in curved stripes down the wave and pale blue shades the foam, so the water looks ridged, not flat. · From the series Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji. The artist was known for landscapes printed with indigo and Prussian blue, an imported pigment."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/south-wind-clear-sky-red-fuji.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","South Wind, Clear Sky (Red Fuji)","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-red-fuji","Katsushika Hokusai · about 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 9 1/2 × 14 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Hokusai reduced the picture to three broad shapes (red mountain, blue sky, green base) and graded the color inside each one. · The red fades to brown at the peak and green at the foot with no line between, so the mountain stays one shape. · The sky is darkest blue along the top edge and pales toward the lower left, so the one flat shape still looks deep. · Color graded in this way is called bokashi: the printer wipes the color by hand."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/shichirigahama-in-sagami-province.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Shichirigahama in Sagami Province","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-shichirigahama","Katsushika Hokusai · about 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 10 × 15 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Hokusai kept the whole picture to blue and blue-green, with nothing printed in red, yellow or brown. · Sky band and headland base are both blue, darker than the pale blue-green sea, so light and dark mostly separate near from far. · The clouds are bare paper, so they show up crisply against the dark blue band behind them. · A print in which Prussian blue dominates is called aizuri, “blue printing.”"],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/evening-snow-at-kanbara.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Evening Snow at Kanbara","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-kanbara-snow","Utagawa Hiroshige · about 1833–34 · Japanese, 1797–1858. Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 8 3/4 × 13 3/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Hiroshige made the picture nearly all gray and white and left the snow as bare paper. · Roofs, hills and road are blank paper edged only by the gray around them, so they read as snow. · Gray shading fades softly on the slopes, with no hatched lines, so the hills look rounded and smooth under the snow. · A station on the Tōkaidō, the road between Edo and Kyoto. The snow is imagined: records indicate the artist passed through in summer."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/driving-rain-at-shono.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Driving Rain at Shōno","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-shono-rain","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1833 · Color woodblock print, 10 × 15 in. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Hiroshige slanted everything: the rain one way, the hillside path the other. · Fine straight lines of rain slant toward the lower left across the sky, so the whole sky seems to lean that way. · The path crosses the rain the opposite way, from the left edge to the lower right, so the two slants pull against each other. · Two groups of travelers pass on a slope at another Tōkaidō station. Three separate blocks printed the grays of the bamboo."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/ghosts-of-the-taira-at-daimotsu-bay.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Ghosts of the Taira at Daimotsu Bay","Utagawa Kuniyoshi","kuniyoshi-taira-ghosts","Utagawa Kuniyoshi · 1849–52 · Japanese, 1797–1861. Triptych of woodblock prints; ink and color on paper, each sheet 14 × 10 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Kuniyoshi set sharp, bright shapes in front against soft dark silhouettes behind. · The ghosts are flat gray and brown shadows with no outline or detail, so they nearly blend into the dark sky. · Ship, sail and crew have crisp lines and full color, so they look close and solid against the blurred shapes behind. · The ghosts of the Taira clan rise against a ship of the Minamoto, who defeated them in a sea battle in 1185."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/plum-garden-at-kameido.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Plum Garden at Kameido","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-kameido-plum","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1857 · Color woodblock print, 14 1/4 × 9 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Hiroshige pushed a dark tree trunk right up against the viewer and let the edges of the sheet crop it. · Flat dark trunk and branches are cut off by the sheet's edges, so the tree looks too close to fit in the view. · Between the branches, the far trees and visitors are drawn tiny, so the trunk seems only an arm's length away. · From One Hundred Famous Views of Edo. Vincent van Gogh painted a copy of the design in 1887."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/fukagawa-susaki-and-jumantsubo.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Fukagawa Susaki and Jūmantsubo","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-fukagawa-susaki","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1857 · Color woodblock print, 13 × 8 3/4 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Hiroshige set the viewpoint high in the air and hung an eagle across the top of the sheet. · The eagle, cut off by the top edge, spreads its wings across the full width, so it looks huge compared with the bay below. · Trees and huts are only small marks, while the eagle's feathers are drawn one by one, so the ground looks far below. · Edo Bay under snow. Viewers at the time could only imagine such a view from the air."],[7,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/japan-1680s-1880s/moon-over-daimotsu-bay-benkei.html","Japanese Woodblock Prints","Moon over Daimotsu Bay: Benkei","Tsukioka Yoshitoshi","yoshitoshi-benkei-moon","Tsukioka Yoshitoshi · 1886 · Japanese, 1839–1892. Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 13 3/4 × 9 3/4 in. Minneapolis Institute of Art · Yoshitoshi curled a tall black wave up the left side and kept almost the whole sheet to black and gray. · White foam frays into thin wisps along the crest, so the black wave's top edge stands out against the dark sky. · Cloud and water are layered in grays from pale to near black, so the cream moon and tan figure look warm beside them. · The warrior-monk Benkei prays in a storm so that slain enemies will not rise from the water. From the series One Hundred Aspects of the Moon."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme.html","Japonisme · Overview","What to Look For","","vangogh-flowering-plum-orchard","Japanese prints, props and design in Western art · Japonisme is the French name for a passion for the art and design of Japan that took hold in France and spread through Europe and North America after Japan resumed trade with the West in the 1850s. The critic Philippe Burty is generally credited with the word, in the early 1870s. Some artists painted fans, kimono and prints as studio props; others borrowed what the woodblock prints did with form: flat, bright color, firm outlines, long or tall formats, views from above, figures cut by the edge and wide empty areas. · Vincent van Gogh · Flowering Plum Orchard (after Hiroshige) · 1887"],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/caprice-in-purple-and-gold-the-golden-screen.html","Japonisme","Caprice in Purple and Gold: The Golden Screen","James McNeill Whistler","whistler-golden-screen","James McNeill Whistler · 1864 · American, 1834–1903. Oil on wood panel, 19 3/4 × 27 in. Freer Gallery of Art, Smithsonian, Washington · Whistler used Japanese objects as studio props: a gold folding screen, purple and red robes, and prints scattered on the floor. · Loosely brushed gray and green buildings sit on flat gold, so the screen reads as a painted prop, not an outdoor view. · Red lining between the dark purple kimono and white under-robe stands out and leads the eye down to the floor. · The model, Joanna Hiffernan, poses as a Japanese courtesan, studying a print from Hiroshige's series Views of the Sixty-odd Provinces."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/la-japonaise-camille-monet-in-japanese-costume.html","Japonisme","La Japonaise (Camille Monet in Japanese Costume)","Claude Monet","monet-la-japonaise","Claude Monet · 1876 · French, 1840–1926. Oil on canvas, 91 1/4 × 56 in. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · Monet filled the center of a canvas more than seven feet tall with a red kimono embroidered in gold and blue, and used fans as props. · The fans tilt in every direction, turning the plain wall into a loose pattern around the figure. · The red robe is set against a cool gray-green wall, which makes the red look stronger. · The museum reads the picture as a playful jab at the Paris taste for all things Japanese; Camille Monet wears a blond wig that marks her as Western. Shown at the second Impressionist exhibition, 1876."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/variations-in-flesh-colour-and-green-the-balcony.html","Japonisme","Variations in Flesh Colour and Green: The Balcony","James McNeill Whistler","whistler-balcony","James McNeill Whistler · 1864–70 · Oil on wood panel, 24 1/4 × 19 1/4 in. Freer Gallery of Art, Smithsonian, Washington · Whistler built the picture from flat bands: a turquoise floor, a thin railing and a misty gray shore. · The turquoise floor is one flat band across the lower third, with no floorboards and almost no shadows to give depth. · The thin railing runs straight across the middle, so floor, river and shore stack up as flat bands instead of receding. · The museum compares the heap beside the chimneys to Mount Fuji in prints by Hiroshige and Hokusai. From this picture onward, Whistler used a butterfly in place of a signature."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/soup-plate-rousseau-service.html","Japonisme","Soup Plate, Rousseau Service","Félix Bracquemond","bracquemond-rousseau-plate","Félix Bracquemond · 1866–75 · French, 1833–1914. Creamware, diameter 9 3/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Bracquemond scattered a few motifs off-center on the white plate and drew each in dark outline. · Wide white space surrounds every motif, so the turkey, moth and sprig read as separate drawings scattered on the plate. · Color is only a few touches, so dark outlines carry each motif and the orange on the turkey's head catches the eye first. · Bracquemond owned Hokusai's Manga, a many-volume book of sketches, earlier than most Europeans, and his designs for the service draw on it."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/boating.html","Japonisme","Boating","Édouard Manet","manet-boating","Édouard Manet · 1874 · French, 1832–1883. Oil on canvas, 38 1/4 × 51 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Manet cut strong diagonals across the canvas with the side of the boat and the mast. · With no horizon, the turquoise water is one flat backdrop, so the slanting boat and mast stand out against it. · The bottom and right edges cut off the boat, so it looks very close and its side reads as a slanting line. · The museum says Manet used the broad planes of color and the diagonals of Japanese prints here, along with the lighter palette of the Impressionists. Exhibited at the 1879 Salon."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/dancers-in-the-rehearsal-room-with-a-double-bass.html","Japonisme","Dancers in the Rehearsal Room with a Double Bass","Edgar Degas","degas-rehearsal-double-bass","Edgar Degas · about 1882–85 · French, 1834–1917. Oil on canvas, 15 1/2 × 35 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Degas pushed the dancers to the right of a long, narrow canvas, leaving much of the left to wall and floor. · The floor is seen from above and tilts up toward the windows, so it reads as a broad empty surface. · The far room's dancers are loose blue and red strokes, so the right end looks deep and busy against the plain left wall. · Colta Ives of the Met says Degas avoided Japanese props and took over principles instead: long formats, off-center designs, high viewpoints and emptied spaces."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/flowering-plum-orchard-after-hiroshige.html","Japonisme","Flowering Plum Orchard (after Hiroshige)","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-flowering-plum-orchard","Vincent van Gogh · 1887 · Dutch, 1853–1890. Oil on canvas, 22 × 18 1/2 in. Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam · Van Gogh copied a Japanese print in oil paint and made its colors stronger: red, green and orange. · The deep red sky sits above bright green grass, and as opposites on the color wheel each makes the other look stronger. · Two orange borders hold large dark green characters, so strong color runs to the edge and the borders read as flat bands. · The first of three paintings he made after prints in his own collection, here Hiroshige's Plum Garden in Kameido. He followed the design closely, strengthened the color and added the borders."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/bridge-in-the-rain-after-hiroshige.html","Japonisme","Bridge in the Rain (after Hiroshige)","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-bridge-in-the-rain","Vincent van Gogh · 1887 · Oil on canvas, 28 3/4 × 21 1/4 in. Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam · Van Gogh painted the rain as long dark strokes over a yellow bridge seen from above. · The yellow bridge climbs diagonally from the lower left to the right edge across the steep rain strokes, so it stands out strongly. · The river is short sideways dashes of green and blue, so the long rain strokes read as a separate layer in front. · Based on a print by Hiroshige. Van Gogh used a standard canvas and filled the extra space with characters copied from other prints."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/almond-blossom.html","Japonisme","Almond Blossom","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-almond-blossom","Vincent van Gogh · 1890 · Oil on canvas, 28 3/4 × 36 1/2 in. Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam · Van Gogh spread blossoming branches across a flat blue ground, seen close and from below, and let the edges cut them off. · Dark outlines run along the thicker branches, so their twisting shapes stand out sharply against the blue ground. · The blue background has no clouds or horizon, only brushwork, so it reads as a flat surface and the branches seem close. · The museum says the choice of subject, the strong outlines and the tree's position in the picture are borrowed from Japanese printmaking. A gift for his brother Theo on the birth of Theo's son."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/volpini-suite-laundresses-les-laveuses.html","Japonisme","Volpini Suite: Laundresses (Les Laveuses)","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-laundresses","Paul Gauguin · 1889 · French, 1848–1903. Zincograph on yellow paper, image 8 1/4 × 10 1/4 in. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Gauguin printed black lines and dark masses on bright yellow paper and showed the bending woman from behind and above. · Froth is drawn as looping lines with little shading, so the water reads as a flat pattern rising up the sheet. · The border cuts off a cow at lower left, so it looks close and just below eye level, strengthening the downward view. · The museum points to the strange viewpoint, the stylized foam and the cropped cow as signs of Japanese woodblock prints. One of his first prints, drawn on zinc plates."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/the-letter.html","Japonisme","The Letter","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-letter","Mary Cassatt · 1890–91 · American, 1844–1926. Color aquatint and drypoint, plate 13 3/4 × 8 1/4 in. Art Institute of Chicago · Cassatt covered the dress and the wallpaper in pattern and kept the color in flat areas. · The desk's blue top is one unshaded, steeply tilted triangle, so it reads as flat and ties in with the blue dress. · The face and hands are plain pale areas with only fine dark outlines, so the busy dress and wallpaper look more patterned. · One of ten color prints she made after seeing an exhibition of Japanese prints in Paris in spring 1890."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/woman-bathing.html","Japonisme","Woman Bathing","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-woman-bathing","Mary Cassatt · 1890–91 · Color aquatint and drypoint, plate 14 1/2 × 10 3/4 in. Art Institute of Chicago · Cassatt showed the room from above, so the floor tilts up behind the bending woman. · The patterned blue carpet climbs over a third of the way up the sheet behind her skirt, so the floor looks tipped up. · The top of the washstand and the inside of the basin are both in view, as they would be from above. · The museum compares the color, pattern and shapes to choices made by Japanese printmakers such as Hiroshige."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/moulin-rouge-la-goulue.html","Japonisme","Moulin Rouge: La Goulue","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec","lautrec-la-goulue","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec · 1891 · French, 1864–1901. Color lithograph, three sheets joined, 74 3/4 × 46 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Toulouse-Lautrec built the poster from flat shapes and bold lettering: black silhouettes, white petticoats and yellow lamps. · The crowd is one solid black shape with no faces, so the dancer's pale skirts and yellow hair show up brightly against it. · Her white petticoats are one flat shape with few lines, so the skirt reads as a cut-out, not as folds of cloth. · The Met's Colta Ives credits Japanese prints of Kabuki actors for the strong color, outlines and facial expressions in his posters."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/the-protest.html","Japonisme","The Protest","Félix Vallotton","vallotton-protest","Félix Vallotton · 1893 · Swiss French, 1865–1925. Woodcut, image 8 × 12 1/2 in. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Vallotton set solid black figures racing across bare paper, seen from above. · The street tilts up steeply toward the buildings at top left, so the figures read as black shapes spread over the paper. · The empty lower-right corner, about a third of the sheet, makes the crowd look packed into the top and about to run into the open space. · The museum links the flat space, tipped-up view and pattern to Japanese color woodcuts. Police are breaking up a political demonstration."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/nursemaids-promenade-frieze-of-carriages.html","Japonisme","Nursemaids' Promenade, Frieze of Carriages","Pierre Bonnard","bonnard-nursemaids-promenade","Pierre Bonnard · 1895 · French, 1867–1947. Color lithographs, four-panel screen, each panel 54 1/4 × 18 3/4 in. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Bonnard carried one scene across four tall panels, made to stand as a folding screen, and left much paper bare. · Carriages are squeezed into a narrow top band, so they look far off and the bare paper below reads as open ground. · The nanny's cape is a flat brown shape with few inner lines, so it looks heavy against the bare paper. · The museum says Bonnard copied the flat blocks of color in the Japanese color woodcuts he collected."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/boats-at-rest.html","Japonisme","Boats at Rest","Arthur Wesley Dow","dow-boats-at-rest","Arthur Wesley Dow · about 1895 · American, 1857–1922. Oil on canvas, 26 × 36 in. Art Institute of Chicago · Dow showed the rowboats from above and let the left edge cut off a shed. · The river curves up toward a high horizon, so the ground seems to tilt and the boats' insides are in full view. · Reflected trees and buildings are flat orange and blue patches, so the water reads as a surface tipped up toward the eye. · The museum points to cropping, a raised viewpoint and flattened space taken from Japanese prints. Through his books and teaching, Dow's ideas on composition shaped many artists and designers."],[8,"/study/art-history/japonisme/europe-and-the-united-states-1864-1900/water-lily-pond.html","Japonisme","Water Lily Pond","Claude Monet","monet-water-lily-pond","Claude Monet · 1900 · Oil on canvas, 35 1/2 × 39 3/4 in. Art Institute of Chicago · Monet arched a Japanese-style footbridge across the upper half and closed the top with foliage, leaving almost no sky. · Lily pads fill the lower half in sideways dabs shrinking toward the bridge, so the pond seems to run back under the arch. · Willow reflections run down the water in vertical strokes, so the pond shows foliage, not sky, and joins the trees above. · In 1893 Monet began making a pond at Giverny and spanned it with a wooden footbridge in the Japanese manner. The canvas is one of eighteen similar versions from 1899–1900."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism.html","Neoclassicism · Overview","What to Look For","","david-horatii","Neoclassicism is the return to the clear lines, calm order and stern subjects of ancient Greece and Rome, from the painters working in Rome in the 1760s to David's Paris around the Revolution. · Jacques-Louis David · The Oath of the Horatii · 1784–85"],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/the-death-of-lucretia.html","Neoclassicism","The Death of Lucretia","Gavin Hamilton","hamilton-lucretia","Gavin Hamilton · 1763–67 · Scottish, 1723–1798. Oil on canvas, 84 × 104 in. Yale Center for British Art, New Haven · Hamilton lined the figures up across the front like a carved relief and gave them big, readable gestures. · A dark curtain closes off the space behind the figures, so nothing opens up beyond them. · Firm outlines and even modeling set each figure apart from the dark like a carved shape, so its gesture reads at once. · Roman men swearing revenge over the body of Lucretia, painted in Rome about twenty years before David's Oath of the Horatii."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/portrait-of-a-young-man.html","Neoclassicism","Portrait of a Young Man","Pompeo Batoni","batoni-young-man","Pompeo Batoni · c. 1760–65 · Italian, 1708–1787. Oil on canvas, 97 1/4 × 69 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Batoni let the props tell the story: the sitter's open hand points to the antique statue and relief beside him. · The red sleeve runs out to the open hand, so the strongest color leads the eye to the pale stone relief. · Velvet, gilt wood and carved stone share one smooth finish, so the relief on the table looks as solid as the sitter. · A Grand Tour portrait: a young visitor to Rome shown with a statue of Minerva, a relief of Antinous, a sphere and books."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/the-death-of-general-wolfe.html","Neoclassicism","The Death of General Wolfe","Benjamin West","west-death-of-wolfe","Benjamin West · 1770 · American, 1738–1820. Oil on canvas, 60 × 84 1/2 in. National Gallery of Canada, Ottawa · West arranged a modern death as a mourning group around a fallen hero, but in the clothes of 1759, not togas. · Officers lean in from both sides under the furled flag, so a lit triangle leads the eye down to the dying general. · The battle is pushed back into a smoky strip on the horizon, so the foreground reads as a mourning scene. · A general dying at the battle of Quebec in 1759; shown at the Royal Academy in London in 1771."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/johann-joachim-winckelmann.html","Neoclassicism","Johann Joachim Winckelmann","Anton Raphael Mengs","mengs-winckelmann","Anton Raphael Mengs · c. 1777 · German, 1728–1779. Oil on canvas, 25 × 19 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Mengs kept the face smooth and left out any setting: only the head, the hand and the book. · The skin is blended smooth and the brown robe is brushed broadly, so the face looks sharper and holds the eye. · Browns, white, the book's pale yellow and a sliver of green sleeve are the only colors, so the lit face and hand stand out. · The scholar whose history of ancient art shaped the movement, shown holding a copy of Homer's Iliad. The portrait was painted after his death."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/voltaire.html","Neoclassicism","Voltaire","Jean-Antoine Houdon","houdon-voltaire","Jean-Antoine Houdon · 1778 · French, 1741–1828. Marble, 20 3/4 × 18 × 13 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Houdon carved a lifelike face, old, lined and smiling, and set it in the format of an ancient portrait bust. · Deep curls and drilled holes in the wig hold small shadows, so its busy surface makes the smooth, lined face stand out. · Small hollows cut for pupils hold a little shadow, so the blank marble eyes get a darker center and seem to look out. · The philosopher in the last year of his life, in his own coat and wig."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/cenotaph-for-newton.html","Neoclassicism","Cenotaph for Newton","Étienne-Louis Boullée","boullee-newton","Étienne-Louis Boullée · 1784 · French, 1728–1799. Ink and wash, sheet 17 1/4 × 26 in. Bibliothèque nationale de France, Paris · Boullée made architecture out of pure geometry: one sphere on a ring of plain bases, with no columns and no windows. · Gray wash shades the sphere from light at lower left to dark at top, so the flat drawing reads as a solid globe. · The people on the steps are specks, which makes the building seem immense. · An unbuilt monument to Isaac Newton, signed by the architect."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/cornelia-mother-of-the-gracchi-pointing-to-her-children-as-her-treasures.html","Neoclassicism","Cornelia, Mother of the Gracchi, Pointing to Her Children as Her Treasures","Angelica Kauffman","kauffman-cornelia","Angelica Kauffman · c. 1785 · Swiss, 1741–1807. Oil on canvas, 40 × 50 in. Virginia Museum of Fine Arts, Richmond · Kauffman kept the color calm and even and stood the figures before plain columns. · Each color, like the two clear reds, keeps to its own area, so the figures stay separate shapes against the tan wall. · Even light, soft edges and no deep shadow keep any part from jumping forward, so the whole row of figures looks still. · A visitor shows off her jewelry, and the Roman mother answers by pointing to her sons as her treasures."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/the-oath-of-the-horatii.html","Neoclassicism","The Oath of the Horatii","Jacques-Louis David","david-horatii","Jacques-Louis David · 1784–85 · French, 1748–1825. Oil on canvas, 130 × 167 1/4 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · David drew hard outlines and straight limbs and set the figures in a space no deeper than a stage. · The men are straight lines and sharp angles, the women soft sinking curves, so the men's side looks rigid by contrast. · The floor grid runs back to three plain arches, dark behind, so the space stops a few steps behind the figures. · Three Roman brothers swear to fight for their city; shown at the Paris Salon of 1785."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/self-portrait-with-two-pupils.html","Neoclassicism","Self-Portrait with Two Pupils","Adélaïde Labille-Guiard","labille-guiard-pupils","Adélaïde Labille-Guiard · 1785 · French, 1749–1803. Oil on canvas, 83 × 59 1/2 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Labille-Guiard painted satin, lace, straw and skin each at full sharpness. · The highlights on the silver-blue silk are crisp, hard-edged strokes of near-white, so the dress reads as stiff, shiny satin. · Lace, straw hat and feathers each get a different touch, so each material can be told apart at a glance. · A painter at her easel with her two students, shown at the Salon of 1785."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/the-death-of-socrates.html","Neoclassicism","The Death of Socrates","Jacques-Louis David","david-socrates","Jacques-Louis David · 1787 · Oil on canvas, 51 × 77 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · David let line and gesture carry the picture: one raised finger, one hand reaching for the cup. · The followers on both sides bend and turn away, so their curved backs set off the upright body and raised arm. · Folds and limbs have clean edges against the dark gray wall, so each pose reads in outline, the raised finger most of all. · The philosopher drinks the poison rather than renounce his teaching; the old man at the foot of the bed is Plato."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/antoine-laurent-lavoisier-and-marie-anne-lavoisier.html","Neoclassicism","Antoine Laurent Lavoisier and Marie Anne Lavoisier","Jacques-Louis David","david-lavoisier","Jacques-Louis David · 1788 · Oil on canvas, 102 1/4 × 76 1/2 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · David described every glass instrument exactly, down to the reflections. · Two sharp window-pane highlights sit on the floor globe, so the glass looks hollow and clear, the red cloth showing through. · The wall behind is plain dark gray, so the tall glass cylinder on the table shows up clearly against it. · A chemist and his wife at a table of laboratory glassware."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/psyche-revived-by-cupids-kiss.html","Neoclassicism","Psyche Revived by Cupid's Kiss","Antonio Canova","canova-psyche","Antonio Canova · 1787–93 · Italian, 1757–1822. Marble and metal, 61 × 66 1/4 × 39 3/4 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Canova polished the marble until it looks like soft skin and built the group on two crossing diagonals. · Cupid's leg and wing rise to upper right and Psyche's body crosses them, so the eye lands on the faces at the crossing. · The hair and drapery are cut in fine, close detail, so the broad polished bodies beside them look even smoother. · Cupid waking Psyche with a kiss, from the ancient tale of the two lovers."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/the-sleep-of-endymion.html","Neoclassicism","The Sleep of Endymion","Anne-Louis Girodet","girodet-endymion","Anne-Louis Girodet · 1791 · French, 1767–1824. Oil on canvas, 78 × 102 3/4 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Girodet lit an idealized body with a single moonbeam and gave it soft edges in place of David's hard outline. · Pale light falls through the leaves from upper left, so the chest and face glow while the legs fade into dark. · Only a muted rose cloth and the leopard skin break the silvery flesh, browns and dark greens, so the picture reads as night. · Endymion asleep on a leopard skin while the boy Zephyr parts the branches; painted in Rome and shown at the Salon of 1793."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/the-death-of-marat.html","Neoclassicism","The Death of Marat","Jacques-Louis David","david-marat","Jacques-Louis David · 1793 · Oil on canvas, 65 × 50 1/2 in. Royal Museums of Fine Arts of Belgium, Brussels · David left the upper half as bare, brushed brown wall and set everything else in a band across the bottom. · The wall is darkest behind the lit head and lightens toward the right, so head and shoulder stand out sharply. · The few colors, white sheet, green cloth and yellow wood, all sit in the lower band, so the brown wall above looks even emptier. · A murdered revolutionary journalist; the crate carries the painter's dedication, dated to year two of the Republic."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/the-intervention-of-the-sabine-women.html","Neoclassicism","The Intervention of the Sabine Women","Jacques-Louis David","david-sabines","Jacques-Louis David · 1799 · Oil on canvas, 151 1/2 × 205 1/2 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · David gave the nude warriors the outlines of statues and held them mid-action. · Against the busy crowd the two main warriors keep clean outlines and smooth modeling, so their pale bodies look like marble statues. · The fortress behind softens in haze while the front figures stay sharp, so the warriors look close and solid. · Women throw themselves between their Roman husbands and their Sabine fathers to end a battle."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/madame-recamier.html","Neoclassicism","Madame Récamier","Jacques-Louis David","david-recamier","Jacques-Louis David · 1800 · Oil on canvas, 68 1/2 × 96 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · David kept the picture sparse: a bare room, a daybed, a lamp, a footstool and a great deal of empty wall. · The white dress is the lightest shape against the gray-brown wall and floor, so the eye goes straight to the figure. · Thin, loose brushing leaves the wall with nothing on it, so the room looks empty and the smoothly finished face looks sharper. · Juliette Récamier in a white dress; the painter left the picture a sketch."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/napoleon-crossing-the-alps.html","Neoclassicism","Napoleon Crossing the Alps","Jacques-Louis David","david-napoleon-alps","Jacques-Louis David · 1800 · Oil on canvas, 102 × 87 in. Musée national du château de Malmaison, Rueil-Malmaison · David built the picture on one strong diagonal: a rearing horse against the slope. · One diagonal runs from the hind legs at lower right to the cloak at upper left, so horse and rider seem to climb. · The white horse and yellow cloak are sharp-edged against gray sky and rock, so the diagonal reads as one bright shape. · The First Consul crossing the Alps in 1800; the rocks below carry the names of Bonaparte, Hannibal and Charlemagne."],[9,"/study/art-history/neoclassicism/rome-paris-and-london-1760s-1806/perseus-with-the-head-of-medusa.html","Neoclassicism","Perseus with the Head of Medusa","Antonio Canova","canova-perseus","Antonio Canova · 1804–6 · Marble, 95 1/2 × 75 1/2 × 40 1/2 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Canova used contrapposto, the stance of the ancient statues, and kept every limb clear of the body. · The weight is on one leg and the other trails back, tilting hips against shoulders, so the body looks balanced, not stiff. · The cloak hangs from the outstretched arm and falls behind the legs, not across them, so nothing hides the body. · A modern marble modeled on the Apollo Belvedere, made after Napoleon's troops took the ancient statue to Paris."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance.html","Northern Renaissance · Overview","What to Look For","","eyck-arnolfini","Oil, close detail and the print · Early Netherlandish painting is the name for work made in the Low Countries from about 1420 to 1550. Jan van Eyck is credited with lifelike surfaces and natural light, made possible by oil paint laid in thin see-through layers called glazes. · A triptych is a painting in three sections, the outer two sometimes hinged as wings that close over the center. In Germany around 1500, Albrecht Dürer took engraving to a new richness of detail. · Jan van Eyck · The Arnolfini Portrait · 1434"],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/annunciation-triptych-merode-altarpiece.html","Northern Renaissance","Annunciation Triptych (Merode Altarpiece)","Workshop of Robert Campin","campin-merode","Workshop of Robert Campin · about 1427–32 · Netherlandish, about 1375–1444. Oil on oak, 25 3/8 × 46 3/8 in. open. The Met Cloisters, New York · The Annunciation is set in a room like the owners' own, with a kettle, a towel, a candle and a bench. · The tabletop is seen from above but the jug on it from the side, so the room seems to tip forward. · Sharp-edged folds in the red and white robes catch light on every ridge, so the cloth looks stiff and angular. · The center panel came first. The man kneeling at left then ordered the wings, and his wife was added behind him in the 1430s."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/the-ghent-altarpiece.html","Northern Renaissance","The Ghent Altarpiece","Hubert and Jan van Eyck","eyck-ghent","Hubert and Jan van Eyck · completed 1432 · Hubert died 1426; Jan active 1422, died 1441. Oil on wood, about 137 × 181 in. open. St Bavo's Cathedral, Ghent · Each material catches light in its own way: metal, gems, hair, skin and cloth are painted to look real enough to touch. · Gold arches sit behind the heads of the three large figures at the top, so the eye goes to that row first. · In the meadow below, hills and towers turn pale blue toward the horizon, so the land seems to run back for miles. · An inscription on the frame says Hubert began the work and Jan finished it in 1432 for Joos Vijd, whose chapel in Ghent held it."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/portrait-of-a-man-self-portrait.html","Northern Renaissance","Portrait of a Man (Self Portrait?)","Jan van Eyck","eyck-man-turban","Jan van Eyck · 1433 · Oil on wood, 10 1/4 × 7 1/2 in. National Gallery, London · A small face is painted in close detail and lit hard against the dark, so every wrinkle and vein shows. · The side of the face turned away is lit and the cheek nearest the ear is in shadow, so the head looks round. · The red headdress is the only strong color, so the eye goes to the head first and the dark coat barely registers. · The original frame carries Van Eyck's motto, his name and the date 21 October 1433. The background was once blue; the black is almost certainly later paint."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/the-arnolfini-portrait.html","Northern Renaissance","The Arnolfini Portrait","Jan van Eyck","eyck-arnolfini","Jan van Eyck · 1434 · Oil on oak, 32 1/4 × 23 1/2 in. National Gallery, London · A round mirror at the center of the back wall repeats the room in miniature, seen from the other side. · The man's face, the woman's white headdress and the brass chandelier catch the same light from the left, which ties the scene together. · The mirror sits at the center between the two heads and above the joined hands, so the eye keeps returning to it. · Van Eyck signed the wall above the mirror in Latin: \"Jan van Eyck was here, 1434.\" The mirror's reflection includes two people coming in at a door."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/saint-luke-drawing-the-virgin.html","Northern Renaissance","Saint Luke Drawing the Virgin","Rogier van der Weyden","weyden-st-luke","Rogier van der Weyden · about 1435–40 · Netherlandish, about 1400–1464. Oil and tempera on panel, 54 1/8 × 43 5/8 in. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · Two large figures frame an opening, and the view through the gap runs past a wall to a far horizon. · The Virgin's dark blue robe and Luke's red robe sit on opposite sides, so the pale opening between them draws the eye. · Two small figures at the wall have their backs turned, so the eye follows their gaze out over the water. · Saint Luke was the patron saint of painters. The picture may have been made for the painters' guild chapel in Brussels, and the museum says Luke may be a self-portrait."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/the-descent-from-the-cross.html","Northern Renaissance","The Descent from the Cross","Rogier van der Weyden","weyden-descent","Rogier van der Weyden · before 1443 · Oil on panel, 80 1/2 × 103 in. Museo del Prado, Madrid · Ten nearly life-size figures crowd a gilded box whose space does not match the room they need. · The fainting Virgin's body bends the same way as Christ's above, so the two figures read as a pair. · The bent figures at the far left and right curve inward, so the eye is turned back toward the center. · Apparently painted for the chapel of the crossbowmen's guild in Leuven; two small crossbows hang in the painted corners. A copy dated 1443 gives the latest possible date."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/portinari-triptych.html","Northern Renaissance","Portinari Triptych","Hugo van der Goes","goes-portinari","Hugo van der Goes · about 1477–78 · Flemish, about 1440–1482. Oil on wood, 107 3/4 × 256 3/4 in. open. Uffizi, Florence · Figures in one scene are painted at different sizes: saints tower over the kneeling family they protect. · The standing saints on the wings are far taller than the donors kneeling below them, so rank shows at a glance. · A wide patch of bare ground is left around the child, so the small pale body is the first thing seen in the center. · Tommaso Portinari, agent of the Medici bank in Bruges, ordered it for a hospital church in Florence. It arrived by sea in 1483, and Florentine painters admired its realistic objects, landscape and faces."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/death-and-the-miser.html","Northern Renaissance","Death and the Miser","Hieronymus Bosch","bosch-death-miser","Hieronymus Bosch · about 1485/90 · Netherlandish, about 1450–1516. Oil on panel, 36 5/8 × 12 3/16 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · The paint is so thin that Bosch's drawing shows through, and the tall panel squeezes the room into a narrow slot. · The bed runs back at a slant and the vault ribs close in overhead, so the narrow room looks deep. · Pink bed hangings and one green robe sit on pale grays and browns, so the bed and the standing man stand out. · Probably the outside of a wing from a lost altarpiece. The scene follows printed books on the art of dying, published in the Netherlands in 1488 and 1491."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/the-haywain-triptych.html","Northern Renaissance","The Haywain Triptych","Hieronymus Bosch","bosch-haywain","Hieronymus Bosch · 1512–15 · Oil on panel, 58 × 88 1/4 in. with frame. Museo del Prado, Madrid · Crowds of small figures are set down fast, some faces just a few dots, across three panels that end in Hell. · Green hills fill the left and center panels and black and red fill the right, so the last panel breaks away from the others. · The pale yellow haystack fills the middle of the center panel, so the crowd reads as one mass pulled toward the hay. · The cart comes from a Bible verse and a Flemish saying that everyone grabs as much as they can. A second signed version at El Escorial is a copy of this one."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/temptations-of-saint-anthony.html","Northern Renaissance","Temptations of Saint Anthony","Hieronymus Bosch","bosch-st-anthony","Hieronymus Bosch · about 1500 · Oil on oak; center panel 51 3/4 × 46 3/4 in., wings 51 3/4 × 20 3/4 in. Museu Nacional de Arte Antiga, Lisbon · Sky, land, water and fire are packed into one dark landscape and filled with creatures built from mismatched parts. · Orange flames from a burning village light the upper left of the center panel, so that corner pulls the eye in a dark picture. · Small patches of bright red recur in all three panels, so the eye jumps from one group of creatures to the next. · Signed at the lower left of the center panel. Closed, the wings show Christ on the way from his arrest to Calvary, painted almost without color."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/the-garden-of-earthly-delights-triptych.html","Northern Renaissance","The Garden of Earthly Delights Triptych","Hieronymus Bosch","bosch-garden","Hieronymus Bosch · 1490–1500 · Oil on oak; 73 1/4 in. high, center panel 68 in. wide, wings 30 in. wide. Museo del Prado, Madrid · One landscape with a high horizon crosses all three panels, and crowds of small figures are stacked up the surface in bands. · The horizon sits near the top of every panel, so the ground rises like a wall and far figures stay clear. · Tall pink and blue structures stand in a row along the far water, so the top of the center panel looks ordered. · First recorded in 1517 in the Nassau palace in Brussels. Technical study shows Bosch first planned a different scene, the creation of Eve, for the left panel."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/the-four-horsemen-from-the-apocalypse.html","Northern Renaissance","The Four Horsemen, from The Apocalypse","Albrecht Dürer","durer-four-horsemen","Albrecht Dürer · 1498 · German, 1471–1528. Woodcut, sheet 15 1/4 × 11 7/16 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Four riders are overlapped and driven across the sheet on one diagonal, which gives a woodcut speed and weight. · Even parallel lines fill the sky with a middle gray, so the white horses and clouds stand out against the tone. · The four horses overlap on one slant with their heads to the right, so the group reads as a single forward rush. · A woodcut is printed from a wood block with everything but the lines cut away. This is one of fifteen scenes from the Book of Revelation published in 1498, the series that made Dürer famous."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/self-portrait-with-fur-trimmed-robe.html","Northern Renaissance","Self-Portrait with Fur-Trimmed Robe","Albrecht Dürer","durer-self-1500","Albrecht Dürer · 1500 · Paint on limewood panel, 26 1/2 × 19 1/4 in. Alte Pinakothek, Munich · The head faces straight out with both halves of the face matched, a pose then used for images of Christ. · Hair falls evenly on both sides and the nose runs down the center line, so the face looks fixed and still. · The pale hand is set low on the center line under the face, so the eye moves between hand and face. · The Latin inscription at right gives his name, his city, Nuremberg, and his age, twenty-eight. The museum notes that the features are still Dürer's own."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/young-hare-feldhase.html","Northern Renaissance","Young Hare (Feldhase)","Albrecht Dürer","durer-hare","Albrecht Dürer · 1502 · Watercolor and bodycolor with white highlights, 10 × 9 in. Albertina, Vienna · Watercolor and opaque paint together make fur so fine that each hair seems to be there. · Light comes from the left and the shadow falls to the right, so the body looks round and seems to rest on a surface. · The paper around the animal is left blank, so nothing competes with the fur. · Bodycolor is opaque water-based paint. The eye reflects the bars of a window, and so many copies were made that at least 25 hares were once in European collections."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/adam-and-eve.html","Northern Renaissance","Adam and Eve","Albrecht Dürer","durer-adam-eve","Albrecht Dürer · 1504 · Engraving, 9 7/8 × 7 7/8 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · An engraving is cut into a metal plate, and fine lines packed close together give form, texture and shading at once. · Skin, bark, fur and leaves each get a different kind of mark, so every surface can be told apart without color. · The two pale bodies stand against dark, densely lined trees, so the figures come forward. · The poses follow a system of ideal proportion that Dürer studied all his life. Adam recalls an ancient statue of Apollo dug up in the late 1400s."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/melencolia-i.html","Northern Renaissance","Melencolia I","Albrecht Dürer","durer-melencolia","Albrecht Dürer · 1514 · Engraving, sheet 9 1/2 × 7 3/8 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The engraving runs from bare white paper to near black, with many grays between, all made from lines. · The ball shades from light to dark and the stone block changes tone face by face, so one looks round and the other flat-sided. · The brightest paper is kept for the lap of the dress and the burst of light at upper left, so both hold the eye. · One of three large engravings of 1513–14 known as the master engravings. The winged figure, head on hand among tools of geometry, stands for melancholy, which thinkers of the time tied to creative genius."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/the-four-apostles.html","Northern Renaissance","The Four Apostles","Albrecht Dürer","durer-four-apostles","Albrecht Dürer · 1526 · Paint on two limewood panels, 83 3/4 × 30 in. and 83 1/2 × 30 in. Alte Pinakothek, Munich · Four figures fill two tall panels almost edge to edge, and heavy robes in a few plain colors hide the bodies. · The red robe at left and the white robe at right are the two largest shapes, so the pair of panels balances. · Long folds run from shoulder to floor on both front figures, so the men look tall and heavy. · Dürer gave the panels to the city council of Nuremberg in 1526. Bible passages in Luther's translation run along the bottom, warning rulers against false prophets."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/isenheim-altarpiece-the-crucifixion.html","Northern Renaissance","Isenheim Altarpiece: The Crucifixion","Matthias Grünewald","grunewald-isenheim","Matthias Grünewald · 1512–16 · Born in Würzburg about 1475–80, died 1528. Oil and tempera on limewood. Musée Unterlinden, Colmar · Christ's body is gray-green, torn and stuck with thorns, and hangs against a dark night sky. · The arms pull the crossbar down into a bow and the fingers splay stiffly, so the body looks heavy. · Red and white robes at the sides are the only bright color, so the pale body stands alone at the center. · Painted for the hospital chapel of the Antonite monks at Isenheim, who nursed people with a disease that caused gangrene. This view showed when the altarpiece was closed."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/isenheim-altarpiece-the-resurrection.html","Northern Renaissance","Isenheim Altarpiece: The Resurrection","Matthias Grünewald","grunewald-resurrection","Matthias Grünewald · 1512–16 · Oil and tempera on limewood. Musée Unterlinden, Colmar · Light pours out of the figure: the face nearly melts into a glowing disk, and the night around the disk stays dark. · The disk shifts from yellow at the center through orange-red to a blue rim, so the figure seems to give off heat and light. · The shroud runs from white and blue at the tomb to red and yellow near the head, so the cloth looks lit from above. · This panel was seen when the outer wings were opened. The museum describes it as the answer of hope to the Crucifixion on the outside."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/the-ambassadors.html","Northern Renaissance","The Ambassadors","Hans Holbein the Younger","holbein-ambassadors","Hans Holbein the Younger · 1533 · German, 1497/8–1543. Oil on oak, 81 1/2 × 82 1/2 in. National Gallery, London · The long smear across the floor is a skull, stretched so far that the shape reads only from a sharp angle. · The green curtain closes off the back just behind the men, so the figures and objects press forward. · The two men stand at the edges with the shelves between them, so the objects hold the center of the picture. · The trick is called anamorphosis. The sitters are Jean de Dinteville, French ambassador to England, and his friend Georges de Selve, a bishop; the skull is a reminder of death."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/the-dutch-proverbs.html","Northern Renaissance","The Dutch Proverbs","Pieter Bruegel the Elder","bruegel-proverbs","Pieter Bruegel the Elder · 1559 · Netherlandish, about 1525/30–1569. Paint on oak panel, 46 1/4 × 64 1/2 in. Gemäldegalerie, Berlin · More than a hundred sayings are acted out side by side in one village, each scene separate from the next. · The woman in red and the man in the blue cloak at the center are the strongest colors nearby, so the eye starts there. · Strong reds and blues recur from corner to corner, which ties the scattered scenes into one picture. · One of Bruegel's first large paintings. An old title, The Blue Cloak, comes from the woman at center who drapes one over her husband, a sign that she deceives him."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/hunters-in-the-snow-winter.html","Northern Renaissance","Hunters in the Snow (Winter)","Pieter Bruegel the Elder","bruegel-hunters","Pieter Bruegel the Elder · 1565 · Paint on oak panel, 45 3/4 × 63 3/4 in. Kunsthistorisches Museum, Vienna · The view starts on a hilltop and drops into a wide valley, and a few cold colors hold the whole scene together. · Dark tree trunks and hunters step down the slope from the left, so the eye follows them into the valley. · The sky and the frozen ponds share one gray-green with white between, so the whole picture reads as cold. · Part of a series of six season paintings made for the circle of the Antwerp collector Nicolaes Jongelinck. The museum calls it the first, and the most prominent, winter landscape in European painting."],[10,"/study/art-history/northern-renaissance/northern-europe-1427-1568/peasant-wedding.html","Northern Renaissance","Peasant Wedding","Pieter Bruegel the Elder","bruegel-wedding","Pieter Bruegel the Elder · 1568 · Paint on oak panel, 44 1/2 × 64 1/2 in. Kunsthistorisches Museum, Vienna · A scene that looks caught by chance is carefully arranged around a long table set on a slant. · The table narrows as it runs from the right foreground to the far door at left, so the barn looks deep and crowded. · Red caps and jackets repeat from the front to the far door, so the eye is led along the table. · The bride sits in front of the green cloth under a paper crown. The food is carried on a door taken off its hinges, and the panel was later cut at the bottom."],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Oil vs Acrylic","","rand-1841-tube-patent","For over five centuries oil has been the paint most painters chose, and John Rand's metal tube of 1841 made it easy to carry. Acrylic came out of the plastics industry, first as a solvent paint called Magna, then in water; by 1965 most makers of artists' paint sold a water-based acrylic."],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/two-binders.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Two Binders","","ostade-painter-studio","Paint is pigment, a colored powder, glued in place by a binder that hardens: in oil paint a plant oil that hardens in air, in acrylic tiny plastic droplets in water. · Two assistants grind colors under the stairs: paint is made by working powder into a binder, here by hand. · The nearer assistant works at a small pot: powder and binder start separate, so one pigment can become oil paint or acrylic. · Pieter Bruegel the Elder · The Harvesters · 1565 · The same oil serves for washes so thin the drawing underneath still shows and for strokes thick enough to hide it. · Water thins acrylic and cleans its brushes; oil's solvents need ventilation, and oily rags, which can ignite by themselves, go in water in a closed can."],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/two-binders.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","The Harvesters","Pieter Bruegel the Elder","thumb/bruegel-harvesters","1565 · The same oil serves for washes so thin the drawing underneath still shows and for strokes thick enough to hide it. · Two Binders"],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/oil-cures.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Oil Cures","","oa-oil-cures","Oil paint does not dry by evaporating; it cures, taking up oxygen until its molecules join into a single solid mesh. · Every oxygen arrow meets the top surface; the paint below waits for oxygen to work its way down. · The deeper amber band on top has hardened; the paler paint below is still soft, which is why thick paint cures very slowly. · Albert Pinkham Ryder · Moonlight Marine · 1870–90 · Ryder built up thick paint layer after layer; wide cracks now cross sky and sea, and the Met reports that the outlines of clouds, sails and hull have shifted since. · Painters work \"fat over lean\", oilier paint on top, so the upper layers stay more flexible; paint that sets over a soft layer can wrinkle and crack."],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/oil-cures.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","Moonlight Marine","Albert Pinkham Ryder","thumb/ryder-moonlight-marine","1870–90 · Ryder built up thick paint layer after layer; wide cracks now cross sky and sea, and the Met reports that the outlines of clouds, sails and hull have shifted since. · Oil Cures"],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/acrylic-dries.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Acrylic Dries","","oa-acrylic-dries","Acrylic dries by losing water: as it leaves, the plastic droplets crowd together and fuse into a film. · In the middle panel the water line has dropped and the droplets press together: water was what kept them apart. · In the last panel they are one film, thinner than the stack of droplets, because the gaps have closed up. · David Nicholson · Early, Still · 2025 · Each flat block set quickly enough for the next to go straight over it, and no color underneath has smudged into the one above. · For about a week after it feels dry, sometimes longer, water can still soften acrylic; dried in the cold, the droplets merge poorly into a porous or powdery film."],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/acrylic-dries.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","Early, Still","David Nicholson","thumb/dn-early-still","2025 · Each flat block set quickly enough for the next to go straight over it, and no color underneath has smudged into the one above. · Acrylic Dries"],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/open-time.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Open Time","","vermeer-lady-writing-detail","Open time is how long paint stays wet enough to move. Acrylic is dry to the touch in minutes to a few hours, oil in about two days, so oil can be reworked long after acrylic sets. · The fingers and the blue cloth's folds have soft edges: where a stroke meets still-wet paint, the colors mingle. · Researchers at the National Gallery of Art trace that blur to working wet into wet, which oil's long open time allows. · Claude Monet · Stacks of Wheat (End of Summer) · 1890–91 · The bigger stack's outline, worked wet into wet, is soft and broken against the hills, while sky color Monet added over dry strokes stays in distinct streaks. · Because oil cures so slowly, painters are advised to wait six months or more before varnishing."],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/open-time.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","Stacks of Wheat (End of Summer)","Claude Monet","thumb/monet-stacks-of-wheat-end-of-summer","1890–91 · The bigger stack's outline, worked wet into wet, is soft and broken against the hills, while sky color Monet added over dry strokes stays in distinct streaks. · Open Time"],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/blending.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Blending","","ginevra-blending-detail","Blending means working two colors into each other while both are wet, so the change between them is gradual. · The brown trees thin into the pale sky in soft, broken patches, which read as a gradual change instead of an edge. · The National Gallery of Art notes that Leonardo blended with his hands where sky meets juniper; his fingerprints remain in the paint, then soft enough to push around. · Pietro Rotari · A Girl with a Flower in Her Hair · 1760–62 · Rotari blended the strokes in her face, the National Gallery of Art reports, so her skin turns from light to shade without a seam; the flower and her features, finished last, stay crisp. · Painters keep acrylic workable by misting the palette or adding a little retarder, which slows drying; too much leaves a soft film that can stay tacky."],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/blending.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","A Girl with a Flower in Her Hair","Pietro Rotari","thumb/rotari-girl-flower","1760–62 · Rotari blended the strokes in her face, the National Gallery of Art reports, so her skin turns from light to shade without a seam; the flower and her features, finished last, stay crisp. · Blending"],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/color-shift-as-it-dries.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Color Shift as It Dries","","oa-color-shift","Acrylic goes on with a milky cast; as it dries the binder turns clear, and colors often dry slightly darker. · In the left square pale rings, the loose droplets, bounce light back before it reaches the red pigment grains, so the square looks lighter. · On the right the rings are gone: the droplets have fused into a clear film, light reaches the pigment, and the same red reads deeper. · David Nicholson · Umbrella, Reverie · 2024 · The painter recalls these colors deepening as the acrylic dried; the canopy's oranges, yellows and blues now sit clear and strong against the dark shapes. · Dry acrylic can also look flatter than it did wet; a varnish brings that depth back."],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/color-shift-as-it-dries.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","Umbrella, Reverie","David Nicholson","thumb/dn-umbrella-reverie","2024 · The painter recalls these colors deepening as the acrylic dried; the canopy's oranges, yellows and blues now sit clear and strong against the dark shapes. · Color Shift as It Dries"],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/impasto.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Impasto","","van-gogh-green-wheat-detail","Impasto is paint laid on thickly enough to hold the shape of each brush or knife stroke. The raised ridges catch light and cast small shadows, so the strokes themselves show. · Lavender swirls cross the white as raised bands instead of mixing into it. · Bristle grooves run through each swirl and lips of paint stand where the brush lifted: stiff paint keeps every mark of the tool. · Rembrandt van Rijn · Lucretia · 1664 · The National Gallery of Art finds extensive palette-knife work in her left cuff, at the right of the picture, and low on her dress, where thick, broken pale patches catch the light. · Some oil colors hold peaks straight from the tube; acrylic texture is often built with gels or pastes thickened with fillers."],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/impasto.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","Lucretia","Rembrandt van Rijn","thumb/rembrandt-lucretia","1664 · The National Gallery of Art finds extensive palette-knife work in her left cuff, at the right of the picture, and low on her dress, where thick, broken pale patches catch the light. · Impasto"],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/painting-over-dry-paint.html","Oil vs Acrylic","Painting Over Dry Paint","","oa-painting-over","Once a layer is dry, new paint no longer mixes with it, so the layer can be changed or covered completely; changes hidden this way are called pentimenti. · The two red shapes are still whole under the later layer: painting over hides earlier work but does not remove it. · The dashed lines run through the later paint toward the red shapes: X-rays can show what the eye cannot, which is how hidden changes are found. · Titian · Venus with a Mirror · about 1555 · X-rays show a double portrait of a man and a woman underneath, turned on its side; Titian kept the man's jacket and made it her red velvet wrap. · Oil paint may be laid over acrylic, but acrylic should not be put over oil."],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/painting-over-dry-paint.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","Venus with a Mirror","Titian","thumb/titian-venus-with-a-mirror","about 1555 · X-rays show a double portrait of a man and a woman underneath, turned on its side; Titian kept the man's jacket and made it her red velvet wrap. · Painting Over Dry Paint"],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/how-oil-ages.html","Oil vs Acrylic","How Oil Ages","","chaperon-craquelure","Over the decades oil paint stiffens, can yellow and grows more see-through, so changes the painter covered may start to show. · A fine net of cracks splits the face into small irregular islands: stiff old paint cannot stretch when the surface under it moves, so it breaks. · The cracks run through light and shadow alike: the whole paint layer stiffened and shrank with age, and split. · Diego Velázquez · Philip IV · about 1628 · Velázquez changed the king's legs and cloak and painted over the first versions; their outlines have begun to show through the top layer as faint shapes beside the cloak and legs. · One study found that oil which yellows in the dark mostly loses that yellow after a short spell in daylight."],[11,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints-compared/how-oil-ages.html","Oil vs Acrylic · Example","Philip IV","Diego Velázquez","thumb/velazquez-philip-iv","about 1628 · Velázquez changed the king's legs and cloak and painted over the first versions; their outlines have begun to show through the top layer as faint shapes beside the cloak and legs. · How Oil Ages"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials.html","Pigments and Materials","Pigments and Materials","","amenhotep-palette","The earliest pigments, the colored powders that go into paint, were dug from the ground or burned, and Egyptian blue, fired in a furnace, is the oldest known synthetic pigment. Most laboratory-made colors arrived after 1800; in 1841 an American painter, John Goffe Rand, devised the collapsible metal paint tube."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/whites.html","Pigments and Materials","Whites","","rickard-white-lead-pot","Until about 1900 the painter's main white was lead white, made by letting the fumes of vinegar eat into lead until it turned to a white crust. · Left: a disc of lead, pierced so more of the metal is exposed to the fumes. · Right: the pot. Vinegar filled the narrow lower part; the discs rested on the shoulder above, so fumes reached the lead but the liquid did not. · Rembrandt van Rijn · Self Portrait at the Age of 63 · 1669 · Rembrandt laid lead white tinted with ochres, colors dug from the earth, thickly on the lit cheek over brown paint, making the face the brightest part of a dark picture. · Lead white is toxic; in 1978 the United States outlawed lead in paint for homes."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/whites.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Self Portrait at the Age of 63","Rembrandt van Rijn","thumb/rembrandt-self-portrait-63","1669 · Rembrandt laid lead white tinted with ochres, colors dug from the earth, thickly on the lit cheek over brown paint, making the face the brightest part of a dark picture. · Whites"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/blacks.html","Pigments and Materials","Blacks","","lampblack-chamber-1906","Traditional blacks take their color from carbon, collected as soot from a flame or left behind when bones or vine twigs are charred. · In the furnace at the lower right, resin burns with too little air, giving dense smoke instead of a clean flame. · The smoke rises into the tall chamber and its soot settles as lamp black, gathered once the chamber has cooled. · Édouard Manet · Music in the Tuileries Gardens · 1862 · Manet built the dark hats and coats mostly from ivory black, tinted with cobalt blue and an orange-brown ochre, so they read as solid dark shapes beside the pale dresses. · Ivory black in tubes is almost always bone black: calcium phosphate from the bone, with carbon only part of the mix."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/blacks.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Music in the Tuileries Gardens","Édouard Manet","thumb/manet-music-tuileries","1862 · Manet built the dark hats and coats mostly from ivory black, tinted with cobalt blue and an orange-brown ochre, so they read as solid dark shapes beside the pale dresses. · Blacks"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/earth-pigments.html","Pigments and Materials","Earth Pigments","","sowerby-ochres","Yellow and red earths owe their color to iron minerals mixed into clay and quartz. · Left: yellow ochres, pale straw to rust brown. Right: red ochres, soft pink to deep red-brown. A different iron mineral colors each plate. · Burnt, the yellows can turn nearly as red as those on the right: fire drives water out of goethite, the yellow iron mineral, leaving hematite, the red. · Rembrandt van Rijn · Self-Portrait · 1659 · Analysis found red, yellow and brown ochres, lead white, carbon black and just traces of vermilion, a mineral red, so the whole picture, flesh included, holds to warm browns, tans and dull reds. · In South Africa's Blombos Cave, people were grinding red ochre into a paint mixture about 100,000 years ago."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/earth-pigments.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Self-Portrait","Rembrandt van Rijn","thumb/rembrandt-self-portrait-1659","1659 · Analysis found red, yellow and brown ochres, lead white, carbon black and just traces of vermilion, a mineral red, so the whole picture, flesh included, holds to warm browns, tans and dull reds. · Earth Pigments"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/reds.html","Pigments and Materials","Reds","","cochineal-plantation","A red could be a mineral, such as vermilion, or a lake: dye from a root or an insect fixed onto a colorless powder so it can be used as paint. · The cactus rows are a cochineal farm: the insect feeds on the pads and holds the red dye in its body, so workers pick it off by hand. · At the left, the harvest cures on a mat. It yields a dye, not yet a paint. · Titian · Venus and Adonis · about 1555–60 · Adonis wears both kinds of red: vermilion in the orange-red sleeve, a lake in the pinker tunic. · Titian's lake was probably extracted from scraps of red-dyed fabric, and its dye has been matched mainly to cochineal."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/reds.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Venus and Adonis","Titian","thumb/titian-venus-adonis","about 1555–60 · Adonis wears both kinds of red: vermilion in the orange-red sleeve, a lake in the pinker tunic. · Reds"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/blues.html","Pigments and Materials","Blues","","sowerby-lapis-azurite","Before 1700 painters had only a few blues. Two were crushed minerals: ultramarine, from the stone lapis lazuli, and azurite, from a copper ore. · Left: lapis lazuli. Ground alone it comes out grayish, so painters worked the blue out of a paste of wax and resin. · Right: azurite, with darker, blocky crystals; its green patches are a reminder that azurite can turn green. · Johannes Vermeer · Girl with a Pearl Earring · about 1665 · Vermeer painted the turban's blue in top-quality natural ultramarine, with lead white in the light and chalk in the shade, so one blue turns from pale to deep around the head. · In fifteenth-century Florence the best ultramarine might cost as much as gold leaf, so painters usually kept it for the most important figure."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/blues.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Girl with a Pearl Earring","Johannes Vermeer","thumb/vermeer-girl-pearl-earring","about 1665 · Vermeer painted the turban's blue in top-quality natural ultramarine, with lead white in the light and chalk in the shade, so one blue turns from pale to deep around the head. · Blues"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/greens.html","Pigments and Materials","Greens","","sowerby-malachite","Many old greens came from copper: malachite, a crushed mineral, and verdigris, made by exposing copper to vinegar. · Malachite forms a crust of rounded green knobs over tan rock. The green is the copper mineral, and copper also makes verdigris green. · Painters added nothing to make the color: the green crust, broken off the rock and crushed, was the pigment. · Paolo Uccello · The Battle of San Romano · probably about 1438–40 · Uccello painted the hedge behind the knights green; the museum records that it has turned nearly black, so its oranges and white flowers now sit against a dark wall. · Cennino Cennini, in his painters' handbook of about 1400, told painters to keep verdigris and lead white apart, calling them \"mortal enemies\"."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/greens.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","The Battle of San Romano","Paolo Uccello","thumb/uccello-battle-san-romano","probably about 1438–40 · Uccello painted the hedge behind the knights green; the museum records that it has turned nearly black, so its oranges and white flowers now sit against a dark wall. · Greens"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/yellows.html","Pigments and Materials","Yellows","","sowerby-orpiment-crocoite","The most brilliant yellows before 1800 were often poisonous, made from arsenic or lead; chrome yellow, one of the vivid new yellows of the 1800s, grew out of a red-orange ore. · Left: orpiment, bright yellow where pure and gray where iron pyrites, another mineral, runs through it. The yellow is the arsenic mineral itself, so color and poison came together. · Right: crocoite, natural lead chromate. The chemist Vauquelin found chromium in its red-orange crystals in 1797 and by 1809 had made the same compound in the laboratory as chrome yellow. · Vincent van Gogh · Sunflowers · 1888 · Van Gogh painted most of the yellows and oranges in several chrome yellows, from pale lemon to deep orange, so wall, table, vase and flowers stay distinct though nearly everything is yellow."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/yellows.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Sunflowers","Vincent van Gogh","thumb/vangogh-sunflowers","1888 · Van Gogh painted most of the yellows and oranges in several chrome yellows, from pale lemon to deep orange, so wall, table, vase and flowers stay distinct though nearly everything is yellow. · Yellows"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/wood-panels.html","Pigments and Materials","Wood Panels","","pm-panel","From the 1200s to the early 1500s most surviving movable paintings were on wood, a single board or several joined with glue and dowels. · Left: three planks joined edge to edge. Wood absorbs and releases moisture as the air turns damp or dry, so the boards keep swelling and shrinking. · Right: the end view, flat when made, then bowed and split in the middle board; over centuries the swelling and shrinking can do this, loosening the paint on a panel's face. · Rogier van der Weyden · Portrait of a Lady · about 1460 · The single oak board leaves no joins to open across the face; its 265 tree rings end in 1438, so, allowing for lost rings and drying time, it was probably painted no earlier than about 1463."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/wood-panels.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Portrait of a Lady","Rogier van der Weyden","thumb/vanderweyden-portrait-lady","about 1460 · The single oak board leaves no joins to open across the face; its 265 tree rings end in 1438, so, allowing for lost rings and drying time, it was probably painted no earlier than about 1463. · Wood Panels"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/canvas.html","Pigments and Materials","Canvas","","encyclopedie-peinture-pl4-chassis","Canvas is strong woven cloth, usually linen, stretched taut across a wooden frame; the tension holds it flat and firm enough to take paint. · From behind, a bar across the middle and braces at the corners hold the frame square against the pull of the cloth. · Between the bars the frame is open. The cloth spans those gaps with no board behind it, which keeps canvas lighter than wood. · Titian · The Vendramin Family, venerating a Relic of the True Cross · about 1540–45 · Nearly 9 1/2 feet across, it is one unseamed length of patterned twill, so no seam crosses sky or figures; before the 1800s most large canvases were stitched from strips. · Venetian painters moved from panels and walls to canvas between about 1450 and 1550, though historians debate why."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/canvas.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","The Vendramin Family, venerating a Relic of the True Cross","Titian","thumb/titian-vendramin-family","about 1540–45 · Nearly 9 1/2 feet across, it is one unseamed length of patterned twill, so no seam crosses sky or figures; before the 1800s most large canvases were stitched from strips. · Canvas"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/grounds.html","Pigments and Materials","Grounds","","pm-layers","A ground is the first coat on a panel or canvas, a smooth, even surface for paint to cling to; its color shows through thin paint. · The white layer on the wood is gesso, gypsum bound with glue, the ground of Italian panels. Sanded glassy, it puts white under every color. · The thin dark line above it is the underdrawing: the painter drew on the ground, not the bare wood, before adding color. · Peter Paul Rubens · Samson and Delilah · about 1609–10 · Over a white chalk ground Rubens streaked a yellow-brown layer, then left it uncovered in Delilah's sleeve folds to serve as a warm half-shadow. · After about 1600 warm brown or red grounds became common; in the 1800s many painters, above all the Impressionists, returned to white."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/grounds.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Samson and Delilah","Peter Paul Rubens","thumb/rubens-samson-delilah","about 1609–10 · Over a white chalk ground Rubens streaked a yellow-brown layer, then left it uncovered in Delilah's sleeve folds to serve as a warm half-shadow. · Grounds"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/egg-tempera.html","Pigments and Materials","Egg Tempera","","fei-madonna-detail","In egg tempera yolk holds the pigment, making a fast-drying paint laid on in thin strokes; it was the standard paint for Italian panels through the Middle Ages and early Renaissance. · Egg paint dries too quickly to blend smoothly, so fine parallel strokes curve along the Child's arm and legs, tracing their roundness. · Shadows come from packing those strokes closer and darker, not from softening their edges. · Giotto · Madonna and Child · about 1310–15 · Giotto set the shadows in a green underpaint before the pink of the flesh went on; it shows through as a cool gray-green on the shaded sides of the faces. · By the late 1400s Italian painters often combined tempera with oil, and after about 1500 tempera became less common."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/egg-tempera.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Madonna and Child","Giotto","thumb/giotto-madonna-child","about 1310–15 · Giotto set the shadows in a green underpaint before the pink of the flesh went on; it shows through as a cool gray-green on the shaded sides of the faces. · Egg Tempera"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/fresco.html","Pigments and Materials","Fresco","","boscoreale-fragment","In fresco the color goes onto lime plaster that is still damp, so it ends up inside the wall instead of on it. · The reds and blue-greens have lasted since about 50–40 BCE: drawing carbon dioxide from the air, the plaster turned back into something like stone around the pigment. · Where the wall broke, the color broke with it; no loose skin of paint lifts along the ragged edges. · Spanish (Castile) · Lion, from the Chapter House of San Pedro de Arlanza · after 1200 · Painted on a monastery wall after 1200, then removed and fixed to canvas in the early 1900s, the lion still holds its pink, orange and gray, locked into the plaster. · Plaster was laid one day's work at a time; each such patch is called a giornata."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/fresco.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Lion, from the Chapter House of San Pedro de Arlanza","Spanish (Castile)","thumb/arlanza-lion","after 1200 · Painted on a monastery wall after 1200, then removed and fixed to canvas in the early 1900s, the lion still holds its pink, orange and gray, locked into the plaster. · Fresco"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/oil.html","Pigments and Materials","Oil","","stradanus-color-olivi","Oil paint holds its pigment in a drying oil, most often linseed, which sets slowly by absorbing oxygen. · At the right, assistants grind dry pigment into oil on slabs; the paint stays wet long enough for the master to work from the palette in his hand. · The Latin caption credits a painter it calls \"master Eyck\" with inventing oil color. That is a legend: oil was in use centuries earlier. · Rogier van der Weyden · The Magdalen Reading · about 1435 · Tests identified linseed oil; the lips melt together where wet paint met wet paint, and the brush pulled through the fur trim before it dried. · Oil also makes many pigments translucent, so thin layers of color can be glazed one over another."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/oil.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","The Magdalen Reading","Rogier van der Weyden","thumb/vanderweyden-magdalen-reading","about 1435 · Tests identified linseed oil; the lips melt together where wet paint met wet paint, and the brush pulled through the fur trim before it dried. · Oil"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/watercolor-and-gouache.html","Pigments and Materials","Watercolor and Gouache","","sargent-red-drapery-detail","Watercolor is pigment bound in gum arabic, a gum that dissolves in water, and thinned with water into transparent washes, so its brightness comes from the white paper beneath. · Patches left bare give the lightest lights, on the thigh and the cloth beneath it. · Where a wash dried, its pigment pooled into a darker rim, as along the folds of the red drapery. · Winslow Homer · The Return, Tynemouth · 1881 · Homer painted the soft pinks of sky and boat with pigments that hold their color in light, and they have survived; many of his other watercolors have lost washes like them. · Gouache carries more pigment for its gum, so it covers the paper instead of letting it glow through."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/watercolor-and-gouache.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","The Return, Tynemouth","Winslow Homer","thumb/homer-return-tynemouth","1881 · Homer painted the soft pinks of sky and boat with pigments that hold their color in light, and they have survived; many of his other watercolors have lost washes like them. · Watercolor and Gouache"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/pastel.html","Pigments and Materials","Pastel","","degas-hair-combed-detail","A pastel stick is pigment and a pale filler like chalk, barely held together by weak gum, so it rubs off as dry powder that clings to rough paper. · Yellow, orange and green strokes overlap down the cushion. Dry powder does not flow like wet paint, so the colors beneath still show. · Each stroke is grainy, a dusting of color, not a smooth film of paint. · Jean-Étienne Liotard · Portrait of Maria Frederike van Reede-Athlone at Seven Years of Age · 1755–56 · Liotard disliked showing his strokes; on vellum, a smooth prepared skin, he blended with a stump, a pointed tool, until face and blue velvet show almost no separate marks. · A pastel portrait needed no drying time and fewer sittings; pastel became fashionable after the painter Rosalba Carriera's stay in Paris in 1720–21."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/pastel.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Portrait of Maria Frederike van Reede-Athlone at Seven Years of Age","Jean-Étienne Liotard","thumb/liotard-maria-frederike","1755–56 · Liotard disliked showing his strokes; on vellum, a smooth prepared skin, he blended with a stump, a pointed tool, until face and blue velvet show almost no separate marks. · Pastel"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/encaustic.html","Pigments and Materials","Encaustic","","eutyches-portrait","Encaustic paint is pigment carried in beeswax. Wax stiffens as it cools, so most painters kept their colors hot; the word comes from the Greek for \"burnt in\". · Warm wax brushes on like paint, so the face could be shaded in smooth, curving strokes from light to dark, much as in oil. · In the white tunic the wax sits thicker, in ridged strokes; it stiffened before the brush marks could settle flat. · Roman Egypt · Portrait of a young woman in red · 90–120 CE · The wax went directly onto a board of linden only millimeters thick, and a hot tool left rows of parallel marks beside the nose. · Roughly 1,000 portraits like this survive from Roman Egyptian mummies, most from the 1st to 3rd centuries CE."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/encaustic.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","Portrait of a young woman in red","Roman Egypt","thumb/fayum-young-woman-red","90–120 CE · The wax went directly onto a board of linden only millimeters thick, and a hot tool left rows of parallel marks beside the nose. · Encaustic"],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/fading-and-darkening.html","Pigments and Materials","Fading and Darkening","","vangogh-roses","Some pigments shift color over the years as they react with light, with the air or with the oil that binds them. · The roses read as white today, but traces of pink at the petal edges show they were painted pink and have faded. · The thick paint of each bloom still stands up from the canvas: the brushwork survived where the color did not. · Vincent van Gogh · The Bedroom · 1889 · Analysts found specks of red lake in the doors, suspected to have been violet and now light blue, and geranium lake, probably faded, in the floor. · Paint shielded from light can keep its first color: in the Amsterdam version of The Bedroom, a stroke hidden by the frame is still lilac."],[12,"/study/pigments-and-materials/what-paintings-are-made-of/fading-and-darkening.html","Pigments and Materials · Example","The Bedroom","Vincent van Gogh","thumb/vangogh-bedroom","1889 · Analysts found specks of red lake in the doors, suspected to have been violet and now light blue, and geranium lake, probably faded, in the floor. · Fading and Darkening"],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism.html","Post-Impressionism · Overview","What to Look For","","vangogh-starry-night","Structure, feeling, flat color and pattern after Impressionism · Post-Impressionism is the label given, after the fact, to painters working in France from about 1884 to 1906 who started from Impressionism and went past it. They kept its bright color and visible brushwork, and used them for something other than recording light: solid structure, strong feeling, flat color, or pattern. · Vincent van Gogh · The Starry Night · 1889"],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/bathers-at-asnieres.html","Post-Impressionism","Bathers at Asnières","Georges Seurat","seurat-bathers","Georges Seurat · 1884 · French, 1859–1891. Oil on canvas, 79 1/4 × 118 in. The National Gallery, London · Seurat built the whole surface from small separate touches of paint and held every figure as still as a statue. · Unblended crisscross strokes of green and yellow cover the grass, so the bank looks sunlit and textured, not one flat green. · The large figures are in strict profile inside firm, unbroken outlines, so each reads as a fixed shape, like a carved relief, with no sign of movement. · Rejected by the official Salon and shown instead at the first Salon des Indépendants, a jury-free exhibition, in 1884. Seurat later reworked the orange hat in dots of yellow and blue."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-circus.html","Post-Impressionism","The Circus","Georges Seurat","seurat-circus","Georges Seurat · 1891 · Oil on canvas, 73 1/4 × 59 3/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Seurat set dots of red, yellow and blue side by side on a white ground; the eye mixes them at a distance. · The horse and the seating bands are left almost white, so the orange and yellow dots beside them look richer. · Blue dots sit among the orange and yellow in the ring's shadowed band, so from a distance it looks a grayer tan. · Shown unfinished at the Salon des Indépendants in March 1891; Seurat died days after the opening. The dark blue border is painted on the canvas itself."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-basket-of-apples.html","Post-Impressionism","The Basket of Apples","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-basket","Paul Cézanne · about 1893 · French, 1839–1906. Oil on canvas, 25 1/2 × 31 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Cézanne painted the table as if from two places at once: its front edge sits lower on the left of the cloth than on the right. · The tabletop tilts up steeply, so the fruit seems about to roll forward. · The basket tips forward and the bottle leans left, so each object looks as if seen from a slightly different spot. · Shown at Cézanne's first one-man exhibition, at the Vollard gallery in Paris, 1895."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-card-players.html","Post-Impressionism","The Card Players","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-card-players","Paul Cézanne · 1890–92 · Oil on canvas, 25 3/4 × 32 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Cézanne built the men like solid blocks: cylinders for arms and bodies, hats as plain dark shapes on top. · Coats and sleeves are shaded in broad patches with abrupt breaks between them, so the arms look thick and solid. · The wall behind is loosely scrubbed in pale blue and lilac, so the figures stand out as the solid parts. · The first of five pictures of card players. Each sitter, a local farm worker, posed alone for studies that were then combined."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/mont-sainte-victoire.html","Post-Impressionism","Mont Sainte-Victoire","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-sainte-victoire","Paul Cézanne · 1902–04 · Oil on canvas, 28 3/4 × 36 1/4 in. Philadelphia Museum of Art · Cézanne made the valley and the sky from the same patches of parallel brushstrokes, and color alone tells near from far. · Valley and sky patches are both clusters of short parallel strokes, so land and sky read as one woven surface. · Warm ochres and yellows fill the plain while mountain and sky are blue and violet; that alone sets the distance. · The mountain stands near Aix-en-Provence, Cézanne's hometown, and he painted it again and again in his last years."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-large-bathers.html","Post-Impressionism","The Large Bathers","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-bathers","Paul Cézanne · 1900–06 · Oil on canvas, 82 3/4 × 98 3/4 in. Philadelphia Museum of Art · Cézanne locked the picture into a triangle: tree trunks lean in from both sides and two groups of bathers fit inside. · The bathers are a few broad patches each, faces barely marked, so each group reads as one shape inside the triangle. · The standing bather at the left leans at the angle of the trunks behind her, so the slant continues down to the ground. · The largest and last of Cézanne's bathers pictures, left unfinished; patches of bare canvas still show."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-potato-eaters.html","Post-Impressionism","The Potato Eaters","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-potato-eaters","Vincent van Gogh · 1885 · Dutch, 1853–1890. Oil on canvas, 32 1/4 × 45 in. Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam (Vincent van Gogh Foundation) · Van Gogh painted this dark room, lit by a single lamp, in earth colors, before he took up bright color. · Browns, grays and muddy greens fill the room, so the lamp, the white caps and the potatoes stand out as light spots. · Faces and hands catch the lamplight only on their ridges, so they look bony and seem to push out of the dark. · Painted at Nuenen, in the Dutch countryside, as a showpiece; Van Gogh compared the earth colors to a dusty, unpeeled potato."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-night-cafe.html","Post-Impressionism","The Night Café","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-night-cafe","Vincent van Gogh · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 28 1/2 × 36 1/4 in. Yale University Art Gallery, New Haven · Van Gogh set blood-red walls against a green ceiling and a green billiard table so that the colors clash. · Red wall meets green ceiling along the top of the room; as opposites on the color wheel, each looks stronger there. · Short yellow and orange strokes ring each gas lamp, so its halo reads as glare and adds a third strong color. · Shows the Café de l'Alcazar in Arles, where Van Gogh took his meals; he wrote that the clashing colors express 'the terrible passions of humanity'."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/sunflowers.html","Post-Impressionism","Sunflowers","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-sunflowers","Vincent van Gogh · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 36 1/4 × 28 3/4 in. The National Gallery, London · Van Gogh painted almost the whole picture in one color: yellow flowers, yellow vase, yellow table, yellow wall. · The yellows run from pale lemon in the wall to deep ochre in the seed heads, so light and dark, not color, separate the shapes. · The seed heads stand off the canvas in thick ridges, so rough texture sets them apart from the flatter wall. · Painted to decorate the house at Arles before Gauguin's visit, one of a series; the flowers run from fresh bud to withered head."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-starry-night.html","Post-Impressionism","The Starry Night","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-starry-night","Vincent van Gogh · 1889 · Oil on canvas, 29 × 36 1/4 in. The Museum of Modern Art, New York · Van Gogh made the sky from short visible strokes laid end to end in long curling lines that roll across the canvas. · Two spirals turn in the center of the sky and the strokes inside bend with them, so the paint itself shows the motion. · The village houses and steeple are drawn with straight lines, so against them the sky's curling strokes look even more in motion. · Based on the view from Van Gogh's window at the asylum of Saint-Rémy and painted in daytime sessions; the village was added from other views."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/self-portrait.html","Post-Impressionism","Self-Portrait","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-self-portrait-1889","Vincent van Gogh · 1889 · Oil on canvas, 25 1/2 × 21 1/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Van Gogh covered jacket and background with the same curved strokes, so the man and the wall move together. · The jacket has the same wavy strokes as the wall, so only its dark blue outline keeps the shoulders from merging with the background. · One cool blue-green covers both jacket and wall, so the orange beard, the strongest warm color, pulls the eye to the face. · One of more than forty self-portraits Van Gogh made in about ten years; here he is dressed in a suit instead of the work clothes he usually painted himself in."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/vision-of-the-sermon-jacob-wrestling-with-the-angel.html","Post-Impressionism","Vision of the Sermon (Jacob Wrestling with the Angel)","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-vision","Paul Gauguin · 1888 · French, 1848–1903. Oil on canvas, 28 1/2 × 35 3/4 in. Scottish National Gallery, Edinburgh · Gauguin painted the ground as a flat field of pure red and set every shape on it inside a dark outline. · The unshaded red is equally strong on both sides of the trunk, so it reads as a flat upright sheet, not ground stretching back. · The cow and wrestlers are small, as if far away, but the flat red gives them no distance from the large heads in front. · Painted at Pont-Aven in Brittany. Breton women are praying after a sermon about Jacob wrestling the angel; Gauguin wrote that the fight exists only in their imagination."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-talisman.html","Post-Impressionism","The Talisman","Paul Sérusier","serusier-talisman","Paul Sérusier · 1888 · French, 1864–1927. Oil on wood, 10 3/4 × 8 1/2 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Sérusier reduced the landscape to patches of pure color, with almost no drawing left. · Yellow, blue and violet sit side by side as separate unshaded blocks, so the picture reads as flat pattern before landscape. · Reflections in the lower half are the same flat patches as the trees above, so water and bank are hard to tell apart. · Painted at Pont-Aven in October 1888 under Gauguin's direction; shown in Paris to the painters who formed the Nabis, for whom the panel became a talisman."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/where-do-we-come-from-what-are-we-where-are-we-going.html","Post-Impressionism","Where Do We Come From? What Are We? Where Are We Going?","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-where","Paul Gauguin · 1897–98 · Oil on canvas, 54 3/4 × 147 1/2 in. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · Gauguin gave this long canvas the look of a mural: muted blue-green, read right to left from sleeping baby to old woman. · Deep blue and green fill most of the background, so the ochre and yellow bodies, barely shaded, stand out as flat light shapes. · Flat yellow patches break off the scene at both upper corners, so the canvas looks like an old wall painting with worn corners. · Painted in Tahiti and first shown at Vollard's Paris gallery in 1898."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/at-the-moulin-rouge.html","Post-Impressionism","At the Moulin Rouge","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec","lautrec-moulin-rouge","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec · 1892–95 · French, 1864–1901. Oil on canvas, 48 1/2 × 55 1/2 in. The Art Institute of Chicago · Toulouse-Lautrec cut the scene off at its edges: a railing slices the lower left and the right edge crops a face turned green by electric light. · The orange railing runs diagonally through the foreground, blocking the viewer's way in. · The pale green face at the right edge, beside yellow hair and red lips, is the brightest spot, so it pulls the eye to that cropped edge. · Lautrec's own friends fill the table at the Moulin Rouge, which was built for electric light; he is in the center background beside his cousin."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/divan-japonais.html","Post-Impressionism","Divan Japonais","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec","lautrec-divan","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec · 1892–93 · Lithograph printed in four colors, 31 3/4 × 24 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Toulouse-Lautrec made the seated woman a flat black shape and let the top edge cut the singer off at the neck. · Dress and hat are unshaded black, so the woman reads as a flat cutout and the orange hair between them is the sharpest accent. · Stage, orchestra and the man behind are flat yellow, gray and olive, so nothing looks farther back than anything else. · A color lithograph advertising a Montmartre café-concert; the spectator is Jane Avril and the performer on stage Yvette Guilbert, two stars of the district."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/woman-in-a-striped-dress.html","Post-Impressionism","Woman in a Striped Dress","Édouard Vuillard","vuillard-striped","Édouard Vuillard · 1895 · French, 1868–1940. Oil on canvas, 25 3/4 × 23 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Vuillard painted the dress, the flowers and the wall all as pattern, so the figure reads as part of the room's surface. · The sleeve's red and white stripes break into loose dabs like the flowers beside them, so the sleeve reads as pattern, not as cloth over an arm. · The hair is close to the wall's red-brown, with no hard edge between, so the heads sink into the wall and the eye goes to the bold stripes. · Made in 1895 for Thadée Natanson, who published the magazine La Revue blanche, as one of five decorations for his home; the red-haired woman may be his wife Misia."],[13,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/france-1884-1906/the-sleeping-gypsy.html","Post-Impressionism","The Sleeping Gypsy","Henri Rousseau","rousseau-sleeping-gypsy","Henri Rousseau · 1897 · French, 1844–1910. Oil on canvas, 51 × 79 in. The Museum of Modern Art, New York · Rousseau made every edge hard and every surface smooth, with no visible brushwork and no haze in the distance. · Lion, sleeper and mandolin have clean, unbroken edges against sand and sky, so each stands apart as its own crisp shape. · The far mountains are as sharp-edged as the lion in front, so the distance looks shallow, like a painted backdrop. · Shown in Paris at the 1897 Salon des Indépendants."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism.html","Realism · Overview","What to Look For","","millet-gleaners","Everyday life, painted as it looks · Realism is a nineteenth-century movement that painted subjects from everyday life in a naturalistic way. Before it, Western art was led by the academic ideal of history painting. The French novelist Champfleury coined the name in the 1840s, and the painter Gustave Courbet became the movement's best-known example. · Jean-François Millet · The Gleaners · 1857"],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/forest-of-fontainebleau.html","Realism","Forest of Fontainebleau","Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot","corot-forest-of-fontainebleau","Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot · 1834 · French, 1796–1875. Oil on canvas, 69 1/4 × 95 1/2 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Corot painted a real forest in browns, olive greens and dull golds, with no grand ruin or classical temple. · The trees at the right merge into one dark brown and olive mass, so the foliage looks thick and deep. · Sunlit patches on the sandy bank are the brightest gold in the picture, so the eye goes there first. · Made in the studio from sketches and studies painted outdoors, and shown at the Salon, the official exhibition of the French Academy."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/rue-transnonain-15-april-1834.html","Realism","Rue Transnonain, 15 April 1834","Honoré Daumier","daumier-rue-transnonain","Honoré Daumier · 1834 · French, 1808–1879. Lithograph, image 11 1/4 × 17 1/2 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Daumier drew the dead man foreshortened across the floor, in tones that run from near-white to deep black. · The pale body and bedding stand out against a dark doorway at the left. · One sole faces out and the head lies higher and farther back, so the body looks stretched back across the floor. · A lithograph, printed from a drawing made with a greasy crayon on a flat stone. The print records an event during the riots of that April, when government troops fired on the people living in a building."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/a-burial-at-ornans.html","Realism","A Burial at Ornans","Gustave Courbet","courbet-burial-at-ornans","Gustave Courbet · 1849–50 · French, 1819–1877. Oil on canvas, 124 × 263 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Courbet painted villagers at life size, on a canvas about 22 feet wide, in heavy paint and deep blacks. · The crowd is mostly one band of black clothing, so the white caps and collars stand out as the brightest spots. · Heads run in one long row at nearly the same height, so the eye moves along the crowd without settling on one figure. · The museum calls the canvas a manifesto of a new aesthetic, Realism. The sitters were people of Ornans."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-sower.html","Realism","The Sower","Jean-François Millet","millet-sower","Jean-François Millet · 1850 · French, 1814–1875. Oil on canvas, 40 × 32 1/2 in. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · Millet made the sower one heavy dark mass against a gray sky, with the face lost in shadow under the hat. · The figure fills almost the whole height of the canvas and stands dark against the paler sky, so he looks large, close and heavy. · The back leg stretches to the lower left in a long stride, so the figure looks like it is moving, not standing. · The figure scatters wheat seed from a bag slung over the shoulder."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-horse-fair.html","Realism","The Horse Fair","Rosa Bonheur","bonheur-horse-fair","Rosa Bonheur · 1852–55 · French, 1822–1899. Oil on canvas, 96 1/4 × 199 1/2 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Bonheur set a stream of horses moving from left to right and painted muscles, hooves and manes in close detail. · A black horse and a white horse rear at the center, so the line of horses peaks there and stops the eye. · Dust from the dirt road softens the lower legs and shadows, so the horses look caught in motion. · A horse market on a Paris boulevard. The painting first appeared at the Salon of 1853 and was retouched by the artist in 1855."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-painters-studio.html","Realism","The Painter's Studio","Gustave Courbet","courbet-painters-studio","Gustave Courbet · 1854–55 · Oil on canvas, 142 1/4 × 235 1/2 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Courbet left the upper half of the canvas nearly empty, washed in thin browns and grays above a crowded floor. · The landscape on the easel and the pale model are the lightest shapes on the floor, so the eye goes to the center. · The wall above the heads is thin brown and gray with almost nothing sharp, so the eye drops to the crowded floor. · Made for the 1855 world's fair in Paris and rejected. The painter then showed the canvas in a Pavilion of Realism built at his own expense."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-gleaners.html","Realism","The Gleaners","Jean-François Millet","millet-gleaners","Jean-François Millet · 1857 · Oil on canvas, 32 3/4 × 43 1/4 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Millet set three stooping figures in a low run of curves across the foreground, solid as sculpture in low, late light. · Light catches the top of each back and arm and leaves the underside dark, so the bodies look round and solid. · The haystacks and cart far behind are pale and small, so the field looks deep and the darker figures stay up front. · Gleaners gathered the ears of grain left after the harvest. The painting was shown at the Salon."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-angelus.html","Realism","The Angelus","Jean-François Millet","millet-angelus","Jean-François Millet · 1857–59 · Oil on canvas, 21 3/4 × 26 in. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Millet set two still figures dark against a glowing sky, with the horizon crossing both at chest height. · Only small spots catch the light, like the man's white shirt front, so the rest of each figure reads as a dark shape. · A pitchfork at the left and a wheelbarrow at the right sit low and dark, so the eye stays on the figures between. · A man and a woman stop the potato harvest to recite the angelus, a prayer said when a church bell rings."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-old-musician.html","Realism","The Old Musician","Édouard Manet","manet-old-musician","Édouard Manet · 1862 · French, 1832–1883. Oil on canvas, 73 3/4 × 97 3/4 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Manet painted the figures as broad, flat shapes with little modeling. · The ground and sky are brushed in broad strokes with little detail, so the space behind the figures looks shallow, like a backdrop. · The standing man's brown cloak and the boy's black sleeves are near-solid patches of one color with few shadows, so they look flat, not rounded. · The museum says the canvas shows homeless and marginalized people, on a scale normally kept for the wealthy and powerful."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-third-class-carriage.html","Realism","The Third-Class Carriage","Honoré Daumier","daumier-third-class-carriage","Honoré Daumier · 1864 · Oil on canvas, 25 3/4 × 35 1/2 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Daumier shaped the figures with dark outlines drawn in paint and left the canvas unfinished. · A faint grid of ruled lines shows through the thin paint, so the picture looks like work still in progress. · Dark painted lines ring the faces and hands of the two lit women, so their shapes stay clear against the brown shadow. · The grid was for copying the design at another size. The museum notes a finished version in Ottawa."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-veteran-in-a-new-field.html","Realism","The Veteran in a New Field","Winslow Homer","homer-veteran-new-field","Winslow Homer · 1865 · American, 1836–1910. Oil on canvas, 24 1/4 × 38 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Homer reduced the picture to three bands: blue sky, a wall of golden wheat and cut stalks in front. · Cut stalks are quick yellow and white strokes that fan out, so the front band reads as a separate layer from the upright wheat. · The wheat rises to one nearly straight edge against the sky, with no distance behind, so it reads as a flat wall. · Completed after the end of the Civil War. The discarded Union jacket and canteen show that the farmer is a returned soldier."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-wave.html","Realism","The Wave","Gustave Courbet","courbet-the-wave","Gustave Courbet · 1869–70 · Oil on canvas, 25 3/4 × 36 1/2 in. Städel Museum, Frankfurt am Main · Courbet laid the paint on thick, pushing and scraping it, so the wave looks heavy and solid. · Foam is laid on in thick white patches that stand off the canvas, so the water looks heavy. · Foreground rocks are red-brown blocks painted as thickly as the water, so the wave looks as solid as the stone. · One of about sixty pictures of storm waves on the Normandy coast, made between 1865 and 1870."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/barge-haulers-on-the-volga.html","Realism","Barge Haulers on the Volga","Ilya Repin","repin-barge-haulers","Ilya Repin · 1870–73 · 1844–1930. Oil on canvas, 51 3/4 × 110 3/4 in. State Russian Museum, St Petersburg · Repin joined eleven men in harness into one long chain from the left toward the ship at the right. · Dark harness straps cross the men's chests, and the repeated bands tie them into one line. · The sand and sky are bright and flat, so the dark group stands out as one shape. · The figures are based on portraits of real haulers, drawn on two trips to the Volga."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-champion-single-sculls-max-schmitt-in-a-single-scull.html","Realism","The Champion Single Sculls (Max Schmitt in a Single Scull)","Thomas Eakins","eakins-champion-single-sculls","Thomas Eakins · 1871 · American, 1844–1916. Oil on canvas, 32 1/4 × 46 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Eakins painted clear, even daylight that shows every detail at every distance. · The bridges far behind are drawn as sharply as the boat in front, so the distance looks clear, not hazy. · The river mirrors the sky and trees smoothly, so the water looks still and each reflection keeps a clear edge. · Believed to mark a rowing win by Max Schmitt in 1870. The painter shows himself as the rower in the middle distance."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-iron-rolling-mill.html","Realism","The Iron Rolling Mill","Adolph Menzel","menzel-iron-rolling-mill","Adolph Menzel · 1872–75 · German, 1815–1905. Oil on canvas, 62 1/4 × 100 in. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · Menzel filled the factory with deep shadow and broke it with a white-hot glow at the center. · Orange light from the hot iron falls on the nearest faces and arms, so the glow pulls the eye to the center. · Sparks are quick yellow and white dabs against near-black machinery, so the hot iron looks brighter than any other spot. · Industrial labor, painted at a size once kept for history painting. The painter drew at a works in Upper Silesia in 1872."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-gross-clinic.html","Realism","The Gross Clinic","Thomas Eakins","eakins-gross-clinic","Thomas Eakins · 1875 · Oil on canvas, 96 × 78 1/2 in. Philadelphia Museum of Art and Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts · Eakins set bright red on the surgeon's fingers and scalpel against browns and blacks. · One strong light from above hits the surgeon's forehead and hand, so the eye runs from his forehead down to the red hand. · The tiers of students behind are dark brown and gray, almost lost in shadow, so the small red patches look brighter. · Made for the 1876 Centennial Exhibition; the art jury rejected it. Formally titled Portrait of Dr. Samuel D. Gross."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-song-of-the-lark.html","Realism","The Song of the Lark","Jules Breton","breton-song-of-the-lark","Jules Breton · 1884 · French, 1827–1906. Oil on canvas, 43 1/2 × 33 3/4 in. Art Institute of Chicago · Breton filled most of the height with one upright figure, her head above the horizon. · The horizon crosses her at the chest, so her head and shoulders stand against open sky. · The sky holds no hard shape near her head, so her darker head and scarf keep a clear outline. · A peasant girl stops work to hear a lark, painted in an idealized style."],[14,"/study/art-history/realism/europe-and-the-united-states-1834-1899/the-gulf-stream.html","Realism","The Gulf Stream","Winslow Homer","homer-gulf-stream","Winslow Homer · 1899 · Oil on canvas, 28 1/4 × 49 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Homer built a rough sea from short, broken strokes of teal, navy and white around a small boat tilted on a diagonal. · Whitecaps are short, curved strokes of white and pale green over the dark blue, so the surface looks choppy and moving. · The boat's deck is painted in smooth, warm browns, so it stands out from the broken blues around it. · A Black man faces danger alone on a damaged boat. The painter reworked the canvas by 1906, the year the museum bought it."],[15,"/study/art-history/rococo.html","Rococo · Overview","What to Look For","","fragonard-swing","Rococo is the light, curving, pastel style of Paris, Venice and Bavaria in the decades after 1715. · Jean-Honoré Fragonard · The Swing · c. 1767–68"],[15,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/pilgrimage-to-the-island-of-cythera.html","Rococo","Pilgrimage to the Island of Cythera","Antoine Watteau","watteau-cythera","Antoine Watteau · 1717 · French, 1684–1721. Oil on canvas, 50 3/4 × 76 1/2 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Watteau let a hillside of couples dissolve into a hazy pink and blue distance. · The far shore and mountains are blue-gray haze with no hard edge, so they look miles behind the sharper, warmer couples. · A line of couples runs from the statue at right down to the boat at left, so the eye drifts across the hill. · Delivered to the Paris Academy in 1717 as Watteau's reception piece and registered as a fête galante, an elegant party out of doors."],[15,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/pierrot.html","Rococo","Pierrot","Antoine Watteau","watteau-pierrot","Antoine Watteau · c. 1718–19 · Oil on canvas, 72 1/2 × 61 1/4 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Watteau painted a whole satin costume white on white, in thin, shimmering layers. · The costume is warm gray in the folds and bright cream on the ridges, so the white reads as satin. · The other actors are pushed down into a strip of heads at knee height, so the white figure stands alone. · A stock character from the Italian comedy."],[15,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/mezzetin.html","Rococo","Mezzetin","Antoine Watteau","watteau-mezzetin","Antoine Watteau · c. 1718–20 · Oil on canvas, 21 3/4 × 17 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Watteau built this small canvas on one serpentine curve and laid the costume in with separate quick strokes. · The body twists in one long S, crossed legs to tipped-back head, so it looks in motion against the straight bench. · The pink, white and pale blue stripes are single small brushstrokes, so the silk flickers instead of lying flat. · A comic character from the Italian stage, singing a serenade; the statue in the garden turns her back on him."],[15,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/gersaints-shopsign.html","Rococo","Gersaint's Shopsign","Antoine Watteau","watteau-gersaint","Antoine Watteau · 1720 · Oil on canvas, 64 1/4 × 120 1/2 in. Schloss Charlottenburg, Berlin · Watteau set the shop out as a shallow stage and kept the whole wide canvas in silver, rose and straw color. · The back wall and stone threshold run flat across the canvas, so the room reads as a shallow box open in front. · The figures are pale silver, pink and gray against dim walls of paintings, so the silk dresses are the brightest shapes. · Painted in autumn 1720 as the sign for a Paris picture dealer's shop; at the left a portrait of Louis XIV is lowered into a crate."],[15,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/the-triumph-of-venus.html","Rococo","The Triumph of Venus","François Boucher","boucher-triumph-venus","François Boucher · 1740 · French, 1703–1770. Oil on canvas, 51 1/4 × 63 3/4 in. Nationalmuseum, Stockholm · Boucher turned the composition into one great spiral of pink flesh, striped silk and blue-green sea. · A striped cloth loops overhead and a chain of bodies curves around below, so the eye keeps circling. · Warm pink skin and striped cloth sit against cool blue sky and sea, so the pink shapes join into one curving band. · Venus carried over the waves by sea nymphs and tritons; shown at the Paris Salon of 1740."],[15,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/le-dejeuner.html","Rococo","Le Déjeuner","François Boucher","boucher-dejeuner","François Boucher · 1739 · Oil on canvas, 32 × 25 3/4 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Boucher filled the room with C- and S-curves, from the clock case to the gilt console at the far left. · The window bars and the mirror's long sides are straight lines, so the gilt curls of the clock and candle brackets look more twisted. · The gilt mirror frame and brackets sit on flat gray-blue panels, so the gold stands out as the wall's busiest part. · A breakfast scene. A Chinese figure sits on the shelf behind the man: the taste for chinoiserie was part of the style."],[15,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/madame-de-pompadour.html","Rococo","Madame de Pompadour","François Boucher","boucher-pompadour","François Boucher · 1756 · Oil on canvas, 80 3/4 × 63 1/2 in. Alte Pinakothek, Munich · Boucher laid pattern on pattern: roses on sea-green satin, against a gold curtain and a carved gilt mirror. · Pink roses, bows and lace run along the neckline, sleeves and every flounce, so the trim breaks up the green satin. · The silk is long smooth strokes and the roses and lace small raised touches, so the satin looks slick under the trim. · Louis XV's official mistress, shown with the books, letters, music and drawings of a cultivated life."],[15,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/the-swing.html","Rococo","The Swing","Jean-Honoré Fragonard","fragonard-swing","Jean-Honoré Fragonard · c. 1767–68 · French, 1732–1806. Oil on canvas, 32 × 25 1/4 in. The Wallace Collection, London · Fragonard sent one shaft of sunlight onto a pink dress and let the garden sink into blue-green shadow. · The feathery foliage turns pale toward the upper left, so the light reads as one shaft from that side. · The dress is crisp white and pink strokes against soft dark trees, so it looks sharp and bright while the garden falls back. · A young woman on a swing, a man hidden in the bushes below, and a stone cupid with a finger to his lips."],[15,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/the-progress-of-love-the-meeting.html","Rococo","The Progress of Love: The Meeting","Jean-Honoré Fragonard","fragonard-meeting","Jean-Honoré Fragonard · 1771–72 · Oil on canvas, 125 × 96 in. The Frick Collection, New York · Fragonard gave most of this tall wall panel to the garden and kept the figures small at the bottom. · The two figures fill only the bottom third, under a statue and massed trees, so the garden looks far bigger than them. · The trees are short green touches lit along the edges, against a smooth sky, so they read as a solid dome. · One of a set of garden scenes painted for Madame du Barry's pavilion outside Paris, then returned to the painter."],[15,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/young-girl-reading.html","Rococo","Young Girl Reading","Jean-Honoré Fragonard","fragonard-girl-reading","Jean-Honoré Fragonard · c. 1769 · Oil on canvas, 32 × 25 1/2 in. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Fragonard painted this picture fast, in lemon yellow, and left the speed showing. · Long loaded strokes run unblended down the sleeve and over the cushion, so every brush pass stays visible and looks quick. · The white ruff's folds are loops scratched into wet paint with the brush handle, so the darker layer underneath reads as shadow. · One of the fantasy figures, a set of quickly painted half-length figures in fancy dress."],[15,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/allegory-of-the-planets-and-continents.html","Rococo","Allegory of the Planets and Continents","Giovanni Battista Tiepolo","tiepolo-planets","Giovanni Battista Tiepolo · 1752 · Italian, 1696–1770. Oil on canvas, 73 × 54 7/8 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Tiepolo painted a ceiling in miniature: figures seen from below crowd the four edges of a wide pale sky. · The figures are shortened as if seen from the floor, legs and undersides showing, so the sky seems to open straight overhead. · The sky figures are a few pale strokes and the edge figures darker, so the center looks high and far away. · The sketch presented in 1752 as the proposal for the staircase ceiling of the Würzburg palace."],[15,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/gustavus-hamilton-second-viscount-boyne-in-masquerade-costume.html","Rococo","Gustavus Hamilton, Second Viscount Boyne, in Masquerade Costume","Rosalba Carriera","carriera-boyne","Rosalba Carriera · 1730–31 · Italian, 1673–1757. Pastel on paper, laid down on canvas, 22 1/4 × 16 7/8 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Carriera used pastel to give the whole surface a soft, powdery skin. · The face is rubbed smooth with no visible stroke, so the skin looks even and powdery. · The fur collar is smudged with no hard outline, so it melts into the blue cloak and reads as soft and deep. · An Irish visitor in Venetian Carnival dress: mask, veil, tricorne and short cape."],[15,"/study/art-history/rococo/france-venice-and-bavaria-1717-1772/pilgrimage-church-of-wies-interior.html","Rococo","Pilgrimage Church of Wies, interior","Dominikus and Johann Baptist Zimmermann","zimmermann-wieskirche","Dominikus and Johann Baptist Zimmermann · 1745–54 · German; Dominikus 1685–1766, Johann Baptist 1680–1758. Stucco, fresco and gilding. Steingaden, Bavaria · The Zimmermann brothers built the same style at full scale, in white and gold stucco curling over walls and piers. · Gold ornament is bunched in scrolls along the arches and pier tops, so the curls show clearly against the white walls. · The pulpit's gilt stucco breaks its outline into curls, so it reads as one ragged cluster on the white pier. · A pilgrimage church in the Bavarian countryside, designed by one brother and decorated by the other."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism.html","Romanticism · Overview","What to Look For","","friedrich-wanderer","Feeling, imagination and wild nature · Romanticism is the name given to a shift in art and literature that was at its height from about 1780 to 1830 and was felt long after. Artists in Britain, Spain, Germany, France and the United States turned away from classical restraint and put personal feeling, the workings of the mind and the natural world at the center of their pictures. · Caspar David Friedrich · Wanderer above the Sea of Fog · about 1817"],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-nightmare.html","Romanticism","The Nightmare","Henry Fuseli","fuseli-nightmare","Henry Fuseli · 1781 · Swiss, 1741–1825. Oil on canvas, 40 × 50 in. Detroit Institute of Arts · Fuseli stretched a limp white body across a dark room and left the creature and the horse half lost in shadow. · The strongest light hits the white gown and arms, so the pale body stands out and the creature and horse sink into the dark. · The red curtain fades to near-black at both sides, so the room looks shallow and the creature blends into the dark. · First shown at the Royal Academy in London in 1782."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-ancient-of-days.html","Romanticism","The Ancient of Days","William Blake","blake-ancient-of-days","William Blake · 1794 · British, 1757–1827. Color-printed relief etching with oil, watercolor and ink on paper, plate 9 1/4 × 6 1/2 in. Yale Center for British Art, New Haven · Blake drew the figure in firm outline and set it inside a glowing disk. · Dark outlines mark each arm and leg muscle, so the body reads as a hard, clear shape, not soft shading. · Black surrounds the orange and gold disk and fine rays spread from its edge, so it reads as a light glowing behind the figure. · The frontispiece, or opening picture, of the artist's own book Europe a Prophecy."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-sleep-of-reason-produces-monsters.html","Romanticism","The Sleep of Reason Produces Monsters","Francisco de Goya","goya-sleep-of-reason","Francisco de Goya · 1799 · Spanish, 1746–1828. Etching and aquatint, plate 8 1/4 × 6 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Goya built the whole picture from black line and flat gray tone, with no color. · The even, grainy gray background has no pen marks, so it reads as flat tone and the black lines stand out. · Scratched lines build the owls and bats and pack closest in the big bat at the top, so line alone makes the darkest shape. · An etching with aquatint, a method that prints areas of tone. Plate 43 of Los Caprichos, a series of 80 plates."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-monk-by-the-sea.html","Romanticism","The Monk by the Sea","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-monk-by-the-sea","Caspar David Friedrich · 1808–10 · German, 1774–1840. Oil on canvas, 43 1/4 × 67 1/2 in. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · Friedrich cut the picture down to three horizontal bands: pale sand, dark sea and a huge sky. · The sky fills about three quarters of the height, so sand and sea look thin and the figure looks tiny. · Nothing closes the sides and the bands run off both edges, so the view seems to run past the frame. · The artist worked on the canvas for about two years, and repeated repainting left the scene unusually empty."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-third-of-may-1808.html","Romanticism","The Third of May 1808","Francisco de Goya","goya-third-of-may","Francisco de Goya · 1814 · Oil on canvas, 105 1/2 × 136 1/2 in. Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid · Goya lit the scene from one lantern on the ground and turned its light on a man in a white shirt. · A row of dark backs with no faces makes the soldiers read as one block, so the eye goes to the white shirt. · The lantern is a white and yellow cube under a flat black sky, so the shirt facing it is the brightest patch. · The Prado's full title adds “in Madrid, or The Executions.”"],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/wanderer-above-the-sea-of-fog.html","Romanticism","Wanderer above the Sea of Fog","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-wanderer","Caspar David Friedrich · about 1817 · Oil on canvas, 37 1/4 × 29 1/2 in. Hamburger Kunsthalle, Hamburg · Friedrich stood a single dark figure dead center, seen from behind, and made the lines of the landscape meet on it. · Figure and rock form one dark triangle rising from the bottom edge, standing out sharply against the pale fog. · Distant ridges slope down from both sides and meet behind the figure's chest, so the eye slides along them to the center. · A figure seen from behind is called a Rückenfigur. The picture shows a person looking, not only the view. One of the artist's few upright canvases."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-raft-of-the-medusa.html","Romanticism","The Raft of the Medusa","Théodore Géricault","gericault-raft-medusa","Théodore Géricault · 1818–19 · French, 1791–1824. Oil on canvas, 193 1/4 × 282 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Géricault piled the bodies into a pyramid climbing from lower left to a man waving a cloth at upper right. · A second diagonal runs up the mast and ropes to the left, giving the picture two peaks the eye moves between. · Raised arms all reach toward the upper right, like arrows pulling the eye up the pyramid to the waving cloth. · The picture records the 1816 wreck of a frigate off Senegal. About 150 people went onto a raft, and 15 were alive when a ship found them 12 days later."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/saturn-devouring-his-son.html","Romanticism","Saturn Devouring His Son","Francisco de Goya","goya-saturn","Francisco de Goya · 1820–23 · Mixed technique, mural transferred to canvas, 56 1/2 × 32 in. Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid · Goya gave the body angular, out-of-proportion limbs and brushed it roughly in a few earth colors against solid black. · Broad strokes show in the hair and thigh, so the body reads as rough patches with edges fading into black. · Red is kept to the small body and the gripping fingers, so among browns and black the eye finds it first. · Painted straight onto a wall of the artist's house and later moved to canvas, with much paint lost in the transfer."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-hay-wain.html","Romanticism","The Hay Wain","John Constable","constable-hay-wain","John Constable · 1821 · British, 1776–1837. Oil on canvas, 51 1/4 × 73 in. The National Gallery, London · Constable flecked the water and the leaves with small dabs of white paint and built the foliage from many separate strokes. · The big trees' greens run from near-black to lit yellow-green, so the foliage reads as broken, leafy masses, not flat green. · The white dabs sit on top of darker paint, so they read as light catching the water and the leaves. · The millpond is at Flatford on the River Stour; the work was done in the artist's London studio."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-death-of-sardanapalus.html","Romanticism","The Death of Sardanapalus","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-death-sardanapalus","Eugène Delacroix · 1827 · French, 1798–1863. Oil on canvas, 154 1/4 × 195 1/4 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Delacroix ran a great red bed on a diagonal across the canvas and gave no single figure the center. · Bodies spill around the bed, and the eye circles from group to group. · The red bed is the largest bright patch against the brown-black background, so its diagonal is the shape that reads first. · The picture did not sell, and the artist held on to it for about 18 years."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/liberty-leading-the-people.html","Romanticism","Liberty Leading the People","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-liberty-leading","Eugène Delacroix · 1830 · Oil on canvas, 102 1/4 × 128 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Delacroix echoed the flag's red, white and blue in small notes across a mostly gray and brown picture. · Jacket and sash on the figure kneeling at Liberty's feet repeat the flag's blue and red, so the eye links them to the flag. · The tiny blue sock on the fallen man in the white shirt shows up at once against gray and brown stone. · Painted from September to December and also called A Barricade."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/women-of-algiers-in-their-apartment.html","Romanticism","Women of Algiers in Their Apartment","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-women-algiers","Eugène Delacroix · 1834 · Oil on canvas, 70 3/4 × 90 1/4 in. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Delacroix filled nearly every surface with color and pattern, shadows included: blue on the tiled wall, deep red in the curtain. · Orange, red and blue touches sit side by side in the fabrics, so each looks stronger and the cloth reads as pattern. · Light from the left falls on the reclining woman's cheek and blouse, so they stand out pale against the wall's blue shadow. · Bought by the state at the Salon, the official French art exhibition, in the year the canvas was painted."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/view-from-mount-holyoke-northampton-massachusetts-after-a-thunderstorm-the-oxbow.html","Romanticism","View from Mount Holyoke, Northampton, Massachusetts, after a Thunderstorm—The Oxbow","Thomas Cole","cole-oxbow","Thomas Cole · 1836 · American, 1801–1848. Oil on canvas, 51 1/2 × 76 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Cole split the canvas between dark storm and forest on the left and sunlit farmland on the right. · Dark gray storm cloud meets a pale yellow and blue clearing sky, so the sky splits dark and light like the land. · Small neat yellow and green fields sit beside massed dark-green forest, so the right looks bright and open, the left dense. · The river is the Connecticut. The artist painted himself at an easel among the rocks at the bottom."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-fighting-temeraire.html","Romanticism","The Fighting Temeraire","J.M.W. Turner","turner-fighting-temeraire","J.M.W. Turner · 1839 · English, 1775–1851. Oil on canvas, 35 3/4 × 47 3/4 in. The National Gallery, London · Turner set cool against warm: a pale ship against blue at the left, an orange and red sunset at the right. · Thin, pale and barely darker than the sky, the tall ship looks faint and far off beside the dark tug. · The sunset's broad orange streaks sit opposite blue on the color wheel, so each side makes the other look stronger. · A warship that fought at Trafalgar in 1805 is shown under tow after being sold to a shipbreaker. The artist probably did not witness the tow and relied on reports."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/slave-ship-slavers-throwing-overboard-the-dead-and-dying-typhoon-coming-on.html","Romanticism","Slave Ship (Slavers Throwing Overboard the Dead and Dying, Typhoon Coming On)","J.M.W. Turner","turner-slave-ship","J.M.W. Turner · 1840 · Oil on canvas, 35 3/4 × 48 1/4 in. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · Turner dragged and layered the paint across the whole surface and let color do the work of drawing. · Sea and sky share dragged strokes of red and orange, so the horizon is hard to find and they read as one. · The ship is a few thin dark mast lines half hidden by spray, hard to tell from the red sky. · The title names the figures in the sea: people thrown overboard."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/rain-steam-and-speed-the-great-western-railway.html","Romanticism","Rain, Steam and Speed – The Great Western Railway","J.M.W. Turner","turner-rain-steam-speed","J.M.W. Turner · 1844 · Oil on canvas, 35 3/4 × 48 in. The National Gallery, London · Turner blurred almost everything into pale haze and kept his sharpest edge for the engine. · The engine, a black funnel above a red-orange glow, is the darkest, sharpest shape in the haze, so the eye goes there first. · Creams, golds and pale blues cover sky, rain and land with no clear horizon, so the distance is one sheet of haze. · A train crosses the Maidenhead railway bridge over the Thames; the bridge was finished in 1838."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/heart-of-the-andes.html","Romanticism","Heart of the Andes","Frederic Edwin Church","church-heart-of-the-andes","Frederic Edwin Church · 1859 · American, 1826–1900. Oil on canvas, 66 1/4 × 120 1/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Church painted a vast view in sharp, tiny detail from the nearest leaf to the farthest peak. · Leaves and ferns are drawn one by one in the right foreground, so the near bank looks close enough to touch. · Space steps back in clear layers: dark foreground, sunlit plain, brown mountains, a white snow peak. · A composite view built from sketches made in Ecuador, first shown in a darkened room under gaslight."],[16,"/study/art-history/romanticism/europe-and-the-united-states-1781-1863/the-rocky-mountains-landers-peak.html","Romanticism","The Rocky Mountains, Lander's Peak","Albert Bierstadt","bierstadt-rocky-mountains","Albert Bierstadt · 1863 · American, 1830–1902. Oil on canvas, 73 1/2 × 120 3/4 in. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Bierstadt painted the far mountains pale and hazy, almost white, and the near ground dark green and sharply drawn. · Color cools and pales with distance, from green to gold to blue-white, so the peaks look miles behind the meadow. · Sunlight in the middle distance lights a waterfall and cliff, so that warm gold band separates the dark near ground from the pale peaks. · The mountains are the Wind River Range, in what is now Wyoming. The picture was made in New York after an 1859 survey trip, and the camp in the foreground is Shoshone."],[17,"/study/value-and-light.html","Value and Light","Value and Light","","cross-fig21","Leonardo da Vinci saw light and shade as what lets a flat picture look solid. In the 1800s, drawing manuals by Mary Merrifield and Anson Cross taught the same ideas with plain white models."],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/the-value-scale.html","Value and Light","The Value Scale","","merrifield-pl02-scale","Value is how light or dark something is, whatever its color. A value scale lays it out in steps from white to black, so any tone can be matched to a step. · Ten bands step from paper white at the left to solid black at the right; every tone in a picture falls between them. · Each flat band looks darker along the edge beside a lighter one: the eye judges a value against its neighbor. · Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · Odalisque in Grisaille · about 1824–34 · Ingres painted this unfinished, smaller repeat of his 1814 Grande Odalisque almost entirely in grays, so value does nearly all the work of building the body. · Merrifield noticed that most of a lit object sits in the middle values, with only small patches at either extreme."],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/the-value-scale.html","Value and Light · Example","Odalisque in Grisaille","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","thumb/ingres-odalisque-grisaille","about 1824–34 · Ingres painted this unfinished, smaller repeat of his 1814 Grande Odalisque almost entirely in grays, so value does nearly all the work of building the body. · The Value Scale"],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/local-value-and-light.html","Value and Light","Local Value and Light","","cross-fig07","Every surface has its own lightness, its local value; the light falling on it then adds lights and shadows on top. · Under one light, both balls turn from lit side to shadow side in the same places: that pattern comes from the light, not the surface. · On the darker ball the whole pattern shifts down into a narrower range, so its shine stands out more. · Francisco de Zurbarán · Still Life with Lemons, Oranges and a Rose · 1633 · Light from the left strikes pale lemons, darker oranges and a white cup, and each keeps its own lightness whether lit or shaded. · Cross noted that the lit side of a dark object can look lighter than the shaded side of a light one."],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/local-value-and-light.html","Value and Light · Example","Still Life with Lemons, Oranges and a Rose","Francisco de Zurbarán","thumb/zurbaran-still-life-lemons","1633 · Light from the left strikes pale lemons, darker oranges and a white cup, and each keeps its own lightness whether lit or shaded. · Local Value and Light"],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/high-and-low-key.html","Value and Light","High and Low Key","","morisot-summers-day","Key is where a picture's values mostly fall. A high-key picture, mostly lights, looks flooded with light; a low-key one, mostly darks, makes its few lights stand out strongly. · Almost all the values sit at the light end, so the whole scene reads as bright air over water. · The biggest dark, the left-hand woman's deep blue coat, covers little of the canvas, so the picture stays high key. · Rembrandt van Rijn · Philosopher in Meditation · 1632 · Rembrandt gave most of the panel to darkness, so the window daylight reaching the old man and one small fire at the right glow against it. · Key and contrast are separate: a bright picture may still hold some deep darks, and a dark one some brilliant lights."],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/high-and-low-key.html","Value and Light · Example","Philosopher in Meditation","Rembrandt van Rijn","thumb/rembrandt-philosopher-in-meditation","1632 · Rembrandt gave most of the panel to darkness, so the window daylight reaching the old man and one small fire at the right glow against it. · High and Low Key"],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/the-light-and-shadow-sequence.html","Value and Light","The Light and Shadow Sequence","","merrifield-pl09","A rounded form turns away from a single light bit by bit, so its values run in a set order: highlight, light side, halftone (a middle tone), darkest shadow band, reflected light bounced into the shadow, then the shadow on the ground. · The lower-right sphere, lit from the upper left, goes from a small shine to darker tones as its surface turns away. · In the upper-right diagram a line from the highlight through the center crosses the middle of the darkest band, so the highlight's position tells where the shadow falls. · Juan Sánchez Cotán · Quince, Cabbage, Melon and Cucumber · about 1602 · Sánchez Cotán let the lit fronts of the quince and cabbage give way to deep shadow on the sides away from the light, so each looks round against the dark opening."],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/the-light-and-shadow-sequence.html","Value and Light · Example","Quince, Cabbage, Melon and Cucumber","Juan Sánchez Cotán","thumb/sanchez-cotan-quince-cabbage","about 1602 · Sánchez Cotán let the lit fronts of the quince and cabbage give way to deep shadow on the sides away from the light, so each looks round against the dark opening. · The Light and Shadow Sequence"],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/highlights.html","Value and Light","Highlights","","cross-fig16","On a polished surface, a highlight is a small reflected image of the light source, so it takes the shape of the source and shifts when the viewer moves. · The dark bottle shows two small highlights, one for each half of the divided window it faced. · The block's pale top shows in the bottle's glaze as a curved shape: a polished surface mirrors any bright thing nearby, and a highlight is the brightest such reflection. · Willem Claesz Heda · Still Life with Oysters, a Silver Tazza, and Glassware · 1635 · Heda put tiny, crisp highlights on the tall glass and the tipped silver cup, so they read as polished, and gave the wet oysters a broader, softer shine."],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/highlights.html","Value and Light · Example","Still Life with Oysters, a Silver Tazza, and Glassware","Willem Claesz Heda","thumb/heda-still-life-oysters","1635 · Heda put tiny, crisp highlights on the tall glass and the tipped silver cup, so they read as polished, and gave the wet oysters a broader, softer shine. · Highlights"],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/cast-shadows.html","Value and Light","Cast Shadows","","merrifield-pl03","When an object stands in the light's path, it throws its shape onto nearby surfaces as a cast shadow. · Each cube throws a band of shadow across the table on the side away from the light. · Each cast shadow is darker than its cube's shaded side: that side still catches some light bounced off the table, and the shadowed table gets less. · Vilhelm Hammershøi · Dust Motes Dancing in the Sunbeams · 1901 · The window bars block parts of the sunbeams, so the patches of sun on the floor carry the window's grid of shadow. · Cast shadows are usually darkest and crispest next to the object, and their direction and length reveal where the light sits."],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/cast-shadows.html","Value and Light · Example","Dust Motes Dancing in the Sunbeams","Vilhelm Hammershøi","thumb/hammershoi-dust-motes","1901 · The window bars block parts of the sunbeams, so the patches of sun on the floor carry the window's grid of shadow. · Cast Shadows"],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/direction-of-light.html","Value and Light","Direction of Light","","cross-figs-08-10-11","Light reaches only the surfaces turned toward it, so its direction sets how a subject divides into lit and shaded areas, and with that how solid it looks. · Top: side light splits each white form into a lit half and a shadow half, so the shapes read clearly. · Middle: light from behind the viewer reaches almost every face, and the forms flatten out. · Bottom: light from behind the group leaves most surfaces in shadow, with bright tops and outlines. · Pierre-Paul Prud'hon · Study of Drapery · about 1813 · Light from one side catches the cloth's ridges in white chalk, the deepest folds go to black and the blue paper is the middle value, so each fold reads as rounded."],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/direction-of-light.html","Value and Light · Example","Study of Drapery","Pierre-Paul Prud'hon","thumb/prudhon-blue-paper","about 1813 · Light from one side catches the cloth's ridges in white chalk, the deepest folds go to black and the blue paper is the middle value, so each fold reads as rounded. · Direction of Light"],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/hard-and-soft-light.html","Value and Light","Hard and Soft Light","","cross-figs-09-12","Hard light makes sharp-edged shadows; soft light makes blurred ones. The smaller a light looks from the subject, the harder it is: the distant sun is hard, a cloudy sky or a big nearby window soft. · Top: the far-off sun acts as a small source, so each spot is either fully lit or fully blocked, and the dark shadows have crisp edges. · Bottom: weak light arriving from several sides wraps around the forms, and the faint shadows fade at their edges. · Joaquín Sorolla · Sewing the Sail · 1896 · Sunshine broken up by the leaves spatters the white sail with crisp spots of light and shade. · Outdoors, Cross observed, light bouncing in from every side makes shadows lighter than they are indoors."],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/hard-and-soft-light.html","Value and Light · Example","Sewing the Sail","Joaquín Sorolla","thumb/sorolla-sewing-the-sail","1896 · Sunshine broken up by the leaves spatters the white sail with crisp spots of light and shade. · Hard and Soft Light"],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/candlelight.html","Value and Light","Candlelight","","wright-air-pump-detail","A candle or lamp within the scene lights nearby things strongly, and its light drops off fast with distance. · The faces nearest the glass bowl are brightest; those set farther back sink toward shadow. · The flame itself is hidden behind the glass bowl at the bottom, yet where it sits is clear from what it lights. · Georges de La Tour · The Magdalen with the Smoking Flame · about 1635–37 · Her face, hand and sleeve, closest to the flame, are brightest; the skull in her lap is dimmer and her legs fade into shadow. · If the flame itself is shown, it turns into part of the subject and gives the whole picture one bright focus."],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/candlelight.html","Value and Light · Example","The Magdalen with the Smoking Flame","Georges de La Tour","thumb/latour-magdalen-smoking-flame","about 1635–37 · Her face, hand and sleeve, closest to the flame, are brightest; the skull in her lap is dimmer and her legs fade into shadow. · Candlelight"],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/edges.html","Value and Light","Edges","","cross-fig13","An edge is where one value meets another: hard when the change is sharp, soft when it blends, lost when the values match. Hard edges catch the eye; soft and lost ones let a form sink into its surroundings. · At the right, the cylinder's shaded end nearly matches the dark shadow beside it, so the outline between them disappears: a lost edge. · Where the cone's lit side meets the darker backdrop, the value jumps suddenly, so the edge is hard. · Diego Velázquez · Juan de Pareja · 1650 · Velázquez kept the white collar crisp and let the beard melt into the hair and the dark coat into the dark background, so the collar and lit face stand forward."],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/edges.html","Value and Light · Example","Juan de Pareja","Diego Velázquez","thumb/velazquez-juan-de-pareja","1650 · Velázquez kept the white collar crisp and let the beard melt into the hair and the dark coat into the dark background, so the collar and lit face stand forward. · Edges"],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/chiaroscuro.html","Value and Light","Chiaroscuro","","rembrandt-aristotle-detail","Chiaroscuro (Italian for \"light-dark\") is the balance and pattern of light and shade across a whole picture, arranged as one design. The shading makes forms look solid, and lights set against darks make each other stand out. · The few things that catch the light, the face, pale sleeve, hand and bust, link into one path of light across the brown gloom. · The hat brim shadows the upper face, so the strongest light falls on the cheek and nose and the eyes stay partly veiled. · Leonardo da Vinci · The Virgin of the Rocks · about 1491/2–9 and 1506–8 · The figures come out of the dark grotto by soft degrees, with no hard outline at the Virgin's temples or nose, an effect later called sfumato, from the Italian for \"to turn to smoke\"."],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/chiaroscuro.html","Value and Light · Example","The Virgin of the Rocks","Leonardo da Vinci","thumb/leonardo-virgin-of-the-rocks","about 1491/2–9 and 1506–8 · The figures come out of the dark grotto by soft degrees, with no hard outline at the Virgin's temples or nose, an effect later called sfumato, from the Italian for \"to turn to smoke\". · Chiaroscuro"],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/tenebrism.html","Value and Light","Tenebrism","","caravaggio-calling-of-saint-matthew-detail","Tenebrism, from the Latin tenebrae, \"darkness\", is chiaroscuro at its most extreme: brightly lit figures against near-total darkness, with little in between, so the lit parts stand out sharply and the rest drops away. · A single beam enters high at the right and slants down the wall: faces and hands in its path are bright, and what it misses stays near black. · The window above gives only a dim glow, too weak to fill the shadows, so the beam alone picks out the figures. · Artemisia Gentileschi · Judith and Her Maidservant with the Head of Holofernes · about 1623–25 · Gentileschi lit the scene with one candle, which Judith's raised hand seems to shade, so the two women stand out brightly while the table and floor sink into near-black. · The first public works of the painter Caravaggio, about 1600, caused a sensation, and visiting artists took the style home."],[17,"/study/value-and-light/light-shade-and-form/tenebrism.html","Value and Light · Example","Judith and Her Maidservant with the Head of Holofernes","Artemisia Gentileschi","thumb/gentileschi-judith-detroit","about 1623–25 · Gentileschi lit the scene with one candle, which Judith's raised hand seems to shade, so the two women stand out brightly while the table and floor sink into near-black. · Tenebrism"]]};
